2026-03-09 09:57:03 [nitro@ryzen5] for vic20 memory map, am considering the json format, discuss only, no code please
2026-03-09 09:57:20 [claude-opus-4-6] Great topic. There are a few considerations worth discussing for representing the VIC-20 memory map
2026-03-09 09:57:20 [claude-opus-4-6] in JSON:
2026-03-09 09:57:20 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-09 09:57:20 [claude-opus-4-6] • Granularity question* — The VIC-20 memory map is notoriously configuration-dependent
2026-03-09 09:57:20 [claude-opus-4-6] (unexpanded vs 3K vs 8K vs 16K vs 24K+ expansion). Do you represent one canonical map, or multiple
2026-03-09 09:57:20 [claude-opus-4-6] configurations as variants?
2026-03-09 09:57:20 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-09 09:57:20 [claude-opus-4-6] • Structure approaches:*
2026-03-09 09:57:20 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-09 09:57:20 [claude-opus-4-6] 1. Flat array of regions — Each entry has start, end, label, type (RAM/ROM/IO). Simple,
2026-03-09 09:57:20 [claude-opus-4-6] easy to query by address range. But doesn't capture how regions change with expansion configs.
2026-03-09 09:57:20 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-09 09:57:20 [claude-opus-4-6] 2. Hierarchical/nested — Group by category (zero page, BASIC area, screen memory, character
2026-03-09 09:57:20 [claude-opus-4-6] ROM, I/O block, etc.) with sub-regions. More readable, mirrors how people actually think about the
2026-03-09 09:57:20 [claude-opus-4-6] map, but harder to do linear address lookups.
2026-03-09 09:57:20 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-09 09:57:20 [claude-opus-4-6] 3. Layered/overlay model — A base map plus expansion overlays that modify it. This is
2026-03-09 09:57:21 [claude-opus-4-6] arguably the most accurate to reality since inserting a 3K expansion at $0400 changes what BASIC
2026-03-09 09:57:21 [claude-opus-4-6] sees, screen memory moves, etc.
2026-03-09 09:57:21 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-09 09:57:21 [claude-opus-4-6] • Specific VIC-20 quirks to account for:*
2026-03-09 09:57:21 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-09 09:57:21 [claude-opus-4-6] - The I/O block at 9000–93FF (VIC chip, VIA chips) has register-level detail worth capturing
2026-03-09 09:57:21 [claude-opus-4-6] - Screen memory and colour memory locations shift depending on config
2026-03-09 09:57:21 [claude-opus-4-6] - The $A000 block can be RAM or cartridge ROM
2026-03-09 09:57:21 [claude-opus-4-6] - Character ROM at $8000 is only visible to the VIC chip, not the CPU — that's a dual-mapping
2026-03-09 09:57:21 [claude-opus-4-6] subtlety
2026-03-09 09:57:21 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-09 09:57:21 [claude-opus-4-6] • Metadata worth including:*
2026-03-09 09:57:21 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-09 09:57:21 [claude-opus-4-6] - Read/write/both permissions
2026-03-09 09:57:21 [claude-opus-4-6] - Whether a region is CPU-visible, VIC-chip-visible, or both
2026-03-09 09:57:21 [claude-opus-4-6] - Aliases (e.g., colour RAM mirroring)
2026-03-09 09:57:21 [claude-opus-4-6] - Common KERNAL/BASIC entry points as named addresses within ROM regions
2026-03-09 09:57:21 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-09 09:57:21 [claude-opus-4-6] • My instinct* — a layered approach with a base unexpanded map and named expansion profiles
2026-03-09 09:57:21 [claude-opus-4-6] that patch regions feels cleanest. It avoids duplicating the entire map per config while staying
2026-03-09 09:57:21 [claude-opus-4-6] accurate. The I/O registers probably deserve their own detailed sub-structure given how often
2026-03-09 09:57:21 [claude-opus-4-6] they're referenced.
2026-03-09 09:57:21 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-09 09:57:21 [claude-opus-4-6] What's your intended use case — emulation, a reference tool, something else? That might steer the
2026-03-09 09:57:21 [claude-opus-4-6] format.
2026-03-09 09:57:21 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-09 09:57:34 [nitro@ryzen5] "memorymap":[
2026-03-09 09:57:34 [nitro@ryzen5] {"hex":"0000","address":0,"length":1,"about":"Jump for USR"},
2026-03-09 09:57:34 [nitro@ryzen5] {"hex":"0001","address":1,"length":2,"about":"Vector for USR"},
2026-03-09 09:57:34 [nitro@ryzen5] ],
2026-03-09 09:57:48 [claude-opus-4-6] That's a solid starting point — flat, simple, easy to parse. A few thoughts:
2026-03-09 09:57:48 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-09 09:57:48 [claude-opus-4-6] • What works well:*
2026-03-09 09:57:48 [claude-opus-4-6] - Having both hex and decimal address is convenient for different consumers
2026-03-09 09:57:48 [claude-opus-4-6] - Length is essential since VIC-20 regions vary from single bytes to multi-KB blocks
2026-03-09 09:57:48 [claude-opus-4-6] - Flat array means straightforward address lookups
2026-03-09 09:57:48 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-09 09:57:48 [claude-opus-4-6] • Potential concerns:*
2026-03-09 09:57:48 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-09 09:57:48 [claude-opus-4-6] • Redundancy* — hex and address represent the same value. You could derive one from the
2026-03-09 09:57:48 [claude-opus-4-6] other and keep the JSON leaner. Though I understand the convenience argument, especially if this is
2026-03-09 09:57:48 [claude-opus-4-6] also a human-readable reference.
2026-03-09 09:57:48 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-09 09:57:48 [claude-opus-4-6] • Length ambiguity at this granularity* — Your example maps individual zero page locations
2026-03-09 09:57:48 [claude-opus-4-6] (length 1, length 2). Are you planning to do that for the entire 64K space? Zero page and the I/O
2026-03-09 09:57:48 [claude-opus-4-6] registers warrant byte-level detail, but the BASIC ROM from C000–DFFF probably doesn't need 8192
2026-03-09 09:57:48 [claude-opus-4-6] individual entries. You might end up with a mix of granularities, which is fine but worth being
2026-03-09 09:57:48 [claude-opus-4-6] intentional about.
2026-03-09 09:57:48 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-09 09:57:48 [claude-opus-4-6] • Missing fields you might want later:*
2026-03-09 09:57:48 [claude-opus-4-6] - Read/write/both
2026-03-09 09:57:48 [claude-opus-4-6] - Category or type tag (zeropage, io, ram, rom, kernal, basic) for filtering
2026-03-09 09:57:48 [claude-opus-4-6] - Which expansion configs the entry applies to — that USR vector is always there, but other regions
2026-03-09 09:57:48 [claude-opus-4-6] shift
2026-03-09 09:57:48 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-09 09:57:48 [claude-opus-4-6] • Naming* — "about" is a bit vague. Something like "label" or "description" might be
2026-03-09 09:57:48 [claude-opus-4-6] clearer, or even split into a short label and a longer description.
2026-03-09 09:57:48 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-09 09:57:48 [claude-opus-4-6] • The big question* remains whether entries that change with expansion configs get separate
2026-03-09 09:57:48 [claude-opus-4-6] records, a config flag, or are handled some other way. At the zero page level it's less of an issue
2026-03-09 09:57:48 [claude-opus-4-6] since those locations are mostly stable across configs.
2026-03-09 09:57:48 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-09 09:57:48 [claude-opus-4-6] Are you sourcing this from a specific reference like the Mapping the VIC or the Programmer's
2026-03-09 09:57:48 [claude-opus-4-6] Reference Guide?
2026-03-09 09:57:48 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-09 09:59:26 [nitro@ryzen5] sourcing from reference, would like to have a short name for usage in assembly where appropriate
2026-03-09 09:59:27 [nitro@ryzen5] "memorymap":[
2026-03-09 09:59:27 [nitro@ryzen5] {"hex":"0000","address":0,"length":1,"about":"Jump for USR"},
2026-03-09 09:59:27 [nitro@ryzen5] {"hex":"0001","address":1,"length":2,"about":"Vector for USR"},
2026-03-09 09:59:27 [nitro@ryzen5] ],
2026-03-09 09:59:38 [claude-opus-4-6] Makes sense. Adding a short name for assembly use is a natural fit. Something like:
2026-03-09 09:59:38 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-09 09:59:38 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-09 09:59:38 [claude-opus-4-6] {"hex":"0001","address":1,"length":2,"name":"USRVEC","about":"Vector for USR"}
2026-03-09 09:59:38 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-09 09:59:38 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-09 09:59:38 [claude-opus-4-6] • On the name field:*
2026-03-09 09:59:38 [claude-opus-4-6] - Not every entry will have a meaningful assembly label — some regions are just reserved blocks or
2026-03-09 09:59:38 [claude-opus-4-6] gaps. So the field should be nullable/optional.
2026-03-09 09:59:38 [claude-opus-4-6] - Worth deciding on a naming convention early — all caps is traditional for 6502 assembly
2026-03-09 09:59:38 [claude-opus-4-6] constants. Some locations already have well-known names from Commodore's own documentation (CINV,
2026-03-09 09:59:38 [claude-opus-4-6] CBINV, ILOAD, etc.) so you could use those where they exist.
2026-03-09 09:59:38 [claude-opus-4-6] - For the VIC chip registers at $9000+, names like VICCR0 through VICCRF are common, or the more
2026-03-09 09:59:38 [claude-opus-4-6] descriptive ones like SCNLIN, MARGIN, etc.
2026-03-09 09:59:38 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-09 09:59:38 [claude-opus-4-6] • One thought on hex* — since you're targeting assembly use, consider whether you want the
2026-03-09 09:59:38 [claude-opus-4-6] hex field zero-padded to 4 characters consistently (which you're already doing) and whether a `$`
2026-03-09 09:59:38 [claude-opus-4-6] prefix would be useful or just noise. Probably leave it bare since the consumer can format it
2026-03-09 09:59:38 [claude-opus-4-6] however they need.
2026-03-09 09:59:38 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-09 09:59:38 [claude-opus-4-6] This is shaping up as a useful reference file. The combination of machine-readable addresses with
2026-03-09 09:59:38 [claude-opus-4-6] assembly names and human descriptions covers the main use cases — lookup by address, lookup by
2026-03-09 09:59:38 [claude-opus-4-6] name, and browsing for understanding.
2026-03-09 09:59:38 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-09 10:00:12 [nitro@ryzen5] name for the constant name field
2026-03-09 10:00:13 [nitro@ryzen5] {"hex":"0000","address":0,"bytes":1,"label":"Jump for USR"},
2026-03-09 10:00:13 [nitro@ryzen5] {"hex":"0001","address":1,"bytes":2,"label":"Vector for USR"},
2026-03-09 10:00:21 [claude-opus-4-6] Clean. So you've renamed "length" to "bytes" and "about" to "label" — both improvements.
2026-03-09 10:00:21 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-09 10:00:21 [claude-opus-4-6] For the assembly constant name, some candidates:
2026-03-09 10:00:21 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-09 10:00:21 [claude-opus-4-6] - "name" — simple, generic, maybe too generic
2026-03-09 10:00:21 [claude-opus-4-6] - "symbol" — accurate assembly terminology, a symbol is exactly what this is
2026-03-09 10:00:21 [claude-opus-4-6] - "alias" — implies it's an alternative reference, not quite right
2026-03-09 10:00:21 [claude-opus-4-6] - "const" — too specific about usage
2026-03-09 10:00:21 [claude-opus-4-6] - "asm" — clear intent but couples it to one use case
2026-03-09 10:00:21 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-09 10:00:21 [claude-opus-4-6] I'd lean toward "symbol". It's the correct term for a named address in assembly, it's
2026-03-09 10:00:21 [claude-opus-4-6] distinct from "label" which you're already using for the description, and it reads naturally:
2026-03-09 10:00:21 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-09 10:00:21 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-09 10:00:21 [claude-opus-4-6] {"hex":"0001","address":1,"bytes":2,"symbol":"USRVEC","label":"Vector for USR"}
2026-03-09 10:00:21 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-09 10:00:21 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-09 10:00:21 [claude-opus-4-6] Keeps each field name doing one unambiguous job — hex and address locate it, bytes sizes it, symbol
2026-03-09 10:00:21 [claude-opus-4-6] names it for code, label describes it for humans.
2026-03-09 10:00:21 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-09 10:01:26 [nitro@ryzen5] symbol is good, maybe lets start with json string array of all official symbols for vic20?
2026-03-09 10:01:36 [claude-opus-4-6] That's a smart move — establish the canonical symbol list first, then map them to addresses after.
2026-03-09 10:01:36 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-09 10:01:36 [claude-opus-4-6] However, "all official symbols" is a bit tricky for the VIC-20. There are a few sources with
2026-03-09 10:01:36 [claude-opus-4-6] slightly different naming:
2026-03-09 10:01:36 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-09 10:01:36 [claude-opus-4-6] - Commodore's own KERNAL jump table — well defined, things like CHRIN, CHROUT, OPEN, CLOSE,
2026-03-09 10:01:36 [claude-opus-4-6] SETLFS, etc.
2026-03-09 10:01:36 [claude-opus-4-6] - Commodore's Programmer's Reference Guide — zero page labels and system variables
2026-03-09 10:01:36 [claude-opus-4-6] - Mapping the VIC by Leemon Sheldon — very thorough but some names are the author's own
2026-03-09 10:01:36 [claude-opus-4-6] conventions rather than Commodore official
2026-03-09 10:01:36 [claude-opus-4-6] - VIC chip register names — vary between sources
2026-03-09 10:01:36 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-09 10:01:36 [claude-opus-4-6] Worth deciding which source you're treating as authoritative. The KERNAL entry points are the most
2026-03-09 10:01:36 [claude-opus-4-6] universally agreed upon. Zero page and system variable names have more variation.
2026-03-09 10:01:36 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-09 10:01:36 [claude-opus-4-6] Also worth considering scope — are you including:
2026-03-09 10:01:36 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-09 10:01:36 [claude-opus-4-6] - KERNAL jump table symbols only
2026-03-09 10:01:36 [claude-opus-4-6] - All documented zero page / system variable symbols
2026-03-09 10:01:36 [claude-opus-4-6] - VIC and VIA register symbols
2026-03-09 10:01:36 [claude-opus-4-6] - BASIC ROM entry points
2026-03-09 10:01:36 [claude-opus-4-6] - All of the above
2026-03-09 10:01:36 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-09 10:01:36 [claude-opus-4-6] I'd suggest starting with the KERNAL jump table since that's the most standardised and
2026-03-09 10:01:36 [claude-opus-4-6] uncontroversial set, then expanding outward. Which source are you working from primarily?
2026-03-09 10:01:36 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-09 10:03:27 [nitro@ryzen5] just want to document zero page from reference manual for now, how about we add a symbol if there is popular usage in wild only?
2026-03-09 10:03:38 [claude-opus-4-6] Perfect approach. Most zero page locations don't have widely used symbols anyway — some are just
2026-03-09 10:03:38 [claude-opus-4-6] internal BASIC/KERNAL working storage that nobody references by name.
2026-03-09 10:03:38 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-09 10:03:38 [claude-opus-4-6] The ones that do have popular usage in the wild tend to be obvious — things people actually use in
2026-03-09 10:03:38 [claude-opus-4-6] their own assembly code regularly. Off the top of my head, candidates would be things like:
2026-03-09 10:03:38 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-09 10:03:38 [claude-opus-4-6] - USRVEC — not that commonly used honestly
2026-03-09 10:03:38 [claude-opus-4-6] - ADRAY1/ADRAY2 — float conversion vectors, fairly popular
2026-03-09 10:03:38 [claude-opus-4-6] - TXTTAB — start of BASIC, very common
2026-03-09 10:03:38 [claude-opus-4-6] - VARTAB, ARYTAB, STREND, FRETOP, MEMSIZ — BASIC memory pointers, commonly referenced
2026-03-09 10:03:38 [claude-opus-4-6] - CURLIN — current BASIC line number, popular for detecting direct mode
2026-03-09 10:03:38 [claude-opus-4-6] - STATUS/ST — I/O status, extremely common
2026-03-09 10:03:38 [claude-opus-4-6] - FNLEN, LA, FA, SA — file parameters, well known
2026-03-09 10:03:38 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-09 10:03:38 [claude-opus-4-6] So the entries without popular symbols would just omit the field:
2026-03-09 10:03:38 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-09 10:03:38 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-09 10:03:38 [claude-opus-4-6] {"hex":"0000","address":0,"bytes":1,"label":"Jump for USR"}
2026-03-09 10:03:38 [claude-opus-4-6] {"hex":"0001","address":1,"bytes":2,"symbol":"USRVEC","label":"Vector for USR"}
2026-03-09 10:03:38 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-09 10:03:38 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-09 10:03:38 [claude-opus-4-6] This keeps it honest — you're not inventing symbols nobody uses, and the ones you do include are
2026-03-09 10:03:38 [claude-opus-4-6] genuinely useful for someone writing assembly. Good editorial discipline.
2026-03-09 10:03:38 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-09 10:03:38 [claude-opus-4-6] Want to start working through the zero page sequentially from your reference?
2026-03-09 10:03:38 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-09 10:03:52 [nitro@ryzen5] yes please
2026-03-09 10:03:56 [claude-opus-4-6] Let's work through it. What does your reference show for $00 through the first chunk? I don't want
2026-03-09 10:03:56 [claude-opus-4-6] to assume which reference manual you're using since there are minor differences between printings
2026-03-09 10:03:56 [claude-opus-4-6] and regional editions.
2026-03-09 10:03:56 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-09 10:03:56 [claude-opus-4-6] Just list out what you have and I'll help you shape each entry, suggest symbols where there's
2026-03-09 10:03:56 [claude-opus-4-6] genuine popular usage, and flag anything worth discussing.
