2026-03-09 10:06:54 [claude-opus-4-6] Good, matches so far. For symbols on those:
2026-03-09 10:06:54 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-09 10:06:54 [claude-opus-4-6] - $00 — no popular symbol
2026-03-09 10:06:54 [claude-opus-4-6] - $01-02 — USRVEC is used but honestly not that common in the wild
2026-03-09 10:06:54 [claude-opus-4-6] - $03-04 — ADRAY1 is genuinely popular, used for redirecting float-to-integer conversion
2026-03-09 10:06:54 [claude-opus-4-6] - $05-06 — ADRAY2 same, the pair is well known
2026-03-09 10:06:54 [claude-opus-4-6] - $07 — no popular symbol
2026-03-09 10:06:54 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-09 10:06:54 [claude-opus-4-6] So I'd suggest:
2026-03-09 10:06:54 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-09 10:06:54 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-09 10:06:54 [claude-opus-4-6] {"hex":"0000","address":0,"bytes":1,"label":"Jump for USR"},
2026-03-09 10:06:54 [claude-opus-4-6] {"hex":"0001","address":1,"bytes":2,"label":"Vector for USR"},
2026-03-09 10:06:54 [claude-opus-4-6] {"hex":"0003","address":3,"bytes":2,"symbol":"ADRAY1","label":"Float-Fixed vector"},
2026-03-09 10:06:54 [claude-opus-4-6] {"hex":"0005","address":5,"bytes":2,"symbol":"ADRAY2","label":"Fixed-Float vector"},
2026-03-09 10:06:54 [claude-opus-4-6] {"hex":"0007","address":7,"bytes":1,"label":"Search character"},
2026-03-09 10:06:54 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-09 10:06:54 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-09 10:06:54 [claude-opus-4-6] I'd actually drop USRVEC — the USR function itself is rarely used in VIC-20 code in the wild. Most
2026-03-09 10:06:54 [claude-opus-4-6] people just poke the address directly and know it as "the USR vector" without a symbolic name.
2026-03-09 10:06:54 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-09 10:06:54 [claude-opus-4-6] Agree? Want to continue with $08 onwards?
