2026-03-09 10:14:43 [nitro@ryzen5] the 24 bytes for the CHRGET routine are interesting
2026-03-09 10:14:55 [claude-opus-4-6] Yes, CHRGET is fascinating. It's one of the cleverest things in the Commodore ROM design.
2026-03-09 10:14:55 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-09 10:14:55 [claude-opus-4-6] They copy a small machine language subroutine into zero page at boot so it runs faster — zero page
2026-03-09 10:14:55 [claude-opus-4-6] addressing saves a cycle per instruction on the 6502. Since CHRGET is called constantly during
2026-03-09 10:14:55 [claude-opus-4-6] BASIC parsing, those saved cycles add up significantly.
2026-03-09 10:14:55 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-09 10:14:55 [claude-opus-4-6] The really clever bit is that the BASIC text pointer at 7A-7B lives inside the subroutine
2026-03-09 10:14:55 [claude-opus-4-6] as the operand of a LDA instruction. So when CHRGET increments the pointer, it's literally
2026-03-09 10:14:55 [claude-opus-4-6] self-modifying code — it changes its own operand. This only works because it's in RAM, which is
2026-03-09 10:14:55 [claude-opus-4-6] another reason it has to be copied out of ROM.
2026-03-09 10:14:55 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-09 10:14:55 [claude-opus-4-6] Some well-known tricks exploit this:
2026-03-09 10:14:55 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-09 10:14:55 [claude-opus-4-6] - Wedge programs hook into CHRGET to intercept BASIC parsing, adding new commands. The DOS wedge
2026-03-09 10:14:55 [claude-opus-4-6] for disk drives is the classic example.
2026-03-09 10:14:55 [claude-opus-4-6] - You can watch 7A-7B to see where BASIC is currently reading from.
2026-03-09 10:14:55 [claude-opus-4-6] - Some copy protection schemes modify CHRGET to interfere with LIST or tamper detection.
2026-03-09 10:14:55 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-09 10:14:55 [claude-opus-4-6] It's also worth noting that your earlier entry had it at 138 bytes which seems too large — the
2026-03-09 10:14:55 [claude-opus-4-6] actual CHRGET routine is more like 24-26 bytes. The reference manual may be grouping a larger
2026-03-09 10:14:55 [claude-opus-4-6] region there. Worth double-checking that boundary against your copy.
2026-03-09 10:14:55 [claude-opus-4-6] 
