2026-03-10 07:56:28 [deepseek-chat] That's a fantastic deep dive! Studying the original Apple II Breakout code by Steve Wozniak
2026-03-10 07:56:28 [deepseek-chat] is like looking at programming archaeology. Woz's code is legendary for its cleverness and tight
2026-03-10 07:56:28 [deepseek-chat] optimization.
2026-03-10 07:56:28 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-10 07:56:28 [deepseek-chat] Key things you'll notice in the original 1976 code:
2026-03-10 07:56:28 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-10 07:56:28 [deepseek-chat] • Technical Context:*
2026-03-10 07:56:28 [deepseek-chat] - Written in 6502 assembly (only ~300 lines!)
2026-03-10 07:56:28 [deepseek-chat] - Designed for the Apple I/early Apple II
2026-03-10 07:56:28 [deepseek-chat] - Uses NTSC TV timing tricks for graphics
2026-03-10 07:56:28 [deepseek-chat] - No frame buffer - draws directly during horizontal/vertical blanking
2026-03-10 07:56:28 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-10 07:56:28 [deepseek-chat] • Notable Optimizations:*
2026-03-10 07:56:28 [deepseek-chat] - The famous "brick" representation: Each brick is just 2 bits in memory
2026-03-10 07:56:28 [deepseek-chat] - Ball physics calculated with minimal math
2026-03-10 07:56:28 [deepseek-chat] - Paddle drawn by toggling a single pixel on/off rapidly
2026-03-10 07:56:28 [deepseek-chat] - Sound generated by timing loops
2026-03-10 07:56:28 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-10 07:56:28 [deepseek-chat] • Historical Significance:*
2026-03-10 07:56:28 [deepseek-chat] - Woz wrote it in 4 nights (while at Atari!)
2026-03-10 07:56:28 [deepseek-chat] - Atari paid him $5,000 bonus for using fewer chips
2026-03-10 07:56:28 [deepseek-chat] - Demonstrated the Apple II's gaming potential
2026-03-10 07:56:28 [deepseek-chat] - Inspired countless clones and variations
2026-03-10 07:56:28 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-03-10 07:56:28 [deepseek-chat] Are you looking at disassembled code, or reading about the algorithms? The way he packed game state
2026-03-10 07:56:28 [deepseek-chat] into minimal memory is particularly elegant.
2026-03-10 07:56:28 [deepseek-chat] 
