2026-03-10 13:47:36 [nitro@ryzen5] docs for ql basic circle command
2026-03-10 13:47:47 [claude-opus-4-6] QBasic CIRCLE Command
2026-03-10 13:47:47 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-10 13:47:47 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-10 13:47:47 [claude-opus-4-6] CIRCLE (x, y), radius [, color [, start, end [, aspect]]]
2026-03-10 13:47:47 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-10 13:47:47 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-10 13:47:47 [claude-opus-4-6] • Parameters:*
2026-03-10 13:47:47 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-10 13:47:47 [claude-opus-4-6] - (x, y) – Center coordinates of the circle/ellipse
2026-03-10 13:47:47 [claude-opus-4-6] - radius – Radius in pixels
2026-03-10 13:47:47 [claude-opus-4-6] - color – Color attribute (optional)
2026-03-10 13:47:47 [claude-opus-4-6] - start, end – Arc angles in radians (optional, range: -2π to 2π). Negative values draw a
2026-03-10 13:47:47 [claude-opus-4-6] line from the center to that arc endpoint (pie slices).
2026-03-10 13:47:47 [claude-opus-4-6] - aspect – Aspect ratio (optional). Values other than 1.0 draw ellipses. Default depends on
2026-03-10 13:47:47 [claude-opus-4-6] screen mode.
2026-03-10 13:47:47 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-10 13:47:47 [claude-opus-4-6] • Examples:*
2026-03-10 13:47:47 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-10 13:47:47 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-10 13:47:47 [claude-opus-4-6] SCREEN 12
2026-03-10 13:47:47 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-10 13:47:47 [claude-opus-4-6] ' Basic circle
2026-03-10 13:47:47 [claude-opus-4-6] CIRCLE (320, 240), 100
2026-03-10 13:47:47 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-10 13:47:47 [claude-opus-4-6] ' Circle with color
2026-03-10 13:47:47 [claude-opus-4-6] CIRCLE (320, 240), 100, 4
2026-03-10 13:47:47 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-10 13:47:47 [claude-opus-4-6] ' Arc (quarter circle)
2026-03-10 13:47:47 [claude-opus-4-6] CIRCLE (320, 240), 100, 4, 0, 1.5708
2026-03-10 13:47:47 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-10 13:47:47 [claude-opus-4-6] ' Pie slice (negative angles draw radii to center)
2026-03-10 13:47:47 [claude-opus-4-6] CIRCLE (320, 240), 100, 4, -0.5, -2.5
2026-03-10 13:47:47 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-10 13:47:47 [claude-opus-4-6] ' Ellipse (aspect ratio 0.5 = wide)
2026-03-10 13:47:47 [claude-opus-4-6] CIRCLE (320, 240), 100, 4, , , 0.5
2026-03-10 13:47:47 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-10 13:47:47 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-10 13:47:47 [claude-opus-4-6] • Notes:*
2026-03-10 13:47:47 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-10 13:47:47 [claude-opus-4-6] - Angles are in radians, not degrees. (π = 3.14159...)
2026-03-10 13:47:47 [claude-opus-4-6] - 0 radians = 3 o'clock, π/2 = 12 o'clock, π = 9 o'clock, 3π/2 = 6 o'clock.
2026-03-10 13:47:47 [claude-opus-4-6] - To convert degrees to radians: `radians = degrees * 3.14159 / 180`
2026-03-10 13:47:47 [claude-opus-4-6] - Omitted optional parameters still need their commas as placeholders (as shown in the ellipse
2026-03-10 13:47:47 [claude-opus-4-6] example).
2026-03-10 13:47:47 [claude-opus-4-6] - The `PAINT` command can be used afterward to fill the shape.
2026-03-10 13:47:47 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-11 14:46:43 [nitro@ryzen5] hi claude can you validate my 6502 cheat sheet
2026-03-11 14:46:47 [claude-opus-4-6] Hi! Sure, happy to take a look. Go ahead and share your 6502 cheat sheet and I'll check it over for
2026-03-11 14:46:47 [claude-opus-4-6] accuracy.
2026-03-11 14:46:47 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-11 14:47:10 [claude-opus-4-6] Nice work, this is well structured. Here are the issues I found:
2026-03-11 14:47:10 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-11 14:47:10 [claude-opus-4-6] Opcode Errors
2026-03-11 14:47:10 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-11 14:47:10 [claude-opus-4-6] - TSX — flags should be `"NZ"`, not `""`. It affects N and Z based on the value
2026-03-11 14:47:10 [claude-opus-4-6] transferred.
2026-03-11 14:47:10 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-11 14:47:10 [claude-opus-4-6] Opcode Verification
2026-03-11 14:47:10 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-11 14:47:10 [claude-opus-4-6] All hex opcodes check out correctly against the NMOS 6502 reference. Good job there.
2026-03-11 14:47:10 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-11 14:47:10 [claude-opus-4-6] BRK Note
2026-03-11 14:47:10 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-11 14:47:10 [claude-opus-4-6] - BRK — you list flags as `""`. It does set the B and I flags on the stack/in status.
2026-03-11 14:47:10 [claude-opus-4-6] Whether you want to reflect that is a design choice, but worth noting. You could argue `"I"` at
2026-03-11 14:47:10 [claude-opus-4-6] minimum since it sets the interrupt disable flag.
2026-03-11 14:47:10 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-11 14:47:10 [claude-opus-4-6] STA Missing Opcode
2026-03-11 14:47:10 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-11 14:47:10 [claude-opus-4-6] - STA has 7 opcodes listed but the standard 8 addressing modes for store-to-memory include
2026-03-11 14:47:10 [claude-opus-4-6] all except immediate. You're missing STA absolute,Y ... wait, no — `99` is there. Let me
2026-03-11 14:47:10 [claude-opus-4-6] recount: `85 95 8D 9D 99 81 91` — that's 7, which is correct. STA has no immediate mode. ✓
2026-03-11 14:47:10 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-11 14:47:10 [claude-opus-4-6] Profile Section
2026-03-11 14:47:10 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-11 14:47:10 [claude-opus-4-6] This is harder to fully validate without knowing your spec index mapping, but I notice:
2026-03-11 14:47:10 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-11 14:47:10 [claude-opus-4-6] - Spec 5 (branches): `{"bytes":[2],"cycles":[2]}` — branch cycles are 2 (not taken), 3
2026-03-11 14:47:10 [claude-opus-4-6] (taken, same page), 4 (taken, page cross). You only list one cycle count. Might be intentional
2026-03-11 14:47:10 [claude-opus-4-6] simplification?
2026-03-11 14:47:10 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-11 14:47:10 [claude-opus-4-6] - Spec 8 (JMP): `{"bytes":[3,3],"cycles":[3,5]}` — JMP absolute is 3 cycles, JMP indirect
2026-03-11 14:47:10 [claude-opus-4-6] is 5. ✓
2026-03-11 14:47:10 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-11 14:47:10 [claude-opus-4-6] - Spec 15 (LDA etc.): `(indirect,X)` is 6 cycles, `(indirect),Y` is 5 (+1 page cross). You
2026-03-11 14:47:10 [claude-opus-4-6] have `6,5` at the end. ✓
2026-03-11 14:47:10 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-11 14:47:10 [claude-opus-4-6] - Spec 13 (LDX/LDY): 5 modes listed but LDX uses zeropage,Y not zeropage,X and LDY uses
2026-03-11 14:47:10 [claude-opus-4-6] zeropage,X not zeropage,Y — the addressing modes differ between LDX and LDY so sharing a single
