2026-03-23 11:27:25 [nitro@ryzen5] standard clock rate for rpi 2040
2026-03-23 11:27:47 [kimi-k2.5] The RP2040 has a maximum rated clock speed of 133 MHz.
2026-03-23 11:27:47 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-03-23 11:27:47 [kimi-k2.5] At boot it runs from the internal ring oscillator (ROSC) at approximately 6.5 MHz (varies
2026-03-23 11:27:47 [kimi-k2.5] with temperature/voltage), which the SDK typically increases to 133 MHz during initialization. The
2026-03-23 11:27:47 [kimi-k2.5] USB PHY requires a 48 MHz reference clock.
2026-03-23 11:27:47 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-03-23 11:30:23 [nitro@ryzen5] c++ sdk command to detect and set clock rate
2026-03-23 11:30:39 [kimi-k2.5] Detect current system clock:
2026-03-23 11:30:39 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-03-23 11:30:39 [kimi-k2.5] #include "hardware/clocks.h"
2026-03-23 11:30:39 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-03-23 11:30:39 [kimi-k2.5] uint32_t current_hz = clock_get_hz(clk_sys);
2026-03-23 11:30:39 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-03-23 11:30:39 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-03-23 11:30:39 [kimi-k2.5] Set clock to specific frequency (automatic PLL calculation):
2026-03-23 11:30:39 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-03-23 11:30:39 [kimi-k2.5] #include "pico/stdlib.h"
2026-03-23 11:30:39 [kimi-k2.5] #include "hardware/clocks.h"
2026-03-23 11:30:39 [kimi-k2.5] #include "hardware/pll.h"
2026-03-23 11:30:39 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-03-23 11:30:39 [kimi-k2.5] bool success = set_sys_clock_hz(133000000, true);
2026-03-23 11:30:39 [kimi-k2.5] if (!success) {
2026-03-23 11:30:39 [kimi-k2.5] 	// Frequency not achievable with current VCO limits
2026-03-23 11:30:39 [kimi-k2.5] }
2026-03-23 11:30:39 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-03-23 11:30:39 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-03-23 11:30:39 [kimi-k2.5] Set clock with explicit PLL parameters (more control):
2026-03-23 11:30:39 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-03-23 11:30:39 [kimi-k2.5] // Configure SYS PLL for 133 MHz: VCO = 1596 MHz, postdiv1 = 6, postdiv2 = 2
2026-03-23 11:30:39 [kimi-k2.5] // 1596 / 6 / 2 = 133 MHz
2026-03-23 11:30:39 [kimi-k2.5] set_sys_clock_pll(1596000000, 6, 2);
2026-03-23 11:30:39 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-03-23 11:30:39 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-03-23 11:30:39 [kimi-k2.5] • Important:* After changing system clock, peripherals derived from `clksys` (UART,
2026-03-23 11:30:39 [kimi-k2.5] SPI, etc.) need reconfiguration as their dividers become incorrect. Call `uartinit()` or
2026-03-23 11:30:39 [kimi-k2.5] similar again after clock changes, or calculate new dividers based on
2026-03-23 11:30:39 [kimi-k2.5] `clockgethz(clk_sys)`.
2026-03-23 11:30:39 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-03-23 11:31:24 [nitro@ryzen5] confirm default speed
2026-03-23 11:32:28 [kimi-k2.5] • Default SDK initialization: 125 MHz*
2026-03-23 11:32:28 [kimi-k2.5] • Hardware reset state:* ~6.5 MHz (ROSC)
2026-03-23 11:32:28 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-03-23 11:32:28 [kimi-k2.5] The RP2040 boots from the internal ring oscillator (~6.5 MHz), but the Pico SDK runtime raises this
2026-03-23 11:32:28 [kimi-k2.5] to 125 MHz during startup before `main()` is called (configured via
2026-03-23 11:32:28 [kimi-k2.5] `PICOPLLSYSVCOFREQ_HZ` = 1500 MHz, postdiv 6/2).
2026-03-23 11:32:28 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-03-23 11:32:28 [kimi-k2.5] To confirm at runtime:
2026-03-23 11:32:28 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-03-23 11:32:28 [kimi-k2.5] #include "hardware/clocks.h"
2026-03-23 11:32:28 [kimi-k2.5] #include <stdio.h>
2026-03-23 11:32:28 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-03-23 11:32:28 [kimi-k2.5] int main() {
2026-03-23 11:32:28 [kimi-k2.5] 	stdio_init_all();
2026-03-23 11:32:28 [kimi-k2.5] 	printf("System clock: %u Hz\n", clock_get_hz(clk_sys));
2026-03-23 11:32:28 [kimi-k2.5] 	// Prints: 125000000 on standard Pico SDK builds
2026-03-23 11:32:28 [kimi-k2.5] }
2026-03-23 11:32:28 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-03-23 11:32:28 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-03-23 11:32:28 [kimi-k2.5] The 133 MHz figure is the maximum rated speed; the SDK uses 125 MHz as a conservative
2026-03-23 11:32:28 [kimi-k2.5] default. You can override this at compile time with CMake:
2026-03-23 11:32:28 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-03-23 11:32:28 [kimi-k2.5] set(PICO_PLL_SYS_VCO_FREQ_HZ 1596000000)
2026-03-23 11:32:28 [kimi-k2.5] set(PICO_PLL_SYS_POSTDIV1 6)
2026-03-23 11:32:28 [kimi-k2.5] set(PICO_PLL_SYS_POSTDIV2 2)
2026-03-23 11:32:28 [kimi-k2.5] # 1596/6/2 = 133 MHz
2026-03-23 11:32:28 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-03-23 11:32:28 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-03-23 11:34:20 [nitro@ryzen5] at 125mhz does pio nop = 1 cycle? how many ns
2026-03-23 11:34:39 [kimi-k2.5] • Yes — 1 cycle = 8 ns*
2026-03-23 11:34:39 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-03-23 11:34:39 [kimi-k2.5] At 125 MHz:
2026-03-23 11:34:39 [kimi-k2.5] - `nop` executes in 1 clock cycle (8 nanoseconds)
2026-03-23 11:34:39 [kimi-k2.5] - Unless delayed by the 5-bit delay field (bits 8-12), which adds 0-31 extra cycles
2026-03-23 11:34:39 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-03-23 11:34:39 [kimi-k2.5] • Caveat:* This assumes the PIO clock divider is set to 1.0 (default). If using
2026-03-23 11:34:39 [kimi-k2.5] `smconfigset_clkdiv()`, multiply accordingly:
2026-03-23 11:34:39 [kimi-k2.5] - `clkdiv = 2.0` → 16 ns per instruction
2026-03-23 11:34:39 [kimi-k2.5] - `clkdiv = 1.0` (default) → 8 ns
2026-03-23 11:34:39 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-03-23 11:35:42 [nitro@ryzen5] for pal video ns of vertical blank period and per line sync period
2026-03-23 11:36:53 [kimi-k2.5] • Horizontal sync pulse: 4.7 µs = 4700 ns*
2026-03-23 11:36:53 [kimi-k2.5] • Vertical blanking interval: 25 lines = 1.6 ms = 1,600,000 ns* (per field)
2026-03-23 11:36:53 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-03-23 11:36:53 [kimi-k2.5] Standard PAL (625/50i) timing:
2026-03-23 11:36:53 [kimi-k2.5] - Line period: 64 µs (15625 Hz)
2026-03-23 11:36:53 [kimi-k2.5] - H-sync width: 4.7 µs (±0.2 µs tolerance)
2026-03-23 11:36:53 [kimi-k2.5] - V-blank: 25 lines per field (312.5 total lines minus 287.5 active)
2026-03-23 11:36:53 [kimi-k2.5] - V-sync pulse (broad): 2.5 lines = 160 µs (160,000 ns)
2026-03-23 11:36:53 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-03-23 11:36:53 [kimi-k2.5] At 125 MHz PIO (8 ns/cycle):
2026-03-23 11:36:53 [kimi-k2.5] - H-sync ≈ 588 cycles
2026-03-23 11:36:53 [kimi-k2.5] - Full V-blank ≈ 200,000 cycles
2026-03-23 11:36:53 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-03-23 11:36:53 [kimi-k2.5] • Note: For RGB SCART or composite video generation, tolerances are forgiving—±0.5 µs on sync edges
2026-03-23 11:36:53 [kimi-k2.5] usually works.*
2026-03-23 11:36:53 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-03-23 11:38:34 [nitro@ryzen5] 1.6 ms = ??? ns
2026-03-23 11:39:05 [kimi-k2.5] • 1.6 ms = 1,600,000 ns* (1.6 × 10⁶ nanoseconds)
2026-03-23 11:39:05 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-03-23 11:39:05 [kimi-k2.5] Or broken down:
2026-03-23 11:39:05 [kimi-k2.5] - 1 ms = 1,000,000 ns
2026-03-23 11:39:05 [kimi-k2.5] - 1.6 ms = 1,600,000 ns
2026-03-23 11:39:05 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-03-23 11:39:05 [kimi-k2.5] At 125 MHz PIO (8 ns/cycle), that's exactly 200,000 clock cycles for the full 25-line
2026-03-23 11:39:05 [kimi-k2.5] V-blank period.
2026-03-23 11:39:05 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-03-23 11:39:37 [nitro@ryzen5] 1 s = ? ns
2026-03-23 11:39:43 [kimi-k2.5] • 1 s = 1,000,000,000 ns* (10⁹ nanoseconds)
2026-03-23 11:39:43 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-03-23 11:39:43 [kimi-k2.5] At 125 MHz PIO clock, that's 125,000,000 cycles per second.
2026-03-23 11:39:43 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-03-23 11:40:52 [nitro@ryzen5] sorry how does At 125 MHz:
2026-03-23 11:41:14 [nitro@ryzen5] - `nop` executes in 1 clock cycle (8 nanoseconds)
2026-03-23 11:41:17 [nitro@ryzen5] make sense?
2026-03-23 11:41:26 [kimi-k2.5] • Math check:*
2026-03-23 11:41:26 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-03-23 11:41:26 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-03-23 11:41:26 [kimi-k2.5] Period = 1 / Frequency
2026-03-23 11:41:26 [kimi-k2.5]        = 1 / 125,000,000 Hz
2026-03-23 11:41:26 [kimi-k2.5]        = 0.000000008 s
2026-03-23 11:41:26 [kimi-k2.5]        = 8 × 10⁻⁹ s
2026-03-23 11:41:26 [kimi-k2.5]        = 8 ns
2026-03-23 11:41:26 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-03-23 11:41:26 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-03-23 11:41:26 [kimi-k2.5] 125 MHz = 125 million cycles per second → each cycle lasts 8 billionths of a second.
2026-03-23 11:41:26 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-03-23 11:41:26 [kimi-k2.5] • Verification:* 125,000,000 cycles × 8 ns = 1,000,000,000 ns = 1 second. ✓
2026-03-23 11:41:26 [kimi-k2.5] 
