2026-03-29 18:30:05 [nitro@ryzen5] in pico2 rp2350 c++ how to detect bootsel button press
2026-03-29 18:30:24 [gemini-3.1-pro] On the Raspberry Pi Pico 2 (RP2350), the BOOTSEL button is not connected to a standard GPIO pin.
2026-03-29 18:30:24 [gemini-3.1-pro] Instead, it is connected to the Chip Select (CS) pin of the onboard QSPI Flash memory.
2026-03-29 18:30:24 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 18:30:24 [gemini-3.1-pro] To detect a button press during normal execution, you cannot simply read a standard GPIO state. The
2026-03-29 18:30:24 [gemini-3.1-pro] general process requires you to:
2026-03-29 18:30:24 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 18:30:24 [gemini-3.1-pro] 1. Disable system interrupts to prevent any background tasks from attempting to read from flash
2026-03-29 18:30:24 [gemini-3.1-pro] memory.
2026-03-29 18:30:24 [gemini-3.1-pro] 2. Ensure the detection routine executes entirely from SRAM rather than flash (XIP).
2026-03-29 18:30:24 [gemini-3.1-pro] 3. Temporarily reconfigure the QSPI CS pad to act as a standard GPIO input.
2026-03-29 18:30:24 [gemini-3.1-pro] 4. Read the pin state (a low state indicates the button is pressed).
2026-03-29 18:30:24 [gemini-3.1-pro] 5. Restore the original QSPI hardware configuration and re-enable interrupts.
2026-03-29 18:30:24 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 18:30:24 [gemini-3.1-pro] Please let me know if you would like me to provide a C++ code example demonstrating this
2026-03-29 18:30:24 [gemini-3.1-pro] implementation.
