2026-03-29 19:18:34 [nitro@ryzen5] tv accepts syn but picture is not stable, i want to add some blank lines and maybe shorten vsync? no code, just discussion please
2026-03-29 19:18:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] Your proposed approach is exactly right. The instability is very likely due to the overall frame
2026-03-29 19:18:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] length and the structure of your vertical blanking interval.
2026-03-29 19:18:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 19:18:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] Currently, your PIO program outputs 288 active lines and about 7.5 lines of continuous vertical
2026-03-29 19:18:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] sync, totaling around 295.5 lines per frame. Most TVs expect exactly 312 lines for a stable 50Hz
2026-03-29 19:18:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] progressive PAL signal (often referred to as 288p). Adding blank lines to reach that 312-line total
2026-03-29 19:18:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] should stop the picture from rolling.
2026-03-29 19:18:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 19:18:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] Shortening the vertical sync is also a great idea. Holding the sync level low for 7.5 continuous
2026-03-29 19:18:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] lines is quite long and can confuse the TV's sync separator. A standard PAL vsync pulse is
2026-03-29 19:18:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] typically 2.5 to 3 lines long. You can achieve a much more stable lock by shortening the continuous
2026-03-29 19:18:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] vsync pulse to 2.5 or 3 lines, and then filling the remaining non-active lines (to reach the 312
2026-03-29 19:18:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] total) with standard blank lines that still output your regular horizontal sync pulses.
2026-03-29 19:18:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 19:18:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] Your horizontal timing (9600 cycles for a 64 µs line at 150 MHz) looks perfectly aligned, so
2026-03-29 19:18:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] adjusting the vertical line count and sync duration should get you a solid picture. Let me know if
2026-03-29 19:18:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] you would like to discuss the specific line distributions or timing intervals further.
