2026-03-29 19:22:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] You are correct to suspect the vsync duration. Outputting 25 continuous lines of vertical sync will
2026-03-29 19:22:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] cause instability, as the TV's sync separator loses horizontal tracking during that extended low
2026-03-29 19:22:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] period.
2026-03-29 19:22:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 19:22:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] For a non-interlaced PAL signal (often referred to as 288p), systems like the Commodore VIC-20
2026-03-29 19:22:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] output exactly 312 scanlines per frame.
2026-03-29 19:22:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 19:22:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] Within that frame, the VIC-20 PAL timing typically consists of:
2026-03-29 19:22:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 3 scanlines for the actual Vertical Sync pulse.
2026-03-29 19:22:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 25 scanlines (approximately) for the Vertical Blanking period.
2026-03-29 19:22:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 19:22:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] To stabilize your picture, you should shorten the continuous low vsync pulse to just 3 lines.
2026-03-29 19:22:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 19:22:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] The remaining lines of your vertical blanking period should be formatted exactly like your active
2026-03-29 19:22:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] video lines—maintaining your standard horizontal sync, front porch, and back porch timings—but
2026-03-29 19:22:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] simply outputting black instead of your active pixel pattern. This keeps the TV's horizontal
2026-03-29 19:22:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] oscillator locked while the electron beam returns to the top of the screen.
