2026-03-29 19:45:16 [nitro@ryzen5] the sync is nice and stable but the brightness of the stripes is not even, darker wide 1, followed by 2 brighter ones, evenly across the screen
2026-03-29 19:46:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] That uneven brightness is a classic symptom of analog-to-digital aliasing inside the TV, rather
2026-03-29 19:46:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] than an issue with your RP2350 code.
2026-03-29 19:46:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 19:46:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] Because the PIO state machine is strictly deterministic, your Pico is outputting those stripes with
2026-03-29 19:46:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] perfect timing. However, the unevenness happens when the TV processes the signal:
2026-03-29 19:46:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 19:46:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] 1. ADC Aliasing: Your PIO loop takes 25 cycles (in your first version). At a 150 MHz system
2026-03-29 19:46:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] clock, this generates a stripe frequency of exactly 6.0 MHz. Modern digital TVs typically sample
2026-03-29 19:46:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] standard-definition composite video at 13.5 MHz. Sampling a 6.0 MHz signal at 13.5 MHz gives
2026-03-29 19:46:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] exactly 2.25 samples per stripe. Because this isn't an integer, the TV's analog-to-digital
2026-03-29 19:46:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] converter samples different parts of the square wave (peak, rising edge, falling edge) on
2026-03-29 19:46:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] consecutive stripes. A 2.25 ratio creates an interference beat that perfectly explains your
2026-03-29 19:46:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] repeating "one dark, two bright" pattern.
2026-03-29 19:46:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] 2. Bandwidth & Comb Filters: Standard PAL composite video has a maximum luma (brightness)
2026-03-29 19:46:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] bandwidth of about 5.0 MHz to 5.5 MHz. At 6.0 MHz, your stripes are exceeding the channel bandwidth
2026-03-29 19:46:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] and sitting right on top of where the TV's comb filter expects high-frequency color subcarrier
2026-03-29 19:46:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] information, causing the TV to misinterpret or suppress the brightness.
2026-03-29 19:46:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 19:46:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] To verify this, try halving the frequency of your stripes to 3.0 MHz. You can do this by pushing
2026-03-29 19:46:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] half the value to the PIO FIFO from your C++ code (e.g., 155 instead of 311) and doubling the `nop`
2026-03-29 19:46:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] delays inside your PIO `stripe` loop so it takes 50 cycles instead of 25.
