2026-03-29 19:58:14 [nitro@ryzen5] if i do an outdirs pindirs i will mess up my osr?
2026-03-29 19:58:43 [gemini-3.1-pro] Yes, using `out pindirs, N` will shift bits out of the OSR, modifying its value. Since your current
2026-03-29 19:58:43 [gemini-3.1-pro] code relies on `mov y, osr` to reload the stripe counter on every scanline, altering the OSR will
2026-03-29 19:58:43 [gemini-3.1-pro] break that loop.
2026-03-29 19:58:43 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 19:58:43 [gemini-3.1-pro] When you transition to DMA for your bitmap, you will need the OSR to receive the incoming pixel
2026-03-29 19:58:43 [gemini-3.1-pro] stream anyway. To free up the OSR while preserving your `287` constant, a common trick is to stash
2026-03-29 19:58:43 [gemini-3.1-pro] the constant in the ISR (Input Shift Register) during initialization:
2026-03-29 19:58:43 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 19:58:43 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 19:58:43 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	pull block
2026-03-29 19:58:43 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	mov isr, osr
2026-03-29 19:58:43 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 19:58:43 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 19:58:43 [gemini-3.1-pro] Then, you can safely use `mov x, isr` and `mov y, isr` to reset your counters. This leaves the OSR
2026-03-29 19:58:43 [gemini-3.1-pro] completely free for `out pins, N` or `out pindirs, N` as you stream your bitmap data from DMA.
2026-03-29 19:58:43 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
