2026-03-29 20:13:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] Regarding your DMA calculation: your math is exactly right. 1248 bits per line requires 156
2026-03-29 20:13:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] bytes of memory per scanline.
2026-03-29 20:13:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 20:13:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] To implement this via DMA:
2026-03-29 20:13:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 20:13:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] 1.  Transfer Size: The PIO TX FIFO is 32 bits wide, so you would configure the DMA for
2026-03-29 20:13:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] 32-bit word transfers. 156 bytes divides cleanly into 39 word transfers per active scanline.
2026-03-29 20:13:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] 2.  Synchronization: The DMA pacing is handled automatically. The PIO state machine asserts
2026-03-29 20:13:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] a DREQ (Data Request) signal whenever there is room in the TX FIFO, ensuring the DMA transfers stay
2026-03-29 20:13:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] perfectly synced to your video timing.
2026-03-29 20:13:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] 3.  Data Consumption: To render the memory, the PIO program will need to replace the
2026-03-29 20:13:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] hardcoded `set` toggle pattern with an `out` instruction (e.g., shifting 1 bit at a time from the
2026-03-29 20:13:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] Output Shift Register to the pins).
2026-03-29 20:13:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] 4.  Chaining: To avoid CPU overhead, you can use a pair of chained DMA channels. When the
