2026-03-29 20:22:08 [nitro@ryzen5] first i think put line count 311 i want to put the 311 count in isr using bit magic
2026-03-29 20:22:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] To load a value larger than 31 (the 5-bit limit of the `set` instruction) directly within the PIO,
2026-03-29 20:22:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] you can split the binary representation of 311 (`100110111`) into two smaller chunks and shift them
2026-03-29 20:22:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] sequentially into the ISR.
2026-03-29 20:22:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 20:22:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] Here is the PIO instruction sequence to build 311:
2026-03-29 20:22:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 20:22:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 20:22:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	set x, 19      ; Upper 5 bits (10011)
2026-03-29 20:22:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	in x, 5        ; Shift into ISR
2026-03-29 20:22:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	set x, 7       ; Lower 4 bits (0111)
2026-03-29 20:22:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	in x, 4        ; Shift into ISR, completing 311
2026-03-29 20:22:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 20:22:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 20:22:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Note: For this to work correctly, you must configure the State Machine's `in` shift
2026-03-29 20:22:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] direction to left* in your C setup code, as the default is right:
2026-03-29 20:22:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 20:22:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 20:22:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] sm_config_set_in_shift(&config, false, false, 32);
2026-03-29 20:22:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 20:22:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 20:22:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] This replaces the `pull` and `mov isr, osr` instructions, freeing you from needing to push the
2026-03-29 20:22:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] value via the TX FIFO in C. Let me know if you'd like to explore other bit-packing tricks.
2026-03-29 20:22:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 20:23:20 [nitro@ryzen5] no i want to keep it as right shift, am used to it now, can we do it in 3?
2026-03-29 20:24:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] Because 311 requires 9 bits (`10011 0111` in binary) and the PIO `set` instruction can only load up
2026-03-29 20:24:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] to 5 bits at a time, it isn't possible to synthesize this specific value in exactly 3 instructions.
2026-03-29 20:24:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] We must use two `set` operations to build the value in halves.
2026-03-29 20:24:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 20:24:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] Using your preferred right-shift configuration, here is the bit magic to assemble 311 in exactly 4
2026-03-29 20:24:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] instructions:
2026-03-29 20:24:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 20:24:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 20:24:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	set	x, 7	; lower 4 bits (0111)
2026-03-29 20:24:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	in	x, 4	; push 4 bits into top of ISR
2026-03-29 20:24:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	set	x, 19	; upper 5 bits (10011)
2026-03-29 20:24:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	in	x, 28	; push 28 bits (10011 zero-extended), shifting the 0111 down to the bottom
2026-03-29 20:24:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 20:24:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 20:24:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] If PIO instruction space becomes too tight, keeping the `pull` from the C++ TX FIFO remains the
2026-03-29 20:24:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] most memory-efficient method, as it only costs 1 instruction.
2026-03-29 20:24:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 20:27:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] Your 4-instruction method for building 311 (`0x137`) in the ISR using right-shifts is very clever
2026-03-29 20:27:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] and works perfectly.
2026-03-29 20:27:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 20:27:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] Regarding your question on doing it in 3 instructions: unfortunately, 4 is the optimal minimum
2026-03-29 20:27:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] here.
2026-03-29 20:27:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 20:27:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] Because the `set` instruction can only load a maximum of 5 bits at a time, building a 9-bit value
2026-03-29 20:27:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] like 311 requires at least two `set` instructions to define the distinct bit patterns (in your
2026-03-29 20:27:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] case, `0111` and `10011`), plus two `in` instructions to shift them into their correct positions.
2026-03-29 20:27:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 20:27:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] Unless a number is composed of a single repeating 5-bit pattern, or you pull data from the
2026-03-29 20:27:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] OSR/pins, there isn't a native bitwise shortcut to reduce this specific constant to 3 instructions.
2026-03-29 20:27:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] Your current approach is tight and exactly how it should be done to maintain the right-shift
2026-03-29 20:27:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] default.
2026-03-29 20:27:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
