2026-03-29 20:39:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] A few observations on the new implementation:
2026-03-29 20:39:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 20:39:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] • ISR Bit Magic: Your shift logic to build `311` inside the PIO is spot on. Shifting `7`
2026-03-29 20:39:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] (`0111`) right by 28 places it at the bottom, and shifting `19` (`0x13`) into the top yields
2026-03-29 20:39:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] `0x00000137` (311). It's an elegant way to free up the C++ side and avoid initial FIFO seeding.
2026-03-29 20:39:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Pin Mappings: You correctly kept `smconfigsetsetpins` alongside the new
2026-03-29 20:39:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] `smconfigsetoutpins`. The RP2040 uses the `OUT` mapping for both `out pins` and
2026-03-29 20:39:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] `out pindirs`, so mixing `out pindirs` in the active loop and `set pindirs` in the blanking periods
2026-03-29 20:39:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] will work perfectly on the same pin.
2026-03-29 20:39:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Autopull & FIFO: Joining the TX FIFO to give yourself an 8-word deep buffer and enabling
2026-03-29 20:39:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] autopull at 32 bits is exactly the right approach. Since 32 is a multiple of your 4-bit loop, the
2026-03-29 20:39:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] autopull will trigger cleanly without breaking your 25-cycle timing.
2026-03-29 20:39:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 20:39:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] • One minor heads-up for the DMA phase:*
2026-03-29 20:39:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] Because `autopull` is enabled, the state machine will intentionally stall on the `out` instruction
2026-03-29 20:39:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] if the TX FIFO runs dry. You will just need to ensure the DMA channel is triggered and has fed the
2026-03-29 20:39:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] first few words into the FIFO before the PIO enters the `active` loop, otherwise the timing
2026-03-29 20:39:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] for that specific scanline will stretch.
2026-03-29 20:39:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
