2026-03-29 20:50:23 [nitro@ryzen5] there is "No Signal" so i guess we are stalling
2026-03-29 20:51:48 [gemini-3.1-pro] You are almost certainly stalling on the TX FIFO.
2026-03-29 20:51:48 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 20:51:48 [gemini-3.1-pro] In your main loop, `if (!piosmistxfifo_full(...))` only pushes one 32-bit
2026-03-29 20:51:48 [gemini-3.1-pro] word before moving on to check the time.
2026-03-29 20:51:48 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 20:51:48 [gemini-3.1-pro] Your PIO loop consumes a 32-bit word every 200 cycles (~1.33 µs at 150MHz). The
2026-03-29 20:51:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] `tomssince_boot()` function performs a 64-bit division under the hood, which takes a lot of
2026-03-29 20:51:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] CPU cycles. The CPU is simply falling behind the PIO, causing the FIFO to empty. When autopull
2026-03-29 20:51:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] triggers on an empty FIFO, the `out` instruction stalls, completely destroying your precise video
2026-03-29 20:51:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] timing and causing the TV to drop the signal.
2026-03-29 20:51:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 20:51:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] To fix this, you need to burst-fill the FIFO using a `while` loop, and it's best to swap the
2026-03-29 20:51:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] expensive time check for a cheap loop counter during this noise test.
2026-03-29 20:51:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 20:51:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] Replace your main `while` loop with this:
2026-03-29 20:51:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 20:51:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 20:51:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	uint32_t tick = 0;
2026-03-29 20:51:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	while (!ReadBootSel()) {
2026-03-29 20:51:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		// Burst-fill the FIFO until it's completely full
2026-03-29 20:51:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		while (!pio_sm_is_tx_fifo_full(pio, VidStateMachine)) {
2026-03-29 20:51:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] 			pio_sm_put(pio, VidStateMachine, noise());
2026-03-29 20:51:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		}
2026-03-29 20:51:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 20:51:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		// Cheap non-blocking LED toggle to avoid starving the PIO
2026-03-29 20:51:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		if (++tick >= 500000) {
2026-03-29 20:51:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] 			led = !led;
2026-03-29 20:51:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] 			gpio_put(LED_PIN, led);
2026-03-29 20:51:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] 			tick = 0;
2026-03-29 20:51:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		}
2026-03-29 20:51:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	}
2026-03-29 20:51:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 20:51:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 20:51:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] This will keep all 8 FIFO slots (256 bits) topped up, giving the CPU plenty of breathing room
2026-03-29 20:51:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] between fills. Give that a run and see if your TV syncs to the static!
