2026-03-29 20:54:18 [nitro@ryzen5] still no sync, is sm_config_set_out_pins the oneout	pindirs command
2026-03-29 20:54:57 [gemini-3.1-pro] Yes, `smconfigsetoutpins` is the correct function. In the RP2040/RP2350
2026-03-29 20:54:57 [gemini-3.1-pro] architecture, the `out` pin mapping applies to both `out pins` and `out pindirs`.
2026-03-29 20:54:57 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 20:54:57 [gemini-3.1-pro] However, your PIO is stalling because the TX FIFO is starving, which completely breaks your sync
2026-03-29 20:54:57 [gemini-3.1-pro] timings.
2026-03-29 20:54:57 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 20:54:57 [gemini-3.1-pro] Here is what is happening:
2026-03-29 20:54:57 [gemini-3.1-pro] 1. Your `stripe` loop runs 312 times per line, executing four `out pindirs, 1` instructions per
2026-03-29 20:54:57 [gemini-3.1-pro] loop.
2026-03-29 20:54:57 [gemini-3.1-pro] 2. This consumes 1,248 bits (39 words) every 52 µs.
2026-03-29 20:54:57 [gemini-3.1-pro] 3. Your joined TX FIFO can only buffer 8 words.
2026-03-29 20:54:57 [gemini-3.1-pro] 4. Inside your main `while` loop, you are calling `ReadBootSel()`, which contains a `for (volatile
2026-03-29 20:54:57 [gemini-3.1-pro] int i = 0; i < 1000; ++i);` blocking delay.
2026-03-29 20:54:57 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 20:54:57 [gemini-3.1-pro] Because of that delay, the C++ loop cannot feed the FIFO fast enough. Once the FIFO empties, the
2026-03-29 20:54:57 [gemini-3.1-pro] PIO's `autopull` stalls the state machine on the `out` instruction until new data arrives. This
2026-03-29 20:54:57 [gemini-3.1-pro] stretches your scanline and destroys the carefully timed sync pulses, resulting in the TV seeing
2026-03-29 20:54:57 [gemini-3.1-pro] "No Signal".
2026-03-29 20:54:57 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 20:54:57 [gemini-3.1-pro] To fix the stall, remove `ReadBootSel()` from your tight FIFO-filling loop, or only poll it
2026-03-29 20:54:57 [gemini-3.1-pro] occasionally using your `currentMillis` timer.
2026-03-29 20:54:57 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 20:58:05 [gemini-3.1-pro] Yes, `smconfigsetoutpins` is exactly the right command. In the RP2040/RP2350 PIO
2026-03-29 20:58:05 [gemini-3.1-pro] architecture, both the `out pins` and `out pindirs` instructions share the exact same `OUT` pin
2026-03-29 20:58:05 [gemini-3.1-pro] mapping. There is no separate setup function for `pindirs`.
2026-03-29 20:58:05 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 20:58:05 [gemini-3.1-pro] Your setup is actually completely solid:
2026-03-29 20:58:05 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Pin Mapping: `smconfigsetoutpins` maps the `OUT` instructions to
2026-03-29 20:58:05 [gemini-3.1-pro] `VID_PIN`.
2026-03-29 20:58:05 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Autopull: `smconfigsetoutshift` enables autopull with a threshold of 32,
2026-03-29 20:58:05 [gemini-3.1-pro] which perfectly feeds your `out pindirs, 1` instructions.
2026-03-29 20:58:05 [gemini-3.1-pro] • FIFO: Your C++ `while` loop successfully saturates the TX FIFO with your Xorshift noise,
2026-03-29 20:58:05 [gemini-3.1-pro] so the PIO will not stall.
2026-03-29 20:58:05 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Bit Magic: Your `in` shift trick to build `311` in the ISR is brilliant and works
2026-03-29 20:58:05 [gemini-3.1-pro] perfectly because the default shift direction is right.
2026-03-29 20:58:05 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 20:58:05 [gemini-3.1-pro] If you are getting a "No Signal" on the TV, the most likely culprit is your analog voltage
2026-03-29 20:58:05 [gemini-3.1-pro] levels via the trimpot, rather than the code.
2026-03-29 20:58:05 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 20:58:05 [gemini-3.1-pro] Here is why:
2026-03-29 20:58:05 [gemini-3.1-pro] When the PIO outputs a blanking level (pin set to input/floating), the voltage at the TV is formed
2026-03-29 20:58:05 [gemini-3.1-pro] by a voltage divider: 3.3V flows through your trimpot, then the 330Ω resistor, then the TV's
2026-03-29 20:58:05 [gemini-3.1-pro] internal 75Ω resistor to ground.
2026-03-29 20:58:05 [gemini-3.1-pro] • If your trimpot is set to ~3.3kΩ, the total resistance is high. The blanking voltage
2026-03-29 20:58:05 [gemini-3.1-pro] drops to around ~66mV.
2026-03-29 20:58:05 [gemini-3.1-pro] • During sync (pin driven low), the voltage is 0V.
2026-03-29 20:58:05 [gemini-3.1-pro] • The TV expects a difference of at least ~300mV between the sync tip and the blanking
2026-03-29 20:58:05 [gemini-3.1-pro] level. A 66mV difference is too small for the TV's sync separator to detect, resulting in "No
2026-03-29 20:58:05 [gemini-3.1-pro] Signal".
2026-03-29 20:58:05 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 20:58:05 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Recommendation:*
2026-03-29 20:58:05 [gemini-3.1-pro] Try adjusting your trimpot down to roughly 470Ω - 680Ω. This will raise your floating
2026-03-29 20:58:05 [gemini-3.1-pro] blanking voltage to roughly 0.25V - 0.3V, giving the TV a clear, standard sync pulse to
2026-03-29 20:58:05 [gemini-3.1-pro] lock onto.
2026-03-29 20:58:05 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 20:58:05 [gemini-3.1-pro] • (Side note: Your cycle counting is excellent. The horizontal line totals 9,622 cycles, which is
2026-03-29 20:58:05 [gemini-3.1-pro] 64.14µs at 150MHz. This is well within the tolerance of almost any PAL display.)*
2026-03-29 20:58:05 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
