2026-03-29 21:08:13 [nitro@ryzen5] in 32 bit words which bit is shifted first with the out?
2026-03-29 21:08:31 [gemini-3.1-pro] Excellent news on getting the signal working.
2026-03-29 21:08:31 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 21:08:31 [gemini-3.1-pro] Because your configuration uses `smconfigsetoutshift(&config, true, true, 32);`,
2026-03-29 21:08:31 [gemini-3.1-pro] the second parameter (`shift_right`) is set to `true`.
2026-03-29 21:08:31 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 21:08:31 [gemini-3.1-pro] This means the Least Significant Bit (LSB) (bit 0) is shifted out first. If you were to
2026-03-29 21:08:31 [gemini-3.1-pro] change that parameter to `false`, it would shift left, outputting the Most Significant Bit (MSB)
2026-03-29 21:08:31 [gemini-3.1-pro] (bit 31) first.
2026-03-29 21:08:31 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 21:11:14 [nitro@ryzen5] in old basics like apple was there an opposite to plot that cleared a pixel?
2026-03-29 21:11:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] Excellent news on getting the signal working!
2026-03-29 21:11:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 21:11:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] To answer your questions:
2026-03-29 21:11:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 21:11:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 1. PIO Out Shift Direction*
2026-03-29 21:11:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] In your configuration, you are using `smconfigsetoutshift(&config, true, true,
2026-03-29 21:11:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] 32)`. The first `true` sets the `shift_right` parameter. Because it is shifting right, the
2026-03-29 21:11:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] Least Significant Bit (LSB, bit 0) is shifted out first. If you set it to `false` (shift
2026-03-29 21:11:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] left), the Most Significant Bit (MSB, bit 31) would be shifted out first.
2026-03-29 21:11:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 21:11:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 2. Clearing Pixels in Retro BASICs*
2026-03-29 21:11:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] It depended heavily on the specific computer's dialect of BASIC:
2026-03-29 21:11:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Sinclair ZX Spectrum: Had a literal opposite command called `UNPLOT X,Y`.
2026-03-29 21:11:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Apple II (Applesoft BASIC): Had no dedicated clear command. You had to set the color to
2026-03-29 21:11:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] black using `COLOR=0` (low-res) or `HCOLOR=0` (high-res) and then call `PLOT X,Y` or `HPLOT X,Y`
2026-03-29 21:11:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] over the existing pixel.
2026-03-29 21:11:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] • BBC Micro: Used logical operation codes within the plot command itself (e.g., `PLOT
2026-03-29 21:11:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] 71,X,Y` to clear, or `PLOT 70,X,Y` to invert).
2026-03-29 21:11:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 21:12:11 [nitro@ryzen5] is this ok
2026-03-29 21:12:13 [nitro@ryzen5] const int wordCount=39*312;uint32_t frame[wordCount];void plot(int x,int y){	uint32_t bit=1<<(x&31);	int offset=(x>>5)+y*39;	frame[offset]|=bit;}void unplot(int x,int y){	uint32_t bit=1<<(x&31);	int offset=(x>>5)+y*39;	frame[offset]&=~bit;}
2026-03-29 21:12:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] In Apple II Applesoft BASIC, there wasn't a specific unplot command; you just changed the color to
2026-03-29 21:12:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] black (`COLOR=0` for low-res or `HCOLOR=0` for hi-res) and used `PLOT` or `HPLOT` again. Sinclair
2026-03-29 21:12:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] ZX Spectrum BASIC, however, famously had an `UNPLOT X,Y` command.
2026-03-29 21:12:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 21:12:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] Your code logic is spot on for 1248 pixels across (39 words * 32 bits), and mapping `x=0` to bit 0
2026-03-29 21:12:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] perfectly matches your right-shifting PIO configuration.
2026-03-29 21:12:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 21:12:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] The only required change is to use `1u` instead of `1` when shifting. In C++, `1` is a signed
2026-03-29 21:12:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] integer, and shifting a 1 into the 31st (sign) bit invokes undefined behavior.
2026-03-29 21:12:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 21:12:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] Here is the safer version:
2026-03-29 21:12:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 21:12:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 21:12:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] const int wordCount = 39 * 312;
2026-03-29 21:12:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] uint32_t frame[wordCount];
2026-03-29 21:12:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 21:12:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] void plot(int x, int y) {
2026-03-29 21:12:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	uint32_t bit = 1u << (x & 31);
2026-03-29 21:12:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	int offset = (x >> 5) + y * 39;
2026-03-29 21:12:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	frame[offset] |= bit;
2026-03-29 21:12:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] }
2026-03-29 21:12:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 21:12:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] void unplot(int x, int y) {
2026-03-29 21:12:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	uint32_t bit = 1u << (x & 31);
2026-03-29 21:12:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	int offset = (x >> 5) + y * 39;
2026-03-29 21:12:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	frame[offset] &= ~bit;
2026-03-29 21:12:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] }
2026-03-29 21:12:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 21:12:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 21:16:44 [nitro@ryzen5] does pico have sinf?
2026-03-29 21:16:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] Yes, the Pico SDK fully supports `sinf()` via the standard `<math.h>` (or `<cmath>`) library.
2026-03-29 21:16:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 21:16:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] The SDK automatically routes floating-point math functions to highly optimized routines (using the
2026-03-29 21:16:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] Bootrom on the RP2040, and the hardware FPU/Bootrom on the RP2350), making it very efficient.
2026-03-29 21:16:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-29 21:16:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] That said, if you plan to calculate sine values on the fly during your active display loop,
2026-03-29 21:16:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] pre-calculating those values into an integer lookup table (LUT) during setup will still be much
2026-03-29 21:16:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] faster and leave you with more CPU cycles for other effects.
2026-03-29 21:16:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 05:25:19 [gemini-3.1-pro] Thank you for sharing the code. Here are a few technical observations on the implementation:
2026-03-30 05:25:19 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 05:25:19 [gemini-3.1-pro] • PIO Constant Generation: Building the loop counter constant (311) directly in the ISR
2026-03-30 05:25:19 [gemini-3.1-pro] using `set` and `in` (`19 << 4 | 7`) is an excellent technique. It avoids consuming a setup word
2026-03-30 05:25:19 [gemini-3.1-pro] from the TX FIFO, leaving it entirely dedicated to streaming the framebuffer.
2026-03-30 05:25:19 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 1-Pin Video DAC: Generating the video signal voltages by toggling `pindirs` between
2026-03-30 05:25:19 [gemini-3.1-pro] High-Z and driven-low against a passive resistor network is a highly efficient hardware
2026-03-30 05:25:19 [gemini-3.1-pro] minimization.
2026-03-30 05:25:19 [gemini-3.1-pro] • RP2350 Compatibility: The `ReadBootSel` function correctly handles the architectural
2026-03-30 05:25:19 [gemini-3.1-pro] differences in the RP2350 QSPI status registers
2026-03-30 05:25:19 [gemini-3.1-pro] (`IOQSPIGPIOQSPISSSTATUSINFROMPAD_BITS`) while maintaining RP2040 backwards
2026-03-30 05:25:19 [gemini-3.1-pro] compatibility.
2026-03-30 05:25:19 [gemini-3.1-pro] • PAL Timing: The cycle counting in the PIO state machine at 150MHz aligns precisely with
2026-03-30 05:25:19 [gemini-3.1-pro] PAL standard timing (64µs per line, divided into 52µs active and 12µs horizontal blanking).
2026-03-30 05:25:19 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
