2026-03-30 05:26:25 [nitro@ryzen5] what is involved in going from 288p to 576i? current 288p is stable on test tv
2026-03-30 05:26:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] To transition from 288p to 576i, the fundamental change is shifting from a progressive 312-line
2026-03-30 05:26:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] frame to an interlaced 625-line frame, which is split across two alternating fields (312.5 lines
2026-03-30 05:26:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] each).
2026-03-30 05:26:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 05:26:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] Here are the primary architectural changes involved:
2026-03-30 05:26:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 05:26:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] • The Half-Line Shift: The core of interlacing is offsetting the vertical sync by exactly
2026-03-30 05:26:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] half a scanline (32 µs) every other field. This physical timing shift is what interweaves the odd
2026-03-30 05:26:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] and even scanlines on the CRT.
2026-03-30 05:26:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Sync Generation (PIO): Your current 288p implementation uses a simplified, repeating
2026-03-30 05:26:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] vertical sync pulse. 576i technically requires "equalizing pulses" (short pulses at twice the
2026-03-30 05:26:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] horizontal frequency) during the vertical blanking interval. This keeps the CRT's horizontal
2026-03-30 05:26:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] oscillator stable during the half-line shift.
2026-03-30 05:26:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Instruction Limits: Adding interlaced sync and equalizing pulses will exceed the
2026-03-30 05:26:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] 32-instruction limit of a single PIO state machine. You will likely need to split the workload
2026-03-30 05:26:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] (e.g., one state machine for sync, one for pixels) or encode sync timings into the data stream fed
2026-03-30 05:26:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] by the CPU/DMA.
2026-03-30 05:26:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Field Alternation: The PIO needs a mechanism to alternate between Field 1 (even) and
2026-03-30 05:26:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] Field 2 (odd) timings.
2026-03-30 05:26:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Buffer Management (C++): The `frame` buffer height must double to 576. The CPU must feed
2026-03-30 05:26:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] the PIO with even lines (0, 2, 4...) during the first field, and odd lines (1, 3, 5...) during the
2026-03-30 05:26:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] second field.
2026-03-30 05:26:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 05:26:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] Let me know if you would like to explore specific implementation strategies for the PIO limitations
2026-03-30 05:26:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] or if you would like to see code examples for the C++ buffer management.
2026-03-30 05:26:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
