2026-03-30 05:32:42 [nitro@ryzen5] 	; 50p = 3 x 9600 = 28800 = 32 x 30 x 30	; 50i = 3.5 x 9600 = 33600 = 32 x 30 x 30	set	x,31vblank:	set	y,29vsync:		jmp	y--,vsync [29]	jmp	x--,vblank
2026-03-30 05:33:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] To move from 288p to 576i, the total number of lines across two consecutive fields must equal
2026-03-30 05:33:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] exactly 625.
2026-03-30 05:33:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 05:33:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] Currently, your 288p setup likely outputs the same number of lines (typically 312) every single
2026-03-30 05:33:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] field. Because 312 + 312 = 624 (an even number), the CRT beam traces over the exact same path every
2026-03-30 05:33:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] time, resulting in a progressive image.
2026-03-30 05:33:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 05:33:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] Here is what is involved in achieving 576i:
2026-03-30 05:33:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 05:33:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 1. Alternating Field Lengths*
2026-03-30 05:33:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] Adding 0.5 lines of cycles to your vsync statically (as in your snippet) will not create an
2026-03-30 05:33:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] interlaced image; it will just create a non-standard progressive signal. To interlace, the timing
2026-03-30 05:33:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] must alternate every field.
2026-03-30 05:33:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Field 1 (Odd): Output 312 lines total.
2026-03-30 05:33:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Field 2 (Even): Output 313 lines total.
2026-03-30 05:33:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 05:33:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] Because 312 + 313 = 625 (an odd number), the TV's sync circuitry will naturally offset the second
2026-03-30 05:33:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] field by half a scanline, interlacing the new scanlines between the previous ones.
2026-03-30 05:33:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 05:33:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 2. PIO Implementation Strategy*
2026-03-30 05:33:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] Because the PIO is limited to 32 instructions, you likely won't have enough space to unroll both
2026-03-30 05:33:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] Field 1 and Field 2 into a single `.wrap` loop.
2026-03-30 05:33:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 05:33:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] The most reliable approach is to control the alternating timing from your C++ code:
2026-03-30 05:33:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Remove the hardcoded `set x, 31` for your vblank loop in the PIO.
2026-03-30 05:33:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] • At the start of the `vblank` routine, use a `pull` instruction to read a delay value from the TX
2026-03-30 05:33:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] FIFO.
2026-03-30 05:33:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] • In your C++ code, push the cycle count for 312 lines during Field 1, and push the cycle count for
2026-03-30 05:33:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 313 lines during Field 2, right before you begin pushing the pixel data for that field.
2026-03-30 05:33:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 05:33:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 3. Pixel Data Sourcing*
2026-03-30 05:33:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] For true 576i, your frame buffer will need to be updated to hold the full 576 active lines. Your
2026-03-30 05:33:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] C++ code will need to push the odd lines (1, 3, 5...) during Field 1, and the even lines (2, 4,
2026-03-30 05:33:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 6...) during Field 2.
2026-03-30 05:33:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 05:33:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] Let me know if you would like an example of how to implement the `pull` logic in your PIO assembly.
2026-03-30 05:33:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 05:34:49 [nitro@ryzen5] i want to focus on just the vsync please
2026-03-30 05:35:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] To move from 288p to 576i, the VSYNC timing must shift by exactly half a horizontal line (4800
2026-03-30 05:35:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] cycles at 150MHz) every other field.
2026-03-30 05:35:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 05:35:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] In a 625-line interlaced signal, Field 1 has 312.5 lines and Field 2 has 312.5 lines. This
2026-03-30 05:35:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] half-line offset is what tells the TV to draw the next set of scanlines between the previous ones.
2026-03-30 05:35:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 05:35:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] If you hardcode VSYNC to 3.5 lines (33600 cycles) for every loop, the TV will likely interpret it
2026-03-30 05:35:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] as a non-standard progressive signal rather than an interlaced one.
2026-03-30 05:35:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 05:35:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] To implement this in your PIO, you need a way to alternate the VSYNC cycle count per field. The two
2026-03-30 05:35:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] most common approaches are:
2026-03-30 05:35:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 05:35:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] 1. Feed the VSYNC duration from C: At the start of the `.wrap_target`, use a `pull`
2026-03-30 05:35:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] instruction to read a timing value from the TX FIFO. The C code then alternates sending the cycle
2026-03-30 05:35:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] count for 3 lines (Field 1) and 3.5 lines (Field 2) before sending the pixel data.
2026-03-30 05:35:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] 2. Toggle a PIO register: If you have a spare scratch register (like `y` or `x` if
2026-03-30 05:35:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] saved/restored), you can use it as a boolean flag to branch to a standard VSYNC loop or an extended
2026-03-30 05:35:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] VSYNC loop, flipping the flag at the end of each field.
2026-03-30 05:35:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 05:35:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] If you would like an example of either approach in PIO assembly, please let me know.
2026-03-30 05:35:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 05:39:22 [nitro@ryzen5] every field is 3.5 lines of vsync - yes or no
2026-03-30 05:39:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] No. Standard PAL broad VSYNC pulses last for exactly 2.5 lines, not 3.5.
2026-03-30 05:39:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 05:39:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] To go from 288p to 576i, the critical change is introducing a half-line offset between your
2026-03-30 05:39:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] alternating fields. This is what tells the CRT to draw the second set of lines between the first
2026-03-30 05:39:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] set.
2026-03-30 05:39:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 05:39:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] Here is what is involved for the VSYNC timing:
2026-03-30 05:39:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 05:39:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] 1. Alternating Fields: A full 576i frame is 625 lines, split into two fields of 312.5 lines
2026-03-30 05:39:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] each.
2026-03-30 05:39:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] 2. Field 1 (Odd): VSYNC starts at the very beginning of a horizontal line.
2026-03-30 05:39:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] 3. Field 2 (Even): VSYNC starts exactly halfway through a horizontal line (a 32 µs / 4800
2026-03-30 05:39:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] cycle offset).
2026-03-30 05:39:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 05:39:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] In a strict broadcast-standard PAL signal, the VSYNC sequence for each field consists of:
2026-03-30 05:39:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 2.5 lines of pre-equalizing pulses
2026-03-30 05:39:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 2.5 lines of broad VSYNC pulses (this is the actual vertical sync)
2026-03-30 05:39:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 2.5 lines of post-equalizing pulses
2026-03-30 05:39:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 05:39:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] If you are using a simplified sync (like your current 3-line solid low pulse), you can often omit
2026-03-30 05:39:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] the equalizing pulses for modern TVs. However, you absolutely must alternate that half-line delay
2026-03-30 05:39:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] before the VSYNC pulse every other field to achieve the interlacing effect. Changing every field to
2026-03-30 05:39:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] have a static 3.5-line VSYNC will not create an interlaced image.
2026-03-30 05:39:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 05:50:25 [nitro@ryzen5] what ops can i do on y register in pio?
2026-03-30 05:50:46 [gemini-3.1-pro] Going from 288p to 576i requires alternating the timing of your fields to create a half-line
2026-03-30 05:50:46 [gemini-3.1-pro] offset.
2026-03-30 05:50:46 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 05:50:46 [gemini-3.1-pro] Here are the answers to your specific questions:
2026-03-30 05:50:46 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 05:50:46 [gemini-3.1-pro] • What is involved in going from 288p to 576i?*
2026-03-30 05:50:46 [gemini-3.1-pro] You must output 312.5 lines per field instead of an integer 312 lines. Field 1 (odd) starts with a
2026-03-30 05:50:46 [gemini-3.1-pro] full line and ends with a half line. Field 2 (even) starts with a half line and ends with a full
2026-03-30 05:50:46 [gemini-3.1-pro] line. Because the PIO loops, you typically either need to double your PIO program length to handle
2026-03-30 05:50:46 [gemini-3.1-pro] both fields in one `.wrap` loop, or use the TX FIFO to signal the PIO which VSYNC pattern to
2026-03-30 05:50:46 [gemini-3.1-pro] execute.
2026-03-30 05:50:46 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 05:50:46 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Do I need to add some cycles to vsync?*
2026-03-30 05:50:46 [gemini-3.1-pro] Yes. To achieve the half-line offset, the VSYNC period timing differs between the even and odd
2026-03-30 05:50:46 [gemini-3.1-pro] fields.
2026-03-30 05:50:46 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 05:50:46 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Every field is 3.5 lines of vsync - yes or no?*
2026-03-30 05:50:46 [gemini-3.1-pro] No. Standard PAL uses 2.5 lines of broad pulses (the actual vertical sync), preceded by 2.5 lines
2026-03-30 05:50:46 [gemini-3.1-pro] of pre-equalizing pulses, and followed by 2.5 lines of post-equalizing pulses. Simplified
2026-03-30 05:50:46 [gemini-3.1-pro] microcontroller PAL often just holds sync low for 2.5 or 3 lines, but 3.5 is non-standard.
2026-03-30 05:50:46 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 05:50:46 [gemini-3.1-pro] • What ops can I do on the Y register in PIO?*
2026-03-30 05:50:47 [gemini-3.1-pro] The RP2040/RP2350 PIO instruction set is highly restricted. For the `Y` scratch register, you can
2026-03-30 05:50:47 [gemini-3.1-pro] only perform the following:
2026-03-30 05:50:47 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 05:50:47 [gemini-3.1-pro] • `set y, <value>`: Load a 5-bit immediate value (0-31).
2026-03-30 05:50:47 [gemini-3.1-pro] • `mov y, <source>`: Copy data from `X`, `ISR`, `OSR`, `PINS`, `STATUS`, or `NULL`.
2026-03-30 05:50:47 [gemini-3.1-pro]        Modifiers:* You can invert the source (`~source`) or bit-reverse it (`::source`)
2026-03-30 05:50:47 [gemini-3.1-pro] during the move.
2026-03-30 05:50:47 [gemini-3.1-pro] • `jmp y--, <label>`: Decrement `Y` and jump if the value before decrementing was
2026-03-30 05:50:47 [gemini-3.1-pro] non-zero.
2026-03-30 05:50:47 [gemini-3.1-pro] • `in y, <bitcount>`: Shift the contents of `Y` into the `ISR`.
2026-03-30 05:50:47 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 05:50:47 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Note: There are no direct arithmetic instructions (add, subtract, multiply) other than the
2026-03-30 05:50:47 [gemini-3.1-pro] decrement built into the jump instruction.*
2026-03-30 05:50:47 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
