2026-03-30 10:03:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] Why VSYNC is jumpy
2026-03-30 10:03:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] For a stable progressive PAL signal (288p), the TV expects exactly 312 lines per frame.
2026-03-30 10:03:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 10:03:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] Currently, your `active` loop generates 312 lines (`frame[1]=311`), and then your `vsync` block
2026-03-30 10:03:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] generates an additional ~2.5 lines. This results in a frame length of ~314.5 lines, causing
2026-03-30 10:03:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] the TV to constantly lose vertical sync.
2026-03-30 10:03:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 10:03:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] The Fix
2026-03-30 10:03:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] To stabilize the image, the total frame must be exactly 312 lines. You can achieve this by reducing
2026-03-30 10:03:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] the active lines to 309, and padding your VSYNC block to be exactly 3 lines (28,800 cycles).
2026-03-30 10:03:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 10:03:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] Your current VSYNC block and overhead takes exactly 24,072 cycles. If you set `frame[0] = 4728`,
2026-03-30 10:03:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] the `lace` loop will consume the remaining 4,728 cycles, making the VBLANK period exactly 28,800
2026-03-30 10:03:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] cycles (3 lines).
2026-03-30 10:03:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 10:03:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] • (309 active lines + 3 VSYNC lines = 312 lines).*
2026-03-30 10:03:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 10:03:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Important: Because you are reducing the active lines to 309, you must* also
2026-03-30 10:03:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] reduce `wordCount` in your C code. If the C code pushes more pixel data than the PIO consumes, the
2026-03-30 10:03:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] FIFO will misalign and your PIO will read pixel data as the next frame's control words.
2026-03-30 10:03:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
