2026-03-30 10:10:19 [gemini-3.1-pro] Regarding your Samsung LCD TV and interlacing:
2026-03-30 10:10:19 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 10:10:19 [gemini-3.1-pro] 1.  VSYNC Stability: The reason your 3-line vsync (28,800 cycles) is "stable as a rock" is
2026-03-30 10:10:19 [gemini-3.1-pro] that it forces the TV to interpret the signal as 288p (progressive scan). This is a classic trick
2026-03-30 10:10:19 [gemini-3.1-pro] used by vintage computers and consoles. True 576i requires a precise half-line offset between the
2026-03-30 10:10:19 [gemini-3.1-pro] odd and even fields. Without that alternating half-line timing, sending a 2.5-line sync pulse will
2026-03-30 10:10:19 [gemini-3.1-pro] often cause the TV's sync separator to fail, resulting in a rolling or dropped image.
2026-03-30 10:10:19 [gemini-3.1-pro] 2.  Flicker at 704x576: While your Samsung TV has a built-in hardware deinterlacer to
2026-03-30 10:10:19 [gemini-3.1-pro] handle 576i, generating a true interlaced signal for a computer interface usually results in
2026-03-30 10:10:19 [gemini-3.1-pro] noticeable flicker. Sharp, 1-pixel high horizontal lines (like text or UI borders) will flash at 25
2026-03-30 10:10:19 [gemini-3.1-pro] Hz because they only exist in one of the two fields.
2026-03-30 10:10:19 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 10:10:19 [gemini-3.1-pro] If your goal is a clean, stable image for graphics or text, sticking with the 288p (3-line vsync)
2026-03-30 10:10:19 [gemini-3.1-pro] approach is highly recommended over attempting 576i.
