2026-03-30 10:27:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] Your 1-pin, 3-level DAC using `pindirs` for Hi-Z black levels is an excellent hardware trick.
2026-03-30 10:27:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 10:27:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] To achieve a stable 576i PAL output, the timing requires exact adherence to the 625-line
2026-03-30 10:27:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] specification (312.5 lines per field). At 150 MHz, each line is 9,600 cycles, meaning every field
2026-03-30 10:27:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] must be exactly 3,000,000 cycles.
2026-03-30 10:27:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 10:27:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] Currently, your PIO program outputs 312 active lines plus a fixed VSYNC, resulting in roughly 314.6
2026-03-30 10:27:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] lines per field. While your C code adds a 4,800-cycle (0.5 line) delay to alternate fields, it
2026-03-30 10:27:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] doesn't maintain the strict 3,000,000 cycle total per field. This slight deviation is likely
2026-03-30 10:27:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] causing the interlacing to fail or jitter on the TV.
2026-03-30 10:27:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 10:27:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] To fix this and achieve cycle-accurate 576i:
2026-03-30 10:27:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] 1. Reduce the active display to the standard 288 lines.
2026-03-30 10:27:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] 2. Divide the remaining 24.5 lines (235,200 cycles) into VSYNC, Pre-Blank, and Post-Blank phases.
2026-03-30 10:27:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] 3. Push these exact phase cycle counts from C to the PIO via the FIFO.
2026-03-30 10:27:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] 4. For Even fields, shift exactly 4,800 cycles (0.5 lines) from Post-Blank to Pre-Blank compared to
2026-03-30 10:27:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] Odd fields. This creates the exact 0.5 line offset for the active video while keeping the total
2026-03-30 10:27:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] field length perfectly locked at 3,000,000 cycles.
2026-03-30 10:27:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 10:27:14 [gemini-3.1-pro] Would you like me to provide the updated `vidblit.pio` and `vidblit.cpp` files implementing these
2026-03-30 10:27:14 [gemini-3.1-pro] timings?
2026-03-30 10:27:14 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 10:29:24 [nitro@ryzen5] no code, just discussion, so active lines per field = 288?
2026-03-30 10:29:34 [gemini-3.1-pro] Yes, exactly. For standard 576i PAL, there are 576 active (visible) lines per full frame. Because
2026-03-30 10:29:34 [gemini-3.1-pro] interlaced video divides the frame into two fields (odd and even), that gives you exactly 288
2026-03-30 10:29:34 [gemini-3.1-pro] active lines per field.
2026-03-30 10:29:34 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 10:29:34 [gemini-3.1-pro] To round out the math for the signal timing: a full PAL frame consists of 625 total lines, which
2026-03-30 10:29:34 [gemini-3.1-pro] translates to 312.5 total lines per field. Subtracting your 288 active lines leaves 24.5 lines per
2026-03-30 10:29:34 [gemini-3.1-pro] field dedicated to the Vertical Blanking Interval (VBI), which accommodates your vertical sync
2026-03-30 10:29:34 [gemini-3.1-pro] pulses and front/back porches.
2026-03-30 10:29:34 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 10:29:34 [gemini-3.1-pro] Your approach of toggling the vsync delay offset between fields is the standard way to achieve that
2026-03-30 10:29:34 [gemini-3.1-pro] half-line shift required for true interlacing.
2026-03-30 10:29:34 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
