2026-03-30 10:32:46 [gemini-3.1-pro] Yes, exactly. A standard 576i PAL signal has 576 visible lines per complete frame, which divides
2026-03-30 10:32:46 [gemini-3.1-pro] perfectly into 288 active lines per interlaced field. Your modification correctly aligns the FIFO
2026-03-30 10:32:46 [gemini-3.1-pro] data transfer with this visible region.
2026-03-30 10:32:46 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 10:32:46 [gemini-3.1-pro] Since you invited comments on the content, there is one timing detail worth discussing for your
2026-03-30 10:32:46 [gemini-3.1-pro] goal of a stable 576i output.
2026-03-30 10:32:46 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 10:32:46 [gemini-3.1-pro] A standard PAL field contains exactly 312.5 lines. Your current PIO implementation outputs a
2026-03-30 10:32:46 [gemini-3.1-pro] 2.5-line vertical sync pulse and then immediately loops through the 288 active video lines. This
2026-03-30 10:32:46 [gemini-3.1-pro] results in a total of 290.5 lines per field.
2026-03-30 10:32:46 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 10:32:46 [gemini-3.1-pro] The missing ~22 lines make up the rest of the Vertical Blanking Interval (VBI). Standard displays
2026-03-30 10:32:46 [gemini-3.1-pro] expect these lines to contain standard horizontal sync pulses but with blank (black) video data.
2026-03-30 10:32:46 [gemini-3.1-pro] While some forgiving displays might synchronize to a 290.5-line field, strict CRT televisions
2026-03-30 10:32:46 [gemini-3.1-pro] usually require the full 312.5 lines to keep the vertical hold stable and prevent the image from
2026-03-30 10:32:46 [gemini-3.1-pro] rolling or shifting.
2026-03-30 10:32:46 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 10:32:46 [gemini-3.1-pro] To achieve standard compliance later, you may eventually need to insert those blank lines between
2026-03-30 10:32:46 [gemini-3.1-pro] the vertical sync and the start of your active bitmap.
2026-03-30 10:32:46 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 10:32:46 [gemini-3.1-pro] Please let me know if you would like to discuss strategies for handling the VBI in your PIO state
2026-03-30 10:32:46 [gemini-3.1-pro] machine or C++ logic.
2026-03-30 10:32:46 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 10:34:17 [nitro@ryzen5] so for blank, pre sync and post sections will use how many lines each?
2026-03-30 10:34:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] For a standard PAL 576i signal (625 lines total, 50Hz interlaced), each field consists of 312.5
2026-03-30 10:34:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] lines. Since you are using 288 active lines per field, you have 24.5 lines of vertical blanking.
2026-03-30 10:34:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 10:34:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] The standard broadcast breakdown for those 24.5 blanking lines per field is:
2026-03-30 10:34:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Pre-sync (Equalizing pulses): 2.5 lines
2026-03-30 10:34:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Vertical Sync (Broad pulses): 2.5 lines (which aligns perfectly with the 24,000 cycles
2026-03-30 10:34:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] mentioned in your PIO comments)
2026-03-30 10:34:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Post-sync (Equalizing pulses + plain blanking): 19.5 lines
2026-03-30 10:34:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 10:34:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] If you are using simplified 312-line fields (often used for 288p or a slightly non-standard stable
2026-03-30 10:34:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] 576i), the 24 blanking lines are typically divided as:
2026-03-30 10:34:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Pre-sync: 2.5 lines
2026-03-30 10:34:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Vertical Sync: 2.5 lines
2026-03-30 10:34:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Post-sync: 19 lines
2026-03-30 10:34:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 10:34:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] To achieve true 576i interlacing, the half-line shift needs to alternate between fields. Are you
2026-03-30 10:34:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] planning to implement the half-line offset for strict broadcast compliance, or are you sticking to
2026-03-30 10:34:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] a whole-line approximation?
2026-03-30 10:34:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 10:35:35 [nitro@ryzen5] i would like to aim at broadcast compliant timing
2026-03-30 10:35:55 [gemini-3.1-pro] For broadcast-compliant 576i PAL (System I/B/G), a full frame consists of 625 lines, interlaced as
2026-03-30 10:35:55 [gemini-3.1-pro] two fields of 312.5 lines. With 288 active lines per field, the vertical blanking interval (VBI)
2026-03-30 10:35:55 [gemini-3.1-pro] occupies the remaining 24.5 lines per field.
2026-03-30 10:35:55 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 10:35:55 [gemini-3.1-pro] Here is the standard broadcast breakdown for those 24.5 non-active lines:
2026-03-30 10:35:55 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 10:35:55 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Pre-equalizing pulses: 2.5 lines (5 half-line pulses)
2026-03-30 10:35:55 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Vertical Sync (Broad) pulses: 2.5 lines (5 half-line pulses)
2026-03-30 10:35:55 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Post-equalizing pulses: 2.5 lines (5 half-line pulses)
2026-03-30 10:35:56 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Vertical Blanking (Black lines): 17 lines (Field 1) or 16.5 lines (Field 2)
2026-03-30 10:35:56 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 10:35:56 [gemini-3.1-pro] To achieve true broadcast-compliant interlace, the sync and equalizing pulses during the first 7.5
2026-03-30 10:35:56 [gemini-3.1-pro] lines of the VBI must be generated at twice the normal horizontal rate (every 32 µs instead of 64
2026-03-30 10:35:56 [gemini-3.1-pro] µs). The half-line shift between Field 1 and Field 2 is what physically offsets the scanlines on a
2026-03-30 10:35:56 [gemini-3.1-pro] CRT to create the 576i picture.
2026-03-30 10:35:56 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 10:40:07 [nitro@ryzen5] alright, can you post rough code for this new vblank implementation, we do not have many instructions available
2026-03-30 10:40:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] For broadcast-compliant PAL 576i (CCIR 624 / ITU-R BT.470), a standard 625-line frame is split into
2026-03-30 10:40:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] two 312.5-line fields.
2026-03-30 10:40:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 10:40:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] The vertical blanking interval (VBLANK) for each field consists of 24.5 lines, broken down as
2026-03-30 10:40:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] follows:
2026-03-30 10:40:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Pre-equalizing pulses: 2.5 lines (5 short pulses at half-line intervals)
2026-03-30 10:40:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Vertical Sync (Broad) pulses: 2.5 lines (5 long pulses at half-line intervals)
2026-03-30 10:40:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Post-equalizing pulses: 2.5 lines (5 short pulses at half-line intervals)
2026-03-30 10:40:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Blanking lines (Black video): 17 lines
2026-03-30 10:40:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 10:40:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] Because a PIO state machine only has 32 instructions, hardcoding the logic for half-line equalizing
2026-03-30 10:40:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] pulses, broad pulses, and full-line active video will exceed the instruction limit.
2026-03-30 10:40:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 10:40:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] The most efficient workaround is to offload the VBLANK complex timing to the CPU or DMA. You can
2026-03-30 10:40:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] use the FIFO to feed pulse widths (Sync duration and Blank duration) during the VBLANK period, and
2026-03-30 10:40:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] then switch to your bitmap rendering loop for the active lines.
2026-03-30 10:40:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 10:40:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] Here is rough PIO code demonstrating how you can implement a generic VBLANK pulse generator to
2026-03-30 10:40:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] achieve strict broadcast compliance without exhausting your instruction memory:
2026-03-30 10:40:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 10:40:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 10:40:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] ; vidblit.pio
2026-03-30 10:40:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] .program vidblit
2026-03-30 10:40:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 10:40:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	; ... [lace delay setup remains here] ...
2026-03-30 10:40:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 10:40:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	; --- VBLANK Sequence ---
2026-03-30 10:40:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	; CPU feeds 32-bit words for the 24.5 lines of VBLANK
2026-03-30 10:40:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	; [15:0] = Blanking duration (High-Z)
2026-03-30 10:40:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	; [31:16] = Sync pulse duration (Drive Low)
2026-03-30 10:40:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	out	isr, 32			; Read number of VBLANK pulses from FIFO
2026-03-30 10:40:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	mov	y, isr
2026-03-30 10:40:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] vblank_loop:
2026-03-30 10:40:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	pull	block
2026-03-30 10:40:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	out	x, 16			; Read sync duration
2026-03-30 10:40:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	set	pindirs, 1		; Drive sync (low)
2026-03-30 10:40:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] sync_pulse:
2026-03-30 10:40:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	jmp	x--, sync_pulse
2026-03-30 10:40:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 10:40:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	out	x, 16			; Read blank duration
2026-03-30 10:40:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	set	pindirs, 0		; Release to blank level (high)
2026-03-30 10:40:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] blank_pulse:
2026-03-30 10:40:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	jmp	x--, blank_pulse
2026-03-30 10:40:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 10:40:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	jmp	y--, vblank_loop
2026-03-30 10:40:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 10:40:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	; --- Active Video Sequence ---
2026-03-30 10:40:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	; Now read the active line counter and pixel data
2026-03-30 10:40:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	out	x, 32			; linecounter = 287
2026-03-30 10:40:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	out	isr, 32			; pixelcount
2026-03-30 10:40:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] active:
2026-03-30 10:40:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	; ... [Your existing active line and hsync code here] ...
