2026-03-30 10:40:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 10:40:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] To use this, your C++ code would push the exact cycle counts for the equalizing pulses (approx
2026-03-30 10:40:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] 2.35µs sync, 29.65µs blank), broad pulses (approx 27.3µs sync, 4.7µs blank), and standard blanking
2026-03-30 10:40:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] lines into the FIFO before pushing the active frame data.
2026-03-30 10:40:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 10:42:04 [nitro@ryzen5] why not use out y,32 ?
2026-03-30 10:42:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] For broadcast-compliant 576i PAL (625 lines total at 50Hz), the vertical blanking interval per
2026-03-30 10:42:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] field is 24.5 lines. The standard sequence is:
2026-03-30 10:42:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 10:42:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Pre-equalizing pulses: 2.5 lines (5 half-line pulses)
2026-03-30 10:42:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Vertical sync (broad) pulses: 2.5 lines (5 half-line pulses)
2026-03-30 10:42:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Post-equalizing pulses: 2.5 lines (5 half-line pulses)
2026-03-30 10:42:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Blanking lines: 17 lines (standard H-sync but no active video)
2026-03-30 10:42:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 10:42:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] Regarding `out y, 32`: Using `out` to pull delay counts or loop counters from the FIFO is actually
2026-03-30 10:42:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] the ideal approach. Because broadcast interlacing requires half-line offsets and complex pulse
2026-03-30 10:42:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] trains, hardcoding the timing in PIO consumes too many of your 32 instructions.
2026-03-30 10:42:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 10:42:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] By using `out`, you can offload the sequence logic to the CPU or DMA. You feed a sequence of
2026-03-30 10:42:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] durations and pin states into the FIFO, allowing a tiny PIO loop to generate any pulse train.
2026-03-30 10:42:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 10:42:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] Here is a rough data-driven approach for your VBLANK/Sync section. It reads the number of
2026-03-30 10:42:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] transitions, then loops through them, setting the pin state and delaying for the requested
2026-03-30 10:42:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] duration.
2026-03-30 10:42:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 10:42:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 10:42:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] ; Pull the number of sync transitions for this field
2026-03-30 10:42:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	out	x, 32
2026-03-30 10:42:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 10:42:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] sync_loop:
2026-03-30 10:42:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	; Pull 32-bit word:
2026-03-30 10:42:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	; Bit 31: pin state (0=low, 1=high)
2026-03-30 10:42:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	; Bit 30: pin direction (0=in/Hi-Z, 1=out)
2026-03-30 10:42:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	; Bits 29-0: delay duration
2026-03-30 10:42:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	out	pins, 1
2026-03-30 10:42:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	out	pindirs, 1
2026-03-30 10:42:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	out	y, 30
2026-03-30 10:42:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 10:42:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] sync_delay:
2026-03-30 10:42:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	jmp	y--, sync_delay
2026-03-30 10:42:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	jmp	x--, sync_loop
2026-03-30 10:42:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 10:42:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 10:42:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] In your C code, you would build an array of these 32-bit words representing the pre-equalizing,
2026-03-30 10:42:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] broad, post-equalizing, and blanking pulses, and push them to the FIFO before sending the active
2026-03-30 10:42:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] video lines. This guarantees broadcast-compliant half-line timings while leaving the majority of
2026-03-30 10:42:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] your PIO memory free for the active video pixel-shifting logic.
2026-03-30 10:42:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 10:43:06 [nitro@ryzen5] no thats rubbish
2026-03-30 10:43:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] For a broadcast-compliant interlaced PAL signal (ITU-R BT.470 / System I), the 625 lines are split
2026-03-30 10:43:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] into two fields of 312.5 lines. The vertical blanking interval per field breaks down as follows:
2026-03-30 10:43:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 10:43:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Pre-equalizing pulses: 2.5 lines (5 short pulses)
2026-03-30 10:43:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Vertical sync (broad) pulses: 2.5 lines (5 long pulses)
2026-03-30 10:43:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Post-equalizing pulses: 2.5 lines (5 short pulses)
2026-03-30 10:43:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Blanking (black level): 17.5 lines
2026-03-30 10:43:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Active video: 287.5 lines
2026-03-30 10:43:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 10:43:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] To achieve the half-line shift required for 576i without exhausting your 32-instruction PIO limit,
2026-03-30 10:43:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] your instinct about `out` is actually on the right track, provided you pack the data.
2026-03-30 10:43:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 10:43:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] Instead of hardcoding loops for each pulse type in assembly, you can use the CPU to stream packed
2026-03-30 10:43:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] 32-bit words containing the exact low and high cycle counts for the VBLANK sequence. This keeps the
2026-03-30 10:43:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] PIO program tiny and guarantees broadcast-accurate timings.
2026-03-30 10:43:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 10:43:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] Here is a rough implementation of a data-driven sync generator:
2026-03-30 10:43:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 10:43:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 10:43:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] ; VBLANK / Sync generator driven by FIFO
2026-03-30 10:43:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] ; C code pushes 32-bit words: [16-bit high cycles] [16-bit low cycles]
2026-03-30 10:43:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] ; A word with 0 for the low cycle count can signal the end of VBLANK
2026-03-30 10:43:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 10:43:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] sync_stream:
2026-03-30 10:43:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	out	x, 16			; Pull sync (low) duration
2026-03-30 10:43:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	out	y, 16			; Pull blank (high-z) duration
2026-03-30 10:43:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	jmp	!x, active_setup	; If low duration is 0, break to active video
2026-03-30 10:43:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 10:43:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	set	pindirs, 1		; Drive low (Sync)
2026-03-30 10:43:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] pulse_low:
2026-03-30 10:43:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	jmp	x--, pulse_low
2026-03-30 10:43:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 10:43:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	set	pindirs, 0		; High-Z (Blanking level)
2026-03-30 10:43:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] pulse_high:
2026-03-30 10:43:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	jmp	y--, pulse_high
2026-03-30 10:43:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 10:43:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	jmp	sync_stream		; Fetch next pulse timing
2026-03-30 10:43:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 10:43:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] active_setup:
2026-03-30 10:43:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	; ... transition into your active video line routine ...
2026-03-30 10:43:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 10:43:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 10:43:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] In your C code, you would populate an array with the exact cycle counts for equalizing pulses,
2026-03-30 10:43:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] broad pulses, and standard blanking lines, then feed them into the FIFO before sending the visible
2026-03-30 10:43:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] frame data. This handles both the odd and even field timings effortlessly.
2026-03-30 10:43:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 10:45:16 [nitro@ryzen5] i would prefer a fixed number of pulses, why should it be variabl?
2026-03-30 10:46:07 [gemini-3.1-pro] For broadcast-compliant PAL (System I) 576i, the 24 lines of the Vertical Blanking Interval (VBI)
2026-03-30 10:46:07 [gemini-3.1-pro] per field are strictly divided as follows:
2026-03-30 10:46:07 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 10:46:07 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 2.5 lines (5 pulses): Pre-equalizing pulses
2026-03-30 10:46:07 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 2.5 lines (5 pulses): Broad pulses (Vertical Sync)
2026-03-30 10:46:07 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 2.5 lines (5 pulses): Post-equalizing pulses
2026-03-30 10:46:07 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 16.5 lines: Blanking (active black level with standard H-sync)
2026-03-30 10:46:07 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 10:46:07 [gemini-3.1-pro] To answer your question regarding `out y, 32`: the reason it is commonly suggested for video
2026-03-30 10:46:07 [gemini-3.1-pro] timings is purely to save PIO instruction space. Generating precise microsecond delays for three
2026-03-30 10:46:07 [gemini-3.1-pro] different pulse types (pre, broad, post) using hardcoded `set` and `jmp` loops quickly consumes the
2026-03-30 10:46:07 [gemini-3.1-pro] 32-instruction limit. Offloading the delay constants to the FIFO bypasses this limit.
2026-03-30 10:46:07 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 10:46:07 [gemini-3.1-pro] However, since you prefer fixed hardcoded pulses and want to avoid variable FIFO reads for sync, we
2026-03-30 10:46:07 [gemini-3.1-pro] can compromise. Modern displays will typically lock onto a compliant serrated broad-pulse sequence
2026-03-30 10:46:07 [gemini-3.1-pro] even if you omit the equalizing pulses.
2026-03-30 10:46:07 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 10:46:07 [gemini-3.1-pro] Here is a rough PIO implementation for a fixed 5-pulse broad sync sequence (2.5 lines) that fits
2026-03-30 10:46:07 [gemini-3.1-pro] efficiently into your instruction constraints. It generates the required ~27.3µs low and ~4.7µs
2026-03-30 10:46:07 [gemini-3.1-pro] high per half-line at 150MHz (4800 cycles total per pulse):
2026-03-30 10:46:07 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 10:46:07 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 10:46:07 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	; 5 broad pulses (2.5 lines)
2026-03-30 10:46:07 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	; 4096 cycles low, 704 cycles high
2026-03-30 10:46:07 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	set	x, 4
2026-03-30 10:46:07 [gemini-3.1-pro] broad_pulses:
2026-03-30 10:46:07 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	set	pindirs, 1
2026-03-30 10:46:07 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	set	y, 127
2026-03-30 10:46:07 [gemini-3.1-pro] b_low:
2026-03-30 10:46:07 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	jmp	y--, b_low [30]		; 128 * 32 = 4096 cycles
2026-03-30 10:46:07 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 10:46:07 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	set	pindirs, 0
2026-03-30 10:46:07 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	set	y, 21
2026-03-30 10:46:07 [gemini-3.1-pro] b_high:
2026-03-30 10:46:07 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	jmp	y--, b_high [30]	; 22 * 32 = 704 cycles
2026-03-30 10:46:07 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 10:46:07 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	jmp	x--, broad_pulses
2026-03-30 10:46:07 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 10:46:07 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 10:46:07 [gemini-3.1-pro] To complete the 24 lines of VBI without adding more PIO instructions, you can handle the remaining
2026-03-30 10:46:07 [gemini-3.1-pro] 21.5 lines of blanking by simply sending empty (black) pixel data from your C++ FIFO loop before
2026-03-30 10:46:07 [gemini-3.1-pro] sending the 288 active lines.
2026-03-30 10:46:07 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 11:34:49 [nitro@ryzen5] hi claude, can you help with 2350 pio coding?
2026-03-30 11:34:52 [claude-opus-4-6] Hi! Yes, I'd be happy to help with RP2350 PIO coding. What are you working on?
2026-03-30 11:34:52 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 11:35:10 [claude-opus-4-6] Interesting project - PAL 288P video from a single GPIO on RP2350 using PIO bit-banging. Nice
2026-03-30 11:35:10 [claude-opus-4-6] approach using `pindirs` to switch between driving low (sync) and high-Z (white via the trimpot
2026-03-30 11:35:10 [claude-opus-4-6] pull-up), with `out pindirs, 1` to clock bitmap data directly into the pin direction.
2026-03-30 11:35:10 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 11:35:10 [claude-opus-4-6] A few observations:
2026-03-30 11:35:10 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 11:35:10 [claude-opus-4-6] - The filename is inconsistent: `vidbit.cpp` vs `vidblit.pio` / `#include <vidblit.pio.h>`
2026-03-30 11:35:10 [claude-opus-4-6] - Your `frame` layout has 22 words per line for 704 pixels but `width=704` only uses bits 0–703, so
2026-03-30 11:35:10 [claude-opus-4-6] bit 704 (word 22, bit 0) is never touched - that's clean.
2026-03-30 11:35:10 [claude-opus-4-6] - The vsync is a simple low hold rather than proper equalising pulses, but for most displays that
2026-03-30 11:35:10 [claude-opus-4-6] should lock fine.
2026-03-30 11:35:10 [claude-opus-4-6] - The hblank timing: front porch 224 + hsync 704 + back porch 900 = 1828 cycles. With the `set
2026-03-30 11:35:10 [claude-opus-4-6] pindirs` instructions that's close to your 1800 target but a bit over - worth verifying against a
2026-03-30 11:35:10 [claude-opus-4-6] scope.
2026-03-30 11:35:10 [claude-opus-4-6] - The active line is 11 cycles per pixel (out + 9 delay + jmp) giving 704×11 = 7744, plus the setup
2026-03-30 11:35:10 [claude-opus-4-6] `mov y,isr` - looks right.
2026-03-30 11:35:10 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 11:35:10 [claude-opus-4-6] What are you looking for help with specifically? Timing adjustments, adding colour burst,
2026-03-30 11:35:10 [claude-opus-4-6] interlace, or something else?
2026-03-30 11:35:10 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 11:36:52 [nitro@ryzen5] the image is stable on samsung la19r71b tv, have decided interlace is too tricky, just was interested in your review
2026-03-30 11:37:02 [claude-opus-4-6] Good stuff, stable on real hardware is the milestone that matters.
2026-03-30 11:37:02 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 11:37:02 [claude-opus-4-6] The PIO program is clean - using `pindirs` for the three-level signal (sync/black/white) from one
2026-03-30 11:37:02 [claude-opus-4-6] pin is elegant. The `out pindirs, 1` as the pixel clock is the key trick and it works well at 11
2026-03-30 11:37:02 [claude-opus-4-6] cycles per pixel.
2026-03-30 11:37:02 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 11:37:02 [claude-opus-4-6] Only minor things I'd note:
2026-03-30 11:37:02 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 11:37:02 [claude-opus-4-6] - The `set pins, 1` / `set pindirs, 0` at the start of each line briefly drives high before going
2026-03-30 11:37:02 [claude-opus-4-6] Hi-Z - that's a tiny glitch into the back porch but clearly the TV doesn't mind
2026-03-30 11:37:02 [claude-opus-4-6] - The `.wrap` means after the last line it falls back to `set pins, 0` and re-reads the three
2026-03-30 11:37:02 [claude-opus-4-6] header words from the FIFO, so your CPU loop needs to keep up or the FIFO stalls gracefully - which
2026-03-30 11:37:02 [claude-opus-4-6] it does since PIO blocks on `out`
2026-03-30 11:37:02 [claude-opus-4-6] - `plot`/`unplot` have no bounds checking but that's fine for a demo
2026-03-30 11:37:02 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 11:37:02 [claude-opus-4-6] Solid single-pin PAL implementation.
2026-03-30 11:37:02 [claude-opus-4-6] 
