2026-03-30 11:41:31 [nitro@ryzen5] with a second pin, could i tristattheoretically 2 extra shades of gray with suitable resistors?
2026-03-30 11:41:41 [claude-opus-4-6] Yes. With two pins each using the `pindirs` trick you get four states per pin (drive low, hi-Z,
2026-03-30 11:41:41 [claude-opus-4-6] drive high... well, two useful: hi-Z and drive high) giving you four combinations:
2026-03-30 11:41:41 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 11:41:41 [claude-opus-4-6] ╭─────────┬─────────┬────────────────────────────────────╮
2026-03-30 11:41:41 [claude-opus-4-6] │  Pin A  │  Pin B  │  Level                             │
2026-03-30 11:41:41 [claude-opus-4-6] ├─────────┼─────────┼────────────────────────────────────┤
2026-03-30 11:41:41 [claude-opus-4-6] │  Hi-Z   │  Hi-Z   │  Black (trimpot sets black level)  │
2026-03-30 11:41:41 [claude-opus-4-6] │  Drive  │  Hi-Z   │  Dark grey                         │
2026-03-30 11:41:41 [claude-opus-4-6] │  Hi-Z   │  Drive  │  Light grey                        │
2026-03-30 11:41:41 [claude-opus-4-6] │  Drive  │  Drive  │  White                             │
2026-03-30 11:41:41 [claude-opus-4-6] ╰─────────┴─────────┴────────────────────────────────────╯
2026-03-30 11:41:41 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 11:41:41 [claude-opus-4-6] With an R-2R style resistor weighting on the two pins into the 75Ω load.
2026-03-30 11:41:41 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 11:41:41 [claude-opus-4-6] The challenge in PIO is you'd need to `out` 2 bits of `pindirs` per pixel instead of 1, halving
2026-03-30 11:41:41 [claude-opus-4-6] your horizontal resolution to 352 pixels at the same clock, or you'd need to drop the delay to
2026-03-30 11:41:41 [claude-opus-4-6] maintain 704. At 11 cycles per pixel you have room - `out pindirs, 2 [9]` should work directly with
2026-03-30 11:41:41 [claude-opus-4-6] 2-bit packed pixel data in the FIFO.
2026-03-30 11:41:41 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 11:41:41 [claude-opus-4-6] Frame buffer doubles to 44 words per line though.
2026-03-30 11:41:41 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 11:43:59 [nitro@ryzen5] that table should have sync v0 also
2026-03-30 11:44:09 [claude-opus-4-6] Right, five levels:
2026-03-30 11:44:09 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 11:44:09 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 11:44:09 [claude-opus-4-6] ╭─────────┬─────────┬─────────╮
2026-03-30 11:44:09 [claude-opus-4-6] │  Pin A  │  Pin B  │  Level  │
2026-03-30 11:44:09 [claude-opus-4-6] ├─────────┼─────────┼─────────┤
2026-03-30 11:44:09 [claude-opus-4-6] │  Low    │  Low    │  Sync   │
2026-03-30 11:44:09 [claude-opus-4-6] │  Hi-Z   │  Hi-Z   │  Black  │
2026-03-30 11:44:09 [claude-opus-4-6] │  Drive  │  Hi-Z   │  Dark   │
2026-03-30 11:44:09 [claude-opus-4-6] │  Hi-Z   │  Drive  │  Light  │
2026-03-30 11:44:09 [claude-opus-4-6] │  Drive  │  Drive  │  White  │
2026-03-30 11:44:09 [claude-opus-4-6] ╰─────────┴─────────┴─────────╘
2026-03-30 11:44:09 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 11:44:09 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 11:44:09 [claude-opus-4-6] So during sync you'd `set pindirs, 3` with `set pins, 0` as you already do, and during active video
2026-03-30 11:44:09 [claude-opus-4-6] `out pindirs, 2` for the pixel data. The `set pins` value selects between sync (low) and the
2026-03-30 11:44:09 [claude-opus-4-6] greyscale levels (high) while `pindirs` controls which pins are actually driving.
2026-03-30 11:44:09 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 11:44:09 [claude-opus-4-6] You'd need the resistor values chosen so the two pins contribute unequal voltages at the 75Ω load
2026-03-30 11:44:09 [claude-opus-4-6] to get evenly spaced grey levels rather than just three distinct levels with a duplicate.
2026-03-30 11:44:09 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 11:46:02 [nitro@ryzen5] i am also curious about hdmi version, can i do audio and 576p over hdmi without running into regulatory hurdles?
2026-03-30 11:46:16 [claude-opus-4-6] The HDMI spec itself is licensed by HDMI Licensing Administrator. Using the HDMI name/logo or
2026-03-30 11:46:16 [claude-opus-4-6] claiming HDMI compliance on a product requires a license and fees. That's the regulatory hurdle.
2026-03-30 11:46:16 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 11:46:16 [claude-opus-4-6] However, the electrical signalling (TMDS over 4 differential pairs) is essentially the same as DVI,
2026-03-30 11:46:16 [claude-opus-4-6] which has no such licensing requirement. Many hobbyist and open source projects output DVI-D
2026-03-30 11:46:16 [claude-opus-4-6] signals over an HDMI connector without calling it HDMI. TVs accept it fine.
2026-03-30 11:46:17 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 11:46:17 [claude-opus-4-6] DVI-D doesn't carry audio though. Audio over TMDS is specifically an HDMI feature, covered by the
2026-03-30 11:46:17 [claude-opus-4-6] HDMI spec.
2026-03-30 11:46:17 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 11:46:17 [claude-opus-4-6] For a personal/hobby project none of this matters practically - no one is policing your bench. If
2026-03-30 11:46:17 [claude-opus-4-6] you're selling a product, you'd want to either:
2026-03-30 11:46:17 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 11:46:17 [claude-opus-4-6] - License HDMI (expensive, annual fees)
2026-03-30 11:46:17 [claude-opus-4-6] - Call it DVI-compatible and skip audio
2026-03-30 11:46:17 [claude-opus-4-6] - Use a different audio path (I2S DAC, 3.5mm jack)
2026-03-30 11:46:17 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 11:46:17 [claude-opus-4-6] On the RP2350 side, Pico2 doesn't have the PIO clock speed for raw TMDS bit-banging the way RP2040
2026-03-30 11:46:17 [claude-opus-4-6] projects (like PicoDVI) managed at 252MHz+. You'd likely need an HDMI serialiser chip (e.g.
2026-03-30 11:46:17 [claude-opus-4-6] SiI9022, TFP410) driven over parallel RGB or I2S, which handles TMDS encoding and audio embedding
2026-03-30 11:46:17 [claude-opus-4-6] for you.
2026-03-30 11:46:17 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 11:46:17 [claude-opus-4-6] Have you looked at what clock speeds you can get out of PIO on your Pico2?
2026-03-30 11:46:17 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 11:47:32 [nitro@ryzen5] how about optical audio? is that clear of licensing?
2026-03-30 11:47:41 [claude-opus-4-6] Yes, S/PDIF over TOSLINK (optical) is license-free. It's an old Sony/Philips standard that's been
2026-03-30 11:47:41 [claude-opus-4-6] open for decades.
2026-03-30 11:47:41 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 11:47:41 [claude-opus-4-6] It's also a great fit for PIO - it's a single-pin biphase mark encoded serial stream at relatively
2026-03-30 11:47:41 [claude-opus-4-6] low bitrates (3.072 MHz for 48kHz stereo 16-bit). There are existing PIO implementations for RP2040
2026-03-30 11:47:41 [claude-opus-4-6] that work directly on RP2350.
2026-03-30 11:47:41 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 11:47:41 [claude-opus-4-6] So DVI video over HDMI connector + TOSLINK audio would give you a fully license-free setup with
2026-03-30 11:47:41 [claude-opus-4-6] digital AV output.
2026-03-30 11:47:41 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 11:47:41 [claude-opus-4-6] A TOSLINK transmitter module is just an LED driver - a few dollars for something like a DLT1111 or
2026-03-30 11:47:41 [claude-opus-4-6] similar, driven directly from a GPIO/PIO pin.
2026-03-30 11:47:41 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 11:51:48 [nitro@ryzen5] is the audio in on my samsung tv digital?
2026-03-30 11:51:56 [claude-opus-4-6] The LA19R71B is an older Samsung LCD - it likely has composite AV inputs (RCA), maybe component,
2026-03-30 11:51:56 [claude-opus-4-6] and possibly a 3.5mm audio in, but almost certainly no optical/digital audio input. Digital audio
2026-03-30 11:51:56 [claude-opus-4-6] inputs (optical or coaxial S/PDIF) are rare on TVs - they usually have digital audio output
2026-03-30 11:51:56 [claude-opus-4-6] for sending TV audio to a soundbar/receiver.
2026-03-30 11:51:56 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 11:51:56 [claude-opus-4-6] So for that TV you're probably looking at analogue audio, which is even simpler - a PIO or PWM DAC
2026-03-30 11:51:56 [claude-opus-4-6] into the RCA audio input you're presumably already near with your composite video connection.
2026-03-30 11:51:56 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 11:51:56 [claude-opus-4-6] A single-pin PWM audio output from RP2350 into a low-pass filter would pair nicely with your
2026-03-30 11:51:56 [claude-opus-4-6] existing setup. Minimal extra hardware.
2026-03-30 11:51:56 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 11:53:06 [nitro@ryzen5] it is light blue audio connector btween vga and hdmi connector, i will double check
2026-03-30 11:53:13 [claude-opus-4-6] That sounds like a 3.5mm stereo analogue input (PC audio in), often paired with the VGA input as a
2026-03-30 11:53:13 [claude-opus-4-6] combo - VGA carries no audio so TVs provide a 3.5mm jack alongside it for the PC use case.
2026-03-30 11:53:13 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 11:53:13 [claude-opus-4-6] If so, that's the simplest option - PWM audio from a PIO or the RP2350's hardware PWM, through an
2026-03-30 11:53:13 [claude-opus-4-6] RC low-pass filter, into that 3.5mm jack. A couple of resistors and a capacitor.
2026-03-30 11:53:13 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 11:54:06 [nitro@ryzen5] circuit please, i intend to to stereo
2026-03-30 11:54:15 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 11:54:15 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 11:54:15 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 11:54:15 [claude-opus-4-6] GPIO_L ─── 1kΩ ──┬── 100nF ──┬──► Left (tip)
2026-03-30 11:54:16 [claude-opus-4-6]                   │           │
2026-03-30 11:54:16 [claude-opus-4-6]                  10kΩ        GND
2026-03-30 11:54:16 [claude-opus-4-6]                   │
2026-03-30 11:54:16 [claude-opus-4-6]                  GND
2026-03-30 11:54:16 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 11:54:16 [claude-opus-4-6] GPIO_R ─── 1kΩ ──┬── 100nF ──┬──► Right (ring)
2026-03-30 11:54:16 [claude-opus-4-6]                   │           │
2026-03-30 11:54:16 [claude-opus-4-6]                  10kΩ        GND
2026-03-30 11:54:16 [claude-opus-4-6]                   │
2026-03-30 11:54:16 [claude-opus-4-6]                  GND
2026-03-30 11:54:16 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 11:54:16 [claude-opus-4-6]                               ──► GND (sleeve)
2026-03-30 11:54:16 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 11:54:16 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 11:54:16 [claude-opus-4-6] The 1kΩ + 10kΩ divides the 3.3V PWM down to ~300mV line level. The 100nF cap with the 10kΩ forms a
2026-03-30 11:54:16 [claude-opus-4-6] low-pass at ~160Hz which is quite aggressive - you might want 10nF instead for a ~16kHz cutoff,
2026-03-30 11:54:16 [claude-opus-4-6] depending on your PWM frequency.
2026-03-30 11:54:16 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 11:54:16 [claude-opus-4-6] For PWM audio at say 48kHz sample rate with 150MHz clock you'd get ~12-bit resolution (150M/48K ≈
2026-03-30 11:54:16 [claude-opus-4-6] 3125 levels). Two PIO state machines or two hardware PWM channels would handle stereo.
2026-03-30 11:54:16 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 11:58:42 [nitro@ryzen5] i have phono to red yellow white rca cable, what is naming of the rings starting from tip (red)
2026-03-30 11:58:49 [claude-opus-4-6] For a 3.5mm stereo jack:
2026-03-30 11:58:49 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 11:58:49 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 11:58:49 [claude-opus-4-6] Tip    ─ Left
2026-03-30 11:58:49 [claude-opus-4-6] Ring   ─ Right
2026-03-30 11:58:49 [claude-opus-4-6] Sleeve ─ Ground
2026-03-30 11:58:49 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 11:58:50 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 11:58:50 [claude-opus-4-6] Your cable maps:
2026-03-30 11:58:50 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 11:58:50 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 11:58:50 [claude-opus-4-6] Red    ─ Right audio
2026-03-30 11:58:50 [claude-opus-4-6] White  ─ Left audio
2026-03-30 11:58:50 [claude-opus-4-6] Yellow ─ Video (composite)
2026-03-30 11:58:50 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 11:58:50 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 11:58:50 [claude-opus-4-6] So the yellow RCA is on the sleeve, which is wrong for audio - that cable is specifically a
2026-03-30 11:58:50 [claude-opus-4-6] camcorder/AV cable where the 3.5mm carries composite video + stereo audio on a single 3-pole jack.
2026-03-30 11:58:50 [claude-opus-4-6] The ring assignments vary by manufacturer though, so check which colour actually carries signal
2026-03-30 11:58:50 [claude-opus-4-6] when you drive each channel.
2026-03-30 11:58:50 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 11:58:50 [claude-opus-4-6] Is this the cable you're using for your PAL composite video currently, or a separate connection?
2026-03-30 11:58:50 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 11:59:49 [nitro@ryzen5] it is 3.5mm but has extra ring, hence my question, meter says red is tip whiterca is tip white rca is next then yellow then common ground on sleeve
2026-03-30 11:59:54 [claude-opus-4-6] So it's a 3.5mm TRRS (4-pole) connector:
2026-03-30 11:59:54 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 11:59:54 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 11:59:54 [claude-opus-4-6] Tip     ─ Red   ─ Right audio
2026-03-30 11:59:54 [claude-opus-4-6] Ring 1  ─ White ─ Left audio
2026-03-30 11:59:54 [claude-opus-4-6] Ring 2  ─ Yellow ─ Composite video
2026-03-30 11:59:54 [claude-opus-4-6] Sleeve  ─ Ground
2026-03-30 11:59:54 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 11:59:54 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 11:59:54 [claude-opus-4-6] That's convenient - you could potentially drive all three signals from the same 3.5mm jack: your
2026-03-30 11:59:54 [claude-opus-4-6] existing PAL composite on the yellow, and PWM stereo audio on red and white. One cable, three PIO
2026-03-30 11:59:54 [claude-opus-4-6] state machines.
2026-03-30 11:59:54 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 12:00:30 [nitro@ryzen5] yes, does that match the raspberry pi?
2026-03-30 12:00:37 [claude-opus-4-6] No, the Raspberry Pi uses a different TRRS mapping:
2026-03-30 12:00:37 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 12:00:37 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 12:00:37 [claude-opus-4-6] Tip     ─ Left audio
2026-03-30 12:00:37 [claude-opus-4-6] Ring 1  ─ Right audio
2026-03-30 12:00:37 [claude-opus-4-6] Ring 2  ─ Ground
2026-03-30 12:00:37 [claude-opus-4-6] Sleeve  ─ Composite video
2026-03-30 12:00:37 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 12:00:37 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 12:00:37 [claude-opus-4-6] So your Samsung cable and the Pi cable are incompatible - swapped ground/video and swapped
2026-03-30 12:00:37 [claude-opus-4-6] left/right. Using a Pi cable on your Samsung would put video on the sleeve (ground) and ground on
2026-03-30 12:00:37 [claude-opus-4-6] the video input.
2026-03-30 12:00:37 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 12:00:37 [claude-opus-4-6] Your Samsung mapping is more like the camcorder/AV standard. Just stick with the cable that came
2026-03-30 12:00:37 [claude-opus-4-6] with or works with the TV.
2026-03-30 12:00:37 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 12:01:07 [nitro@ryzen5] yup it is from av equipment
2026-03-30 12:01:13 [claude-opus-4-6] Makes sense. So your plan could be:
2026-03-30 12:01:13 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 12:01:13 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 12:01:13 [claude-opus-4-6] PIO0 SM0 ─ GPIO 28 ─── PAL composite video
2026-03-30 12:01:13 [claude-opus-4-6] PIO0 SM1 ─ GPIO x ──── PWM right audio (tip/red)
2026-03-30 12:01:13 [claude-opus-4-6] PIO0 SM2 ─ GPIO y ──── PWM left audio (ring1/white)
2026-03-30 12:01:13 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 12:01:13 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 12:01:13 [claude-opus-4-6] All into that single TRRS jack via the AV cable to the Samsung. Tidy setup.
2026-03-30 12:01:13 [claude-opus-4-6] 
