2026-03-30 12:44:32 [nitro@ryzen5] i found another TRRS cable, this one splits to headphone socket and microphone, is there a standard for this config?
2026-03-30 12:44:41 [claude-opus-4-6] Yes, there are two competing standards for headset TRRS:
2026-03-30 12:44:41 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 12:44:41 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 12:44:41 [claude-opus-4-6] CTIA (most common - Apple, Samsung, most modern devices):
2026-03-30 12:44:41 [claude-opus-4-6] Tip     ─ Left audio
2026-03-30 12:44:41 [claude-opus-4-6] Ring 1  ─ Right audio
2026-03-30 12:44:41 [claude-opus-4-6] Ring 2  ─ Ground
2026-03-30 12:44:41 [claude-opus-4-6] Sleeve  ─ Microphone
2026-03-30 12:44:41 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 12:44:41 [claude-opus-4-6] OMTP (older - Nokia, some Sony Ericsson):
2026-03-30 12:44:41 [claude-opus-4-6] Tip     ─ Left audio
2026-03-30 12:44:42 [claude-opus-4-6] Ring 1  ─ Right audio
2026-03-30 12:44:42 [claude-opus-4-6] Ring 2  ─ Microphone
2026-03-30 12:44:42 [claude-opus-4-6] Sleeve  ─ Ground
2026-03-30 12:44:42 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 12:44:42 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 12:44:42 [claude-opus-4-6] Your cable splitting to headphone + mic suggests CTIA since that's the dominant standard now. You
2026-03-30 12:44:42 [claude-opus-4-6] can confirm with a meter - check which of ring 2 or sleeve is common to the headphone socket
2026-03-30 12:44:42 [claude-opus-4-6] ground.
2026-03-30 12:44:42 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 12:44:42 [claude-opus-4-6] Not directly useful for your AV setup though since the pin assignment is completely different from
2026-03-30 12:44:42 [claude-opus-4-6] your Samsung AV cable. Different world, same connector - the curse of TRRS.
2026-03-30 12:44:42 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 12:45:56 [nitro@ryzen5] can you expand the CTIA and OMTP acronums
2026-03-30 12:46:00 [claude-opus-4-6] • CTIA* - Cellular Telecommunications Industry Association
2026-03-30 12:46:00 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 12:46:01 [claude-opus-4-6] • OMTP* - Open Mobile Terminal Platform
2026-03-30 12:46:01 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 12:46:01 [claude-opus-4-6] Neither name gives any hint that they define headset jack wiring, but here we are.
2026-03-30 12:46:01 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 13:50:07 [nitro@ryzen5] new plan, have located large mono phono, or maybe stereo, should the outer socket be ground? or is that just for chasis?
2026-03-30 13:50:23 [claude-opus-4-6] On a full-size 6.35mm (1/4 inch) jack socket, the outer contact with the switching tab is
2026-03-30 13:50:23 [claude-opus-4-6] ground/sleeve. That's standard for both mono and stereo.
2026-03-30 13:50:23 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 13:50:23 [claude-opus-4-6] You can tell mono vs stereo by the number of contacts - mono has two (tip + sleeve), stereo has
2026-03-30 13:50:23 [claude-opus-4-6] three (tip + ring + sleeve).
2026-03-30 13:50:23 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 13:50:23 [claude-opus-4-6] If it's stereo you could use it as a breakout point for your TRRS AV cable - solder your three
2026-03-30 13:50:23 [claude-opus-4-6] signals plus ground to the contacts, plug the AV cable in via a 3.5mm-to-6.35mm adapter. A bit ugly
2026-03-30 13:50:23 [claude-opus-4-6] but functional on the bench.
2026-03-30 13:50:23 [claude-opus-4-6] 
