2026-03-30 14:04:09 [claude-opus-4-6] Good luck. While you're there, grab a few extra values - 1kΩ, 10kΩ, 100nF and 10nF are all handy to
2026-03-30 14:04:09 [claude-opus-4-6] have in stock for this kind of thing.
2026-03-30 14:04:09 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 14:04:09 [claude-opus-4-6] For TRRS, if Jaycar doesn't have sockets, look in their 3.5mm audio adapter section - sometimes a
2026-03-30 14:04:09 [claude-opus-4-6] TRRS extension cable cut in half is the easiest way to get a wired connector.
2026-03-30 14:04:09 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 14:08:48 [nitro@ryzen5] i just found a pcm5102 i2s dac from adafruit
2026-03-30 14:08:56 [claude-opus-4-6] That's a much better solution than PWM. The PCM5102 is a proper 32-bit stereo DAC with line-level
2026-03-30 14:08:56 [claude-opus-4-6] output built in - no resistor dividers or low-pass filters needed.
2026-03-30 14:08:56 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 14:08:56 [claude-opus-4-6] It takes I2S which PIO handles natively. Three signals:
2026-03-30 14:08:56 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 14:08:56 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 14:08:56 [claude-opus-4-6] BCK  ─ Bit clock
2026-03-30 14:08:56 [claude-opus-4-6] LRCK ─ Left/right word select
2026-03-30 14:08:56 [claude-opus-4-6] DIN  ─ Serial data
2026-03-30 14:08:56 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 14:08:56 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 14:08:56 [claude-opus-4-6] You can run all three from a single PIO state machine using side-set for the clocks and `out` for
2026-03-30 14:08:56 [claude-opus-4-6] data. There are existing PIO I2S implementations for Pico that would work directly.
2026-03-30 14:08:56 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 14:08:56 [claude-opus-4-6] That gives you clean stereo audio alongside your PAL video with minimal wiring. Much cleaner than
2026-03-30 14:08:56 [claude-opus-4-6] PWM into a 1/4 inch jack.
2026-03-30 14:08:56 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 14:11:55 [nitro@ryzen5] i see DIN and BCK to the left are VIN GND and WSEL
2026-03-30 14:12:02 [claude-opus-4-6] So wiring to the Pico 2:
2026-03-30 14:12:02 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 14:12:02 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 14:12:02 [claude-opus-4-6] PCM5102       Pico 2
2026-03-30 14:12:02 [claude-opus-4-6] ─────────     ──────
2026-03-30 14:12:02 [claude-opus-4-6] VIN       ─── 3V3
2026-03-30 14:12:02 [claude-opus-4-6] GND       ─── GND
2026-03-30 14:12:02 [claude-opus-4-6] WSEL      ─── GPIO x  (LRCK)
2026-03-30 14:12:02 [claude-opus-4-6] DIN       ─── GPIO x+1 (data)
2026-03-30 14:12:02 [claude-opus-4-6] BCK       ─── GPIO x+2 (bit clock)
2026-03-30 14:12:02 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 14:12:02 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 14:12:02 [claude-opus-4-6] Pick three consecutive GPIOs that suit your layout - GP16-18 or similar on the opposite end from
2026-03-30 14:12:02 [claude-opus-4-6] your video circuit would keep things tidy.
2026-03-30 14:12:02 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 14:12:02 [claude-opus-4-6] What sample rate are you aiming for?
2026-03-30 14:12:02 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 14:12:48 [nitro@ryzen5] can vin handle 5v?
2026-03-30 14:12:54 [claude-opus-4-6] Yes, the PCM5102 datasheet specifies 3.3V operation but the Adafruit breakout board has an onboard
2026-03-30 14:12:54 [claude-opus-4-6] voltage regulator, so it accepts 5V on VIN. You could run it from VBUS if that's more convenient
2026-03-30 14:12:54 [claude-opus-4-6] than 3V3.
2026-03-30 14:12:54 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 14:13:31 [nitro@ryzen5] it is, i am pulling up my video out to 3v3
2026-03-30 14:13:41 [claude-opus-4-6] Makes sense - keeping 3V3 for the video trimpot pull-up and running the DAC from VBUS keeps the
2026-03-30 14:13:41 [claude-opus-4-6] supplies separate. Less noise on your video from the DAC's digital switching too.
2026-03-30 14:13:41 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 14:20:36 [nitro@ryzen5] can i power it from gpio?
2026-03-30 14:20:44 [claude-opus-4-6] Technically yes - a GPIO can source up to ~12mA on RP2350 and the PCM5102 draws around 20mA. So
2026-03-30 14:20:44 [claude-opus-4-6] it's marginal and not recommended.
2026-03-30 14:20:44 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 14:20:44 [claude-opus-4-6] Stick with VBUS or 3V3 from the power pins.
2026-03-30 14:20:44 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 15:53:51 [nitro@ryzen5] ok all hooked up
2026-03-30 15:53:59 [nitro@ryzen5] GP2 - WSELGP3 - DINGP4 - BCLK
2026-03-30 15:54:05 [claude-opus-4-6] Good. Ready for the PIO I2S program? What sample rate and bit depth are you targeting -
2026-03-30 15:54:05 [claude-opus-4-6] 44.1kHz/16-bit or 48kHz/16-bit?
2026-03-30 15:54:05 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 15:54:27 [nitro@ryzen5] 44/16.1/16 sounds fine
2026-03-30 15:54:35 [claude-opus-4-6] At 44.1kHz 16-bit stereo, the bit clock needs to be 44100 × 32 × 2 = 2,822,400 Hz.
2026-03-30 15:54:35 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 15:54:35 [claude-opus-4-6] With PIO at 150MHz, that's a divider of about 53.15. Not exact - you'll get slight pitch drift.
2026-03-30 15:54:35 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 15:54:35 [claude-opus-4-6] 48kHz is cleaner: 48000 × 64 = 3,072,000 Hz, divider = 48.828, also not exact.
2026-03-30 15:54:35 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 15:54:35 [claude-opus-4-6] For exact 44.1kHz you'd want a system clock of 135.4752 MHz (or multiple). RP2350's PLL can be
2026-03-30 15:54:35 [claude-opus-4-6] configured for this.
2026-03-30 15:54:35 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 15:54:35 [claude-opus-4-6] Want me to write the PIO program and C setup code for 44.1kHz, or go with 48kHz and accept a tiny
2026-03-30 15:54:35 [claude-opus-4-6] frequency error?
2026-03-30 15:54:35 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 15:54:54 [nitro@ryzen5] ok 48/16 then
2026-03-30 15:55:09 [claude-opus-4-6] Here's the PIO program:
2026-03-30 15:55:09 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 15:55:09 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 15:55:10 [claude-opus-4-6] ; i2s.pio
2026-03-30 15:55:10 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 15:55:10 [claude-opus-4-6] .program i2s
2026-03-30 15:55:10 [claude-opus-4-6] .side_set 2
2026-03-30 15:55:10 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 15:55:10 [claude-opus-4-6] ; side-set bit 1 = BCK
2026-03-30 15:55:10 [claude-opus-4-6] ; side-set bit 0 = WSEL
2026-03-30 15:55:10 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 15:55:10 [claude-opus-4-6]                     ;        BCK  WSEL
2026-03-30 15:55:10 [claude-opus-4-6] bitloop_left:
2026-03-30 15:55:10 [claude-opus-4-6]     out pins, 1       side 0b10  ; data out, BCK high, WSEL low
2026-03-30 15:55:10 [claude-opus-4-6]     jmp x-- bitloop_left side 0b00  ; BCK low, WSEL low
2026-03-30 15:55:10 [claude-opus-4-6]     out pins, 1       side 0b11  ; last bit, BCK high, WSEL high
2026-03-30 15:55:10 [claude-opus-4-6]     set x, 14         side 0b01  ; BCK low, WSEL high
2026-03-30 15:55:10 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 15:55:10 [claude-opus-4-6] bitloop_right:
2026-03-30 15:55:10 [claude-opus-4-6]     out pins, 1       side 0b11  ; data out, BCK high, WSEL high
2026-03-30 15:55:10 [claude-opus-4-6]     jmp x-- bitloop_right side 0b01  ; BCK low, WSEL high
2026-03-30 15:55:10 [claude-opus-4-6]     out pins, 1       side 0b10  ; last bit, BCK high, WSEL low
2026-03-30 15:55:10 [claude-opus-4-6]     set x, 14         side 0b00  ; BCK low, WSEL low
2026-03-30 15:55:10 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 15:55:10 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 15:55:10 [claude-opus-4-6] And the C setup:
2026-03-30 15:55:10 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 15:55:10 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 15:55:10 [claude-opus-4-6] #include <i2s.pio.h>
2026-03-30 15:55:10 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 15:55:10 [claude-opus-4-6] const uint I2S_DIN = 3;
2026-03-30 15:55:10 [claude-opus-4-6] const uint I2S_WSEL = 2;
2026-03-30 15:55:10 [claude-opus-4-6] const uint I2S_BCK = 4;
2026-03-30 15:55:10 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 15:55:10 [claude-opus-4-6] void i2s_init(PIO pio, int sm) {
2026-03-30 15:55:10 [claude-opus-4-6] 	uint offset = pio_add_program(pio, &i2s_program);
2026-03-30 15:55:10 [claude-opus-4-6] 	pio_sm_config c = i2s_program_get_default_config(offset);
2026-03-30 15:55:10 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 15:55:10 [claude-opus-4-6] 	sm_config_set_out_pins(&c, I2S_DIN, 1);
2026-03-30 15:55:10 [claude-opus-4-6] 	sm_config_set_sideset_pins(&c, I2S_WSEL);
2026-03-30 15:55:10 [claude-opus-4-6] 	sm_config_set_out_shift(&c, false, true, 32);
2026-03-30 15:55:10 [claude-opus-4-6] 	sm_config_set_fifo_join(&c, PIO_FIFO_JOIN_TX);
2026-03-30 15:55:10 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 15:55:10 [claude-opus-4-6] 	// 150MHz / 48.828125 ≈ 3.072MHz BCK
2026-03-30 15:55:10 [claude-opus-4-6] 	// 2 PIO cycles per bit = 48kHz × 32 × 2
2026-03-30 15:55:10 [claude-opus-4-6] 	sm_config_set_clkdiv_int_frac(&c, 24, 109);
2026-03-30 15:55:10 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 15:55:10 [claude-opus-4-6] 	pio_gpio_init(pio, I2S_DIN);
2026-03-30 15:55:10 [claude-opus-4-6] 	pio_gpio_init(pio, I2S_WSEL);
2026-03-30 15:55:10 [claude-opus-4-6] 	pio_gpio_init(pio, I2S_BCK);
2026-03-30 15:55:10 [claude-opus-4-6] 	pio_sm_set_consecutive_pindirs(pio, sm, I2S_WSEL, 3, true);
2026-03-30 15:55:10 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 15:55:10 [claude-opus-4-6] 	pio_sm_init(pio, sm, offset, &c);
2026-03-30 15:55:10 [claude-opus-4-6] 	pio_sm_set_enabled(pio, sm, true);
2026-03-30 15:55:10 [claude-opus-4-6] }
2026-03-30 15:55:10 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 15:55:10 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 15:55:10 [claude-opus-4-6] Each 32-bit FIFO word packs left and right as two 16-bit samples: left in upper 16 bits, right in
2026-03-30 15:55:10 [claude-opus-4-6] lower 16. The auto-pull at 32 bits feeds both channels per word.
2026-03-30 15:55:10 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 15:55:10 [claude-opus-4-6] Want a simple tone generator to test it?
2026-03-30 15:55:10 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 15:55:58 [nitro@ryzen5] is there no handshaking to keep clk in sync?
2026-03-30 15:56:10 [claude-opus-4-6] No, I2S is a master/slave protocol with no handshaking. The Pico is the master - it generates BCK
2026-03-30 15:56:10 [claude-opus-4-6] and WSEL, the DAC just follows.
2026-03-30 15:56:10 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 15:56:10 [claude-opus-4-6] The sync concern is between your FIFO feeding and the PIO clock. If the FIFO underruns, PIO stalls
2026-03-30 15:56:10 [claude-opus-4-6] on `out` waiting for data - BCK stops, then resumes when data arrives. The PCM5102 will handle that
2026-03-30 15:56:10 [claude-opus-4-6] gracefully, you'll just hear a glitch.
2026-03-30 15:56:10 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 15:56:10 [claude-opus-4-6] The real sync question is between your video PIO and audio PIO. Both are on PIO0 sharing the same
2026-03-30 15:56:10 [claude-opus-4-6] clock, so they'll stay in lockstep. Your main loop needs to feed both FIFOs fast enough:
2026-03-30 15:56:10 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 15:56:10 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 15:56:10 [claude-opus-4-6] while (!ReadBootSel()) {
2026-03-30 15:56:10 [claude-opus-4-6]     if (!pio_sm_is_tx_fifo_full(pio, VidStateMachine)) {
2026-03-30 15:56:10 [claude-opus-4-6]         pio_sm_put(pio, VidStateMachine, frame[p++]);
2026-03-30 15:56:10 [claude-opus-4-6]         if (p >= wordCount) p = 0;
2026-03-30 15:56:10 [claude-opus-4-6]     }
2026-03-30 15:56:10 [claude-opus-4-6]     if (!pio_sm_is_tx_fifo_full(pio, AudioStateMachine)) {
2026-03-30 15:56:10 [claude-opus-4-6]         pio_sm_put(pio, AudioStateMachine, next_audio_sample());
2026-03-30 15:56:10 [claude-opus-4-6]     }
2026-03-30 15:56:10 [claude-opus-4-6] }
2026-03-30 15:56:10 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 15:56:10 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 15:56:10 [claude-opus-4-6] DMA would be cleaner - set up two DMA channels, one for video and one for audio, both
2026-03-30 15:56:10 [claude-opus-4-6] auto-repeating, and the CPU is free to update the frame buffer.
2026-03-30 15:56:10 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 15:56:52 [nitro@ryzen5] so lets go with something simpler that is a clean multiple
2026-03-30 15:57:00 [claude-opus-4-6] At 150MHz, clean dividers for I2S:
2026-03-30 15:57:00 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 15:57:00 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 15:57:00 [claude-opus-4-6] 150MHz / 50 = 3.0MHz BCK
2026-03-30 15:57:00 [claude-opus-4-6] 3.0MHz / 64 = 46,875 Hz sample rate
2026-03-30 15:57:00 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 15:57:00 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 15:57:00 [claude-opus-4-6] Close to 48kHz but not exact. Or:
2026-03-30 15:57:00 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 15:57:00 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 15:57:00 [claude-opus-4-6] 150MHz / 48 = 3.125MHz BCK
2026-03-30 15:57:00 [claude-opus-4-6] 3.125MHz / 64 = 48,828 Hz
2026-03-30 15:57:00 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 15:57:00 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 15:57:01 [claude-opus-4-6] Neither is clean. The issue is 150MHz doesn't divide evenly to standard audio rates.
2026-03-30 15:57:01 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 15:57:01 [claude-opus-4-6] You could change the system clock. For exact 48kHz:
2026-03-30 15:57:01 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 15:57:01 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 15:57:01 [claude-opus-4-6] 147.456MHz / 48 = 3.072MHz BCK = exactly 48kHz
2026-03-30 15:57:01 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 15:57:01 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 15:57:01 [claude-opus-4-6] RP2350 PLL can hit 147.456MHz. That would make your video PIO 9,216 cycles per line instead of
2026-03-30 15:57:01 [claude-opus-4-6] 9,600 - your timing constants would need adjusting but it's workable.
2026-03-30 15:57:01 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 15:57:01 [claude-opus-4-6] Or keep 150MHz and accept 46,875Hz - the DAC won't care and no one will hear the difference.
2026-03-30 15:57:01 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 15:57:01 [claude-opus-4-6] Your preference?
2026-03-30 15:57:01 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 15:58:01 [nitro@ryzen5] i am going to run a synth so not fussed about "standards" if i am master
2026-03-30 15:58:06 [claude-opus-4-6] Right, if you're generating the audio it doesn't matter. Pick the cleanest divider.
2026-03-30 15:58:06 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 15:58:06 [claude-opus-4-6] 150MHz / 50 = 3.0MHz BCK, 46,875 Hz sample rate. Nice round numbers.
2026-03-30 15:58:06 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 15:58:06 [claude-opus-4-6] That's `smconfigsetclkdivint_frac(&c, 25, 0)` since PIO takes 2 cycles per bit.
