2026-03-30 16:00:34 [nitro@ryzen5] 5,512.5 Hz	Supported in Flash.[18]8,000 Hz	Telephone and encrypted walkie-talkie, wireless intercom and wireless microphone transmission; adequate for human speech but without sibilance (ess sounds like eff (/s/, /f/)).11,025 Hz	One quarter the sampling rate of audio CDs; used for lower-quality PCM, MPEG audio and for audio analysis of subwoofer bandpasses.[citation needed]16,000 Hz	Wideband frequency extension over standard telephone narrowband 8,000 Hz. Used in most modern VoIP and VVoIP communication products.[19][unreliable source?]22,050 Hz	One half the sampling rate of audio CDs; used for lower-quality PCM and MPEG audio and for audio analysis of low-frequency energy. Suitable for digitizing early 20th century audio formats such as 78s and AM Radio.[20]32,000 Hz	miniDV digital video camcorder, video tapes with extra channels of audio (e.g. DVCAM with four channels of audio), DAT (LP mode), Germany's Digitales Satellitenradio, NICAM digital audio, used alongside analogue television sound in some countries. High-quality digital wireless microphones.[21] Suitable for digitizing FM radio.[citation needed]37,800 Hz	CD-XA audio44,055.9 Hz	Used by digital audio locked to NTSC color video signals (3 samples per line, 245 lines per field, 59.94 fields per second = 29.97 frames per second).44,100 Hz	Audio CD, also most commonly used with MPEG-1 audio (VCD, SVCD, MP3). Originally chosen by Sony because it could be recorded on modified video equipment running at either 25 frames per second (PAL) or 30 frame/s (using an NTSC monochrome video recorder) and cover the 20 kHz bandwidth thought necessary to match professional analog recording equipment of the time. A PCM adaptor would fit digital audio samples into the analog video channel of, for example, PAL video tapes using 3 samples per line, 588 lines per frame, 25 frames per second.47,250 Hz	world's first commercial PCM sound recorder by Nippon Columbia (Denon)48,000 Hz	The standard audio sampling rate used by professional digital video equipment such as tape recorders, video servers, vision mixers and so on. This rate was chosen because it could reconstruct frequencies up to 22 kHz and work with 29.97 frames per second NTSC video, as well as 25 frame/s, 30 frame/s and 24 frame/s systems. With 29.97 frame/s systems, it is necessary to handle 1601.6 audio samples per frame, delivering an integer number of audio samples only every fifth video frame.[13] Also used for sound with consumer video formats like DV, digital TV, DVD, and films. The professional serial digital interface (SDI) and High-definition Serial Digital Interface (HD-SDI) used to connect broadcast television equipment together use this audio sampling frequency. Most professional audio gear uses 48 kHz sampling, including mixing consoles, and digital recording devices.50,000 Hz	First commercial digital audio recorders from the late 70s from 3M and Soundstream.50,400 Hz	Sampling rate used by the Mitsubishi X-80 digital audio recorder.64,000 Hz	Uncommonly used, but supported by some hardware[22][23] and software.[24][25]88,200 Hz	Sampling rate used by some professional recording equipment when the destination is CD (multiples of 44,100 Hz). Some pro audio gear uses (or is able to select) 88.2 kHz sampling, including mixers, EQs, compressors, reverb, crossovers, and recording devices.96,000 Hz	DVD-Audio, some LPCM DVD tracks, BD-ROM (Blu-ray Disc) audio tracks, HD DVD (High-Definition DVD) audio tracks. Some professional recording and production equipment is able to select 96 kHz sampling. This sampling frequency is twice the 48 kHz standard commonly used with audio on professional equipment.176,400 Hz	Sampling rate used by HDCD recorders and other professional applications for CD production. Four times the frequency of 44.1 kHz.192,000 Hz	DVD-Audio, some LPCM DVD tracks, BD-ROM (Blu-ray Disc) audio tracks, and HD DVD (High-Definition DVD) audio tracks, High-Definition audio recording devices and audio editing software. This sampling frequency is four times the 48 kHz standard commonly used with audio on professional video equipment.352,800 Hz	Digital eXtreme Definition, used for recording and editing Super Audio CDs, as 1-bit Direct Stream Digital (DSD) is not suited for editing. 8 times the frequency of 44.1 kHz.384,000 Hz	Maximum sample rate available in common software.[citation needed]2,822,400 Hz	SACD, 1-bit delta-sigma modulation process known as Direct Stream Digital, co-developed by Sony and Philips.5,644,800 Hz	Double-Rate DSD, 1-bit Direct Stream Digital at 2x the rate of the SACD. Used in some professional DSD recorders.11,289,600 Hz	Quad-Rate DSD, 1-bit Direct Stream Digital at 4x the rate of the SACD. Used in some uncommon professional DSD recorders.22,579,200 Hz	Octuple-Rate DSD, 1-bit Direct Stream Digital at 8x the rate of the SACD. Used in rare experimental DSD recorders. Also known as DSD512.45,158,400 Hz	Sexdecuple-Rate DSD, 1-bit Direct Stream Digital at 16x the rate of the SACD. Used in rare experimental DSD recorders. Also known as DSD1024.[B]
2026-03-30 16:00:46 [claude-opus-4-6] At 150MHz with 64 BCK cycles per sample (2 PIO cycles per bit = 32 divider cycles per sample):
2026-03-30 16:00:46 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 16:00:46 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 16:00:46 [claude-opus-4-6] 150,000,000 / (divider × 64) = sample rate
2026-03-30 16:00:46 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 16:00:46 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 16:00:46 [claude-opus-4-6] Exact integer dividers that hit your list:
2026-03-30 16:00:46 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 16:00:46 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 16:00:46 [claude-opus-4-6] Divider    BCK          Sample Rate
2026-03-30 16:00:46 [claude-opus-4-6] ──────────────────────────────────────
2026-03-30 16:00:46 [claude-opus-4-6] 4          37,500,000   585,937.5  ✗
2026-03-30 16:00:46 [claude-opus-4-6] ...none hit exactly.
2026-03-30 16:00:46 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 16:00:46 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 16:00:46 [claude-opus-4-6] The problem is 150,000,000 / 64 = 2,343,750 and none of those standard rates divide evenly into
2026-03-30 16:00:46 [claude-opus-4-6] that.
2026-03-30 16:00:46 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 16:00:46 [claude-opus-4-6] However, DSD64 at 2,822,400 Hz is interesting: 150MHz / 53.17 - not exact either.
2026-03-30 16:00:46 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 16:00:46 [claude-opus-4-6] Honestly, at 150MHz you can't hit any of those exactly. Your options:
2026-03-30 16:00:46 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 16:00:46 [claude-opus-4-6] 1. Change system clock - e.g. 147.456MHz gives exact 48kHz
2026-03-30 16:00:46 [claude-opus-4-6] 2. Use fractional divider - close enough for a synth
2026-03-30 16:00:46 [claude-opus-4-6] 3. Pick 46,875 Hz - own it, it's your synth
2026-03-30 16:00:46 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 16:00:46 [claude-opus-4-6] I'd say option 3. You're the master, 46,875Hz is above Nyquist for 20kHz audio, and it's a clean
2026-03-30 16:00:46 [claude-opus-4-6] `divider = 25` with zero jitter.
2026-03-30 16:00:47 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 16:01:55 [nitro@ryzen5] what did amiga use for its audio?
2026-03-30 16:02:03 [claude-opus-4-6] The Amiga used 3,546,895 Hz (PAL) or 3,579,545 Hz (NTSC) as its base clock for audio - derived from
2026-03-30 16:02:03 [claude-opus-4-6] the TV colour carrier.
2026-03-30 16:02:03 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 16:02:03 [claude-opus-4-6] Sample rate was variable per channel - you set a period register (minimum 124) that divided the
2026-03-30 16:02:03 [claude-opus-4-6] clock:
2026-03-30 16:02:03 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 16:02:03 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 16:02:03 [claude-opus-4-6] PAL:  3,546,895 / 124 = 28,604 Hz max
2026-03-30 16:02:03 [claude-opus-4-6] NTSC: 3,579,545 / 124 = 28,867 Hz max
2026-03-30 16:02:03 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 16:02:03 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 16:02:03 [claude-opus-4-6] Common rates were around 8,000–28,000 Hz depending on the module. Most MOD tracker music used
2026-03-30 16:02:03 [claude-opus-4-6] periods giving roughly 16,000–22,000 Hz.
2026-03-30 16:02:03 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 16:02:03 [claude-opus-4-6] 8-bit unsigned samples, 4 hardware channels, two panned hard left and two hard right.
2026-03-30 16:02:03 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 16:02:03 [claude-opus-4-6] Notably, no standard sample rate - every module chose its own replay speed. The ecosystem just
2026-03-30 16:02:03 [claude-opus-4-6] didn't care about fixed rates, similar to your situation.
2026-03-30 16:02:03 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 16:03:44 [nitro@ryzen5] what is max rate for the peripheral? 32 bit 384khz?
2026-03-30 16:03:58 [claude-opus-4-6] The PCM5102 datasheet specs:
2026-03-30 16:03:58 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 16:03:58 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 16:03:58 [claude-opus-4-6] Min     8 kHz
2026-03-30 16:03:58 [claude-opus-4-6] Max   384 kHz
2026-03-30 16:03:58 [claude-opus-4-6] Depth  16/24/32 bit
2026-03-30 16:03:58 [claude-opus-4-6] BCK    max 50.4 MHz
2026-03-30 16:03:58 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 16:03:58 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 16:03:58 [claude-opus-4-6] So at 150MHz PIO you could push it hard:
2026-03-30 16:03:58 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 16:03:58 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 16:03:58 [claude-opus-4-6] 150MHz / 2 = 75MHz BCK - too fast
2026-03-30 16:03:58 [claude-opus-4-6] 150MHz / 4 = 37.5MHz BCK
2026-03-30 16:03:58 [claude-opus-4-6] 37.5MHz / 64 = 585,937 Hz - over 384kHz
2026-03-30 16:03:58 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 16:03:58 [claude-opus-4-6] 150MHz / 6 = 25MHz BCK
2026-03-30 16:03:58 [claude-opus-4-6] 25MHz / 64 = 390,625 Hz - still over
2026-03-30 16:03:58 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 16:03:58 [claude-opus-4-6] 150MHz / 8 = 18.75MHz BCK
2026-03-30 16:03:58 [claude-opus-4-6] 18.75MHz / 64 = 292,968 Hz - within spec
2026-03-30 16:03:58 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 16:03:58 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 16:03:58 [claude-opus-4-6] So divider 8 gives you ~293kHz sample rate at 32-bit stereo, well within the DAC's limits. Overkill
2026-03-30 16:03:58 [claude-opus-4-6] for a synth but available if you want oversampling for cleaner output.
2026-03-30 16:03:58 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 16:06:20 [nitro@ryzen5] ok, 46875 for lets go with 46,875 16 bit stereo
2026-03-30 16:06:30 [claude-opus-4-6] Clean. So the final config:
2026-03-30 16:06:30 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 16:06:30 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 16:06:30 [claude-opus-4-6] 150MHz / 25 = 6MHz PIO clock
2026-03-30 16:06:30 [claude-opus-4-6] 2 PIO cycles per bit = 3MHz BCK
2026-03-30 16:06:30 [claude-opus-4-6] 3MHz / 64 = 46,875 Hz
2026-03-30 16:06:30 [claude-opus-4-6] 16-bit stereo, left-justified
2026-03-30 16:06:30 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 16:06:30 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 16:06:30 [claude-opus-4-6] `smconfigsetclkdivint_frac(&c, 25, 0)` - zero jitter.
2026-03-30 16:06:30 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 16:06:30 [claude-opus-4-6] FIFO format per 32-bit word:
2026-03-30 16:06:30 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 16:06:30 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 16:06:30 [claude-opus-4-6] [31:16] Left sample
2026-03-30 16:06:30 [claude-opus-4-6] [15:0]  Right sample
2026-03-30 16:06:30 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 16:06:30 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 16:06:30 [claude-opus-4-6] Want me to update the PIO program and C code with these final values?
2026-03-30 16:06:30 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 16:06:55 [nitro@ryzen5] pio code first, so we can discuss
2026-03-30 16:07:04 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 16:07:04 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 16:07:04 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 16:07:04 [claude-opus-4-6] ; i2s.pio
2026-03-30 16:07:04 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 16:07:04 [claude-opus-4-6] .program i2s
2026-03-30 16:07:04 [claude-opus-4-6] .side_set 2
2026-03-30 16:07:04 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 16:07:04 [claude-opus-4-6] ; side-set bit 1 = BCK (GP4)
2026-03-30 16:07:04 [claude-opus-4-6] ; side-set bit 0 = WSEL (GP2)
2026-03-30 16:07:04 [claude-opus-4-6] ; out pin = DIN (GP3)
2026-03-30 16:07:04 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 16:07:04 [claude-opus-4-6] ; 16-bit stereo, left justified
2026-03-30 16:07:04 [claude-opus-4-6] ; 2 PIO cycles per BCK cycle at clkdiv 25
2026-03-30 16:07:04 [claude-opus-4-6] ; BCK = 3MHz, sample rate = 46,875 Hz
2026-03-30 16:07:04 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 16:07:04 [claude-opus-4-6]                             ;        BCK  WSEL
2026-03-30 16:07:04 [claude-opus-4-6]     set x, 14         side 0b00
2026-03-30 16:07:04 [claude-opus-4-6] bitloop_left:
2026-03-30 16:07:04 [claude-opus-4-6]     out pins, 1        side 0b10
2026-03-30 16:07:04 [claude-opus-4-6]     jmp x-- bitloop_left side 0b00
2026-03-30 16:07:04 [claude-opus-4-6]     out pins, 1        side 0b10
2026-03-30 16:07:04 [claude-opus-4-6]     set x, 14          side 0b00
2026-03-30 16:07:04 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 16:07:04 [claude-opus-4-6] bitloop_right:
2026-03-30 16:07:04 [claude-opus-4-6]     out pins, 1        side 0b11
2026-03-30 16:07:04 [claude-opus-4-6]     jmp x-- bitloop_right side 0b01
2026-03-30 16:07:04 [claude-opus-4-6]     out pins, 1        side 0b11
2026-03-30 16:07:04 [claude-opus-4-6]     set x, 14          side 0b01
2026-03-30 16:07:04 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 16:07:04 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 16:07:04 [claude-opus-4-6] WSEL low = left, WSEL high = right. BCK toggles every instruction. Auto-pull at 32 bits grabs a new
2026-03-30 16:07:04 [claude-opus-4-6] word at the boundary between right ending and left starting.
2026-03-30 16:07:04 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 16:07:04 [claude-opus-4-6] 16 bits per channel: 1 initial `out` + 14 loops + 1 final `out` = 16 bits each side.
2026-03-30 16:07:04 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 16:07:04 [claude-opus-4-6] Thoughts?
2026-03-30 16:07:04 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 16:08:48 [nitro@ryzen5] is left justified the default direction of the fifo?
2026-03-30 16:08:56 [claude-opus-4-6] No, left-justified means MSB first which requires shift-left direction:
2026-03-30 16:08:56 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 16:08:56 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 16:08:56 [claude-opus-4-6] sm_config_set_out_shift(&c, false, true, 32);
2026-03-30 16:08:56 [claude-opus-4-6] //                       ↑ false = shift left (MSB first)
2026-03-30 16:08:56 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 16:08:56 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 16:08:56 [claude-opus-4-6] The default PIO out shift is right (LSB first). Your video PIO uses right shift because it clocks
2026-03-30 16:08:56 [claude-opus-4-6] pixels left-to-right from bit 0. For I2S you need the opposite - MSB of each 16-bit sample goes out
2026-03-30 16:08:56 [claude-opus-4-6] first.
2026-03-30 16:08:56 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 16:08:56 [claude-opus-4-6] So the FIFO word `0x8000_4000` would output left=0x8000 then right=0x4000, MSB first for each
2026-03-30 16:08:56 [claude-opus-4-6] channel.
2026-03-30 16:08:57 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 16:10:34 [nitro@ryzen5] what is the side stuff?
2026-03-30 16:10:48 [claude-opus-4-6] Side-set lets a PIO instruction do two things simultaneously - the main operation plus setting
2026-03-30 16:10:48 [claude-opus-4-6] extra pins on the same cycle.
2026-03-30 16:10:48 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 16:10:48 [claude-opus-4-6] In this case every instruction both does its job (out data, jmp, set) AND sets the BCK and WSEL
2026-03-30 16:10:48 [claude-opus-4-6] pins:
2026-03-30 16:10:48 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 16:10:48 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 16:10:49 [claude-opus-4-6] out pins, 1        side 0b10
2026-03-30 16:10:49 [claude-opus-4-6] ;   ↑ main op              ↑ side-set: BCK=1, WSEL=0
2026-03-30 16:10:49 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 16:10:49 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 16:10:49 [claude-opus-4-6] Without side-set you'd need separate instructions to toggle the clocks, doubling the program length
2026-03-30 16:10:49 [claude-opus-4-6] and halving the speed.
2026-03-30 16:10:49 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 16:10:49 [claude-opus-4-6] The `.side_set 2` directive reserves 2 bits of the delay/side-set field for this. That costs you 2
2026-03-30 16:10:49 [claude-opus-4-6] bits of available delay (max delay drops from 31 to 7) but for I2S you don't need delays.
2026-03-30 16:10:49 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 16:10:49 [claude-opus-4-6] In the C setup it's configured with:
2026-03-30 16:10:49 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 16:10:49 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 16:10:49 [claude-opus-4-6] sm_config_set_sideset_pins(&c, I2S_WSEL);
2026-03-30 16:10:49 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 16:10:49 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 16:10:49 [claude-opus-4-6] This tells PIO that side-set bit 0 controls GP2 (WSEL) and bit 1 controls GP3... actually that's a
2026-03-30 16:10:49 [claude-opus-4-6] problem. Your DIN is on GP3 and side-set would claim GP3 too since it maps consecutive pins from
2026-03-30 16:10:49 [claude-opus-4-6] the base.
2026-03-30 16:10:49 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 16:10:49 [claude-opus-4-6] We need to reorder:
2026-03-30 16:10:49 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 16:10:49 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 16:10:49 [claude-opus-4-6] GP2 - BCK
2026-03-30 16:10:49 [claude-opus-4-6] GP3 - WSEL
2026-03-30 16:10:49 [claude-opus-4-6] GP4 - DIN
2026-03-30 16:10:49 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 16:10:49 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 16:10:49 [claude-opus-4-6] Or pick non-conflicting pins. What's your flexibility on the wiring?
2026-03-30 16:10:49 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 16:13:46 [nitro@ryzen5] happy to rewire, prefer you didnt use side_set stuff
2026-03-30 16:13:55 [claude-opus-4-6] Fair enough. Without side-set, clocking BCK explicitly:
2026-03-30 16:13:55 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 16:13:55 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 16:13:55 [claude-opus-4-6] ; i2s.pio
2026-03-30 16:13:55 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 16:13:55 [claude-opus-4-6] .program i2s
2026-03-30 16:13:55 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 16:13:55 [claude-opus-4-6] ; out pin = DIN (GP2)
2026-03-30 16:13:55 [claude-opus-4-6] ; set pins = BCK (GP3), WSEL (GP4)
2026-03-30 16:13:55 [claude-opus-4-6] ; 16-bit stereo, 46,875 Hz
2026-03-30 16:13:55 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 16:13:55 [claude-opus-4-6]     set x, 14
2026-03-30 16:13:55 [claude-opus-4-6] bitloop_left:
2026-03-30 16:13:55 [claude-opus-4-6]     set pins, 0b00
2026-03-30 16:13:55 [claude-opus-4-6]     out pins, 1
2026-03-30 16:13:55 [claude-opus-4-6]     set pins, 0b01
2026-03-30 16:13:55 [claude-opus-4-6]     jmp x-- bitloop_left
2026-03-30 16:13:55 [claude-opus-4-6]     set pins, 0b00
2026-03-30 16:13:55 [claude-opus-4-6]     out pins, 1
2026-03-30 16:13:55 [claude-opus-4-6]     set pins, 0b01
2026-03-30 16:13:55 [claude-opus-4-6]     set x, 14
