2026-03-30 16:19:23 [nitro@ryzen5] why all the out
2026-03-30 16:22:48 [nitro@ryzen5] is this ok?
2026-03-30 16:22:50 [nitro@ryzen5] ; i2s.pio; 16-bit stereo, 46,875 Hz; out pin = DIN (GP2); set pins = BCK (GP3), WSEL (GP4).program i2s	set	x, 15left:	set	pins, 0	out	pins, 1	set	pins, 1	jmp	x--, left	set	x, 15right:	set	pins, 2	out	pins, 1	set	pins, 3	jmp	x--, right.wrap
2026-03-30 16:23:01 [claude-opus-4-6] Yes that's cleaner. 16 iterations for 16 bits, the `out` inside the loop handles all of them.
2026-03-30 16:23:01 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 16:23:01 [claude-opus-4-6] One issue: the `.wrap` jumps back to the top `set x, 15` but there's no WSEL transition between
2026-03-30 16:23:01 [claude-opus-4-6] right ending and left starting. The last instruction before wrap is `jmp x--, right` which falls
2026-03-30 16:23:01 [claude-opus-4-6] through when x wraps, then `.wrap` jumps to `set x, 15`. WSEL is still high (2) from the right
2026-03-30 16:23:01 [claude-opus-4-6] channel `set pins, 2/3`. It needs to go low before the first left bit.
2026-03-30 16:23:01 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 16:23:01 [claude-opus-4-6] Your original `vidblit.pio` uses `.wrap` at the bottom only - you'd need `.wrap_target` at the `set
2026-03-30 16:23:01 [claude-opus-4-6] x, 15` line. And WSEL needs clearing before left starts.
2026-03-30 16:23:01 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 16:23:01 [claude-opus-4-6] The `set x, 15` at the top already serves as that extra cycle where you could clear WSEL. So it
2026-03-30 16:23:01 [claude-opus-4-6] works if you add `.wrap_target` there - the flow is: right channel ends, wraps to `set x, 15`, then
2026-03-30 16:23:01 [claude-opus-4-6] `left:` starts with `set pins, 0` which clears WSEL. That's fine.
2026-03-30 16:23:01 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 16:25:51 [nitro@ryzen5] for set the second arg is the value, for out, the second argument is the number of bits?
2026-03-30 16:25:57 [claude-opus-4-6] Yes exactly.
2026-03-30 16:25:57 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 16:25:57 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 16:25:57 [claude-opus-4-6] set	pins, 0      ; set pins to value 0
2026-03-30 16:25:57 [claude-opus-4-6] set	x, 15        ; set x to value 15
2026-03-30 16:25:57 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 16:25:57 [claude-opus-4-6] out	pins, 1      ; shift 1 bit from OSR to pins
2026-03-30 16:25:57 [claude-opus-4-6] out	x, 32        ; shift 32 bits from OSR to x
2026-03-30 16:25:57 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 16:25:57 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 16:25:57 [claude-opus-4-6] `set` loads an immediate value. `out` moves N bits from the output shift register.
2026-03-30 16:25:57 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 16:27:02 [nitro@ryzen5] the comment only lists 2 set pins?
2026-03-30 16:27:08 [claude-opus-4-6] Right, `set pins` with 2 pins configured gives you bits 0 and 1, so valid values are 0-3:
2026-03-30 16:27:08 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 16:27:08 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 16:27:08 [claude-opus-4-6] 0 = BCK low,  WSEL low
2026-03-30 16:27:08 [claude-opus-4-6] 1 = BCK high, WSEL low
2026-03-30 16:27:08 [claude-opus-4-6] 2 = BCK low,  WSEL high
2026-03-30 16:27:08 [claude-opus-4-6] 3 = BCK high, WSEL high
2026-03-30 16:27:08 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 16:27:08 [claude-opus-4-6] 
2026-03-30 16:27:08 [claude-opus-4-6] That's all four combinations, fully documented in the PIO code. Nothing missing.
2026-03-30 16:27:08 [claude-opus-4-6] 
