2026-03-30 17:01:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] The 3125 cycle timing in `i2s.pio` perfectly matches the 150MHz / 48kHz requirement. The C++ PIO
2026-03-30 17:01:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] configuration also correctly sets `shift_right` to `false` for MSB-first output.
2026-03-30 17:01:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 17:01:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] One detail in `vidblit.cpp`: your main loop currently feeds the video FIFO but not the audio FIFO.
2026-03-30 17:01:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] Because `out pins, 1` blocks when the OSR is empty, the I2S state machine will stall immediately,
2026-03-30 17:01:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] which will halt your BCK and WSEL clocks.
2026-03-30 17:01:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 17:01:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] To keep the clocks running and test the DAC output, you will need to feed data to `pio1` in your
2026-03-30 17:01:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] main loop alongside the video feed:
2026-03-30 17:01:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 17:01:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 17:01:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	while (!ReadBootSel()) {
2026-03-30 17:01:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		while (!pio_sm_is_tx_fifo_full(pio0, VideoStateMachine)) {
2026-03-30 17:01:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] 			uint32_t pix=frame[p++];
2026-03-30 17:01:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] 			if(p>=wordCount) {
2026-03-30 17:01:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] 				p=0;f++;
2026-03-30 17:01:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] 			}
2026-03-30 17:01:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] 			pio_sm_put(pio0, VideoStateMachine, pix);
2026-03-30 17:01:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		}
2026-03-30 17:01:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 17:01:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		// Feed the audio DAC to keep BCK/WSEL clocks running
2026-03-30 17:01:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		while (!pio_sm_is_tx_fifo_full(pio1, AudioStateMachine)) {
2026-03-30 17:01:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] 			pio_sm_put(pio1, AudioStateMachine, noise());
2026-03-30 17:01:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		}
2026-03-30 17:01:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 17:01:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		led=!led;
2026-03-30 17:01:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		gpio_put(LED_PIN, led);
2026-03-30 17:01:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	}
2026-03-30 17:01:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 17:01:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-30 17:01:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] Note: Your PIO outputs the MSB at the exact same time WSEL toggles. This is technically
2026-03-30 17:01:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] "Left-Justified" format rather than standard I2S (which delays the MSB by one BCK clock). The
2026-03-30 17:01:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] PCM5102 DAC usually handles this fine depending on its FMT pin configuration, but it is worth
2026-03-30 17:01:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] keeping in mind if the channels seem shifted by a bit.
2026-03-30 17:01:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 10:47:23 [nitro@ryzen5] am coding rp2350 in arm modedoes integer multiply take how many cycles?
2026-03-31 10:47:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] On the RP2350's ARM Cortex-M33 cores, a standard 32-bit integer multiplication (yielding a 32-bit
2026-03-31 10:47:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] result) takes exactly 1 clock cycle.
2026-03-31 10:47:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 10:47:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] If you are using instructions that yield a 64-bit result (such as a 32x32 to 64-bit multiply like
2026-03-31 10:47:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] `UMULL` or `SMULL`), it takes 2 clock cycles.
2026-03-31 10:47:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 10:47:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] Please let me know if you would like to see any specific implementation examples.
2026-03-31 10:47:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
