2026-03-31 11:24:26 [nitro@ryzen5] thanks! now for a logic test, can you spot my mistake? no code just discussion
2026-03-31 11:24:27 [nitro@ryzen5] void rect(int x,int y,int w,int h){	int offset=3+(x>>5)+y*22;	while(w>0){		int xoff=x&31;		uint32_t rbits=0xffffffff;		uint32_t bits=rbits>>xoff;		int shift=32-(xoff+w);		if(shift<32) {			bits&=(rbits<<shift);		}		uint32_t mask=~bits;		int off=offset;		for(int i=0;i<h;i++){			frame[off]=(frame[off]&mask)|bits;			off+=22;		}		x+=(32-xoff);		w-=(32-xoff);		offset++;	}}
2026-03-31 11:24:46 [gemini-3.1-pro] The issue lies in how the `shift` variable is handled when the remaining width `w` spans beyond the
2026-03-31 11:24:46 [gemini-3.1-pro] current 32-bit word.
2026-03-31 11:24:46 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 11:24:47 [gemini-3.1-pro] If `xoff + w` is greater than 32, your `shift` calculation becomes a negative number. Because your
2026-03-31 11:24:47 [gemini-3.1-pro] condition only checks if `shift < 32`, it evaluates to true for these negative values.
2026-03-31 11:24:47 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 11:24:47 [gemini-3.1-pro] Bitwise shifting by a negative amount is undefined behavior in C/C++. On ARM processors, the
2026-03-31 11:24:47 [gemini-3.1-pro] negative shift value will be interpreted modulo 32 (or 256 depending on the exact instruction),
2026-03-31 11:24:47 [gemini-3.1-pro] which will wildly shift your mask and corrupt the drawing for that column.
2026-03-31 11:24:47 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 11:24:47 [gemini-3.1-pro] To resolve this, you should only apply that secondary bitmask if the rectangle's width terminates
2026-03-31 11:24:47 [gemini-3.1-pro] within the current 32-bit boundary (meaning `shift > 0`).
2026-03-31 11:24:47 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 11:27:09 [nitro@ryzen5] better! but there is still an issue with right edge (test is 	rect(0,0,100,100)
2026-03-31 11:30:34 [gemini-3.1-pro] Because your test case starts at `x = 0`, `xoff` is always `0`. This means your left-edge mask
2026-03-31 11:30:34 [gemini-3.1-pro] (`rbits >> xoff`) does nothing and remains `0xffffffff`, which happens to perfectly hide a
2026-03-31 11:30:34 [gemini-3.1-pro] bit-ordering (endianness) mismatch.
2026-03-31 11:30:34 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 11:30:34 [gemini-3.1-pro] In the final iteration for a width of 100, your remaining `w` is 4, making `shift = 28`. Your
2026-03-31 11:30:34 [gemini-3.1-pro] right-edge mask calculates `0xffffffff << 28`, which results in `0xf0000000` (setting bits 31
2026-03-31 11:30:34 [gemini-3.1-pro] through 28).
2026-03-31 11:30:34 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 11:30:34 [gemini-3.1-pro] If your display controller treats the LSB (bit 0) as the leftmost pixel—which is standard for
2026-03-31 11:30:34 [gemini-3.1-pro] Little-Endian architectures like the RP2350 when memory is pushed to a display byte-by-byte—then
2026-03-31 11:30:34 [gemini-3.1-pro] `0xf0000000` will draw those 4 pixels at the far right of that 32-pixel block. This leaves
2026-03-31 11:30:34 [gemini-3.1-pro] a 28-pixel gap instead of seamlessly continuing the rectangle.
2026-03-31 11:30:34 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 11:30:34 [gemini-3.1-pro] If your display is indeed LSB-first, your shift directions are backwards. You would need a
2026-03-31 11:30:34 [gemini-3.1-pro] left-shift for `xoff` (to clear the lowest bits on the left edge) and a right-shift for `shift` (to
2026-03-31 11:30:34 [gemini-3.1-pro] clear the highest bits on the right edge).
2026-03-31 11:30:34 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 11:42:30 [nitro@ryzen5] thanks! latest version passes basic testing. is it optimal?
2026-03-31 11:42:31 [nitro@ryzen5] void rect(int x,int y,int w,int h){	int offset=3+(x>>5)+y*22;	while(w>0){		int xoff=x&31;		uint32_t rbits=0xffffffff;		uint32_t bits=rbits<<xoff;		int shift=32-(xoff+w);		if(shift>0) {			bits&=(rbits>>shift);		}		uint32_t mask=~bits;		int off=offset;		for(int i=0;i<h;i++){			frame[off]=(frame[off]&mask)|bits;			off+=22;		}		x+=(32-xoff);		w-=(32-xoff);		offset++;	}}
2026-03-31 11:43:00 [gemini-3.1-pro] It is functional, but there is room for optimization. Here are a few areas to consider:
2026-03-31 11:43:00 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 11:43:00 [gemini-3.1-pro] 1. Redundant Bitwise Logic: Your inner loop calculates `(frame[off] & mask) | bits`.
2026-03-31 11:43:00 [gemini-3.1-pro] Because `mask` is `~bits`, this perfectly simplifies to `frame[off] | bits`. Removing the mask
2026-03-31 11:43:00 [gemini-3.1-pro] completely eliminates a read, a bitwise AND, and the mask generation. (Note: If you eventually want
2026-03-31 11:43:00 [gemini-3.1-pro] to draw 0s instead of 1s, you will need to introduce a color parameter).
2026-03-31 11:43:00 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 11:43:00 [gemini-3.1-pro] 2. Memory Access Pattern: Your loops process the rectangle in vertical columns (`off +=
2026-03-31 11:43:00 [gemini-3.1-pro] 22`), resulting in strided memory access. Iterating horizontally (outer loop for height, inner loop
2026-03-31 11:43:00 [gemini-3.1-pro] for width) provides sequential memory access. Sequential writes are generally faster on the ARM
2026-03-31 11:43:00 [gemini-3.1-pro] memory bus and allow the compiler to better optimize.
2026-03-31 11:43:00 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 11:43:00 [gemini-3.1-pro] 3. Full-Word Fast Path: If you switch to a row-by-row approach, any 32-bit blocks
2026-03-31 11:43:00 [gemini-3.1-pro] completely inside the rectangle (`bits == 0xffffffff`) can be written directly without needing a
2026-03-31 11:43:00 [gemini-3.1-pro] read-modify-write cycle. For wide rectangles, this provides a massive speed increase.
2026-03-31 11:43:00 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 11:43:00 [gemini-3.1-pro] Let me know if you would like to see a code example of the row-by-row approach.
2026-03-31 11:43:00 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
