2026-03-31 19:44:36 [nitro@ryzen5] it tooks a few frames to sync but the pixels are now solid white!
2026-03-31 19:49:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] If you mean the graphics (the sine wave and rectangles) are now sharp, bright, and stable—that is
2026-03-31 19:49:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] excellent! The exact 64.00 µs line timing (9504 cycles at 148.5 MHz) prevents the horizontal drift
2026-03-31 19:49:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] that usually causes fuzziness or tearing.
2026-03-31 19:49:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 19:49:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] However, if you mean the entire screen is a solid white block, the TV is likely
2026-03-31 19:49:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] misinterpreting your voltage levels. Since the timing is mathematically exact and the TV is
2026-03-31 19:49:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] successfully syncing, the PIO is doing its job perfectly. Here is what usually causes a full-white
2026-03-31 19:49:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] screen on this type of 1-bit DAC:
2026-03-31 19:49:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 19:49:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] 1. Trimpot Adjustment (High-Z Voltage):
2026-03-31 19:49:35 [gemini-3.1-pro]    During the active video and back porch, the PIO sets the pin to `High-Z` (input) to output
2026-03-31 19:49:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] Black. If your trimpot is adjusted too close to 3.3V, the floating voltage will be too high. The TV
2026-03-31 19:49:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] will sample this high voltage during the back porch, clamp to it, and interpret your "Black"
2026-03-31 19:49:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] background as "White". Try slowly turning the trimpot to lower the resistance to ground until the
2026-03-31 19:49:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] background drops to black.
2026-03-31 19:49:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] 2. TV AGC (Automatic Gain Control):
2026-03-31 19:49:35 [gemini-3.1-pro]    Because the test frame is mostly black, some TVs will aggressively crank up their internal
2026-03-31 19:49:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] amplifier to try and "find" the image, blowing out the black levels until the whole screen looks
2026-03-31 19:49:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] white. You can test this by adding a large white rectangle to the background in `initFrame()` to
2026-03-31 19:49:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] give the TV a bright reference point to anchor its contrast.
2026-03-31 19:49:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] 3. Inverted interpretation:
2026-03-31 19:49:35 [gemini-3.1-pro]    If the TV is AC-coupled and the sync depth (difference between 0V sync and the High-Z black
2026-03-31 19:49:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] level) is too shallow, the TV's clamping circuit might fail, causing it to see the entire active
2026-03-31 19:49:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] period as a saturated white signal.
2026-03-31 19:49:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 19:49:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] The C code and the PIO FIFO alignment are perfectly matched (exactly 6867 words per frame, 22 words
2026-03-31 19:49:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] per line), so the data being fed to the pin is correct!
2026-03-31 19:49:35 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
