2026-03-31 19:55:27 [nitro@ryzen5] if i want to double the horizontal resolution, does the following mods look ok?
2026-03-31 19:55:57 [gemini-3.1-pro] The C code modifications for memory and coordinates (`words=44`, `width=1408`) are perfectly
2026-03-31 19:55:57 [gemini-3.1-pro] correct, but the PIO modification will break the video sync.
2026-03-31 19:55:57 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 19:55:57 [gemini-3.1-pro] In your modified PIO, the `active` loop takes 6 cycles per pixel (1 for `out`, 4 for delay, 1 for
2026-03-31 19:55:57 [gemini-3.1-pro] `jmp`).
2026-03-31 19:55:57 [gemini-3.1-pro] 1408 pixels × 6 cycles = 8,448 active cycles.
2026-03-31 19:55:57 [gemini-3.1-pro] Added to your 1,760 blanking cycles, the line length becomes 10,208 cycles (~68.7 µs), which will
2026-03-31 19:55:57 [gemini-3.1-pro] cause the TV to lose sync.
2026-03-31 19:55:57 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 19:55:57 [gemini-3.1-pro] To double the resolution while keeping the exact 64 µs (9,504 cycles) PAL line timing, you need
2026-03-31 19:55:57 [gemini-3.1-pro] exactly 5.5 cycles per pixel. You can achieve this by unrolling the PIO loop to process two
2026-03-31 19:55:57 [gemini-3.1-pro] pixels per iteration (11 cycles total).
2026-03-31 19:55:57 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 19:55:57 [gemini-3.1-pro] Here is the corrected approach:
2026-03-31 19:55:57 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 19:55:57 [gemini-3.1-pro] 1. PIO Modification
2026-03-31 19:55:57 [gemini-3.1-pro] Process two `out` instructions per loop. This perfectly preserves your 7,744 active cycles and
2026-03-31 19:55:57 [gemini-3.1-pro] 1,760 blanking cycles.
2026-03-31 19:55:57 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 19:55:57 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 19:55:57 [gemini-3.1-pro] line:
2026-03-31 19:55:57 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	set	pindirs, 0
2026-03-31 19:55:57 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	set	pins, 1
2026-03-31 19:55:57 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	; active = 52.15 µs = 7,744 cycles = 704 loops x 11 cycles (2 pixels per loop)
2026-03-31 19:55:57 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	mov	y,isr
2026-03-31 19:55:57 [gemini-3.1-pro] active:
2026-03-31 19:55:57 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	out	pindirs, 1 [4]	; 5 cycles (Pixel 1)
2026-03-31 19:55:57 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	out	pindirs, 1 [4]	; 5 cycles (Pixel 2)
2026-03-31 19:55:57 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	jmp	y--,active		; 1 cycle
2026-03-31 19:55:57 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 19:55:57 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 19:55:57 [gemini-3.1-pro] 2. C Code Modification
2026-03-31 19:55:57 [gemini-3.1-pro] Because the PIO loop now processes two pixels per iteration, your `pixelcounter` in the FIFO needs
2026-03-31 19:55:57 [gemini-3.1-pro] to revert to `703` (which runs the loop 704 times, outputting 1408 pixels).
2026-03-31 19:55:57 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 19:55:57 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 19:55:57 [gemini-3.1-pro] int initFrame(){
2026-03-31 19:55:57 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	frame[0]=28511;
2026-03-31 19:55:57 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	frame[1]=311;
2026-03-31 19:55:57 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	frame[2]=703;		// 704 iterations * 2 pixels = 1408 pixels
2026-03-31 19:55:57 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	for(int x=0;x<width;x++){
2026-03-31 19:55:57 [gemini-3.1-pro] // ...
2026-03-31 19:55:57 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 19:55:57 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 19:55:57 [gemini-3.1-pro] This keeps the timing mathematically identical to your working 704-pixel version while doubling the
2026-03-31 19:55:57 [gemini-3.1-pro] horizontal density.
2026-03-31 19:55:57 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 19:57:49 [nitro@ryzen5] i would prefer to pick a differnt horizontal resolution that matches standard AV standards
2026-03-31 19:58:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] If you want to match standard AV formats, you have two mathematically perfect options at 148.5 MHz
2026-03-31 19:58:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] that divide cleanly into 32-bit words and perfectly hit the PAL 52 µs active video specification.
2026-03-31 19:58:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 19:58:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] Both options will use exactly 7,680 active cycles (51.71 µs) and 1,824 blanking
2026-03-31 19:58:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] cycles (12.28 µs), maintaining your exact 9,504 cycle line length.
2026-03-31 19:58:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 19:58:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] Option 1: 768 pixels (Square Pixel PAL)
2026-03-31 19:58:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] 768x576 (or 768x288p) is the standard 4:3 aspect ratio for digitised PAL. Pixels are perfectly
2026-03-31 19:58:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] square.
2026-03-31 19:58:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 10 cycles per pixel
2026-03-31 19:58:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 24 words per line
2026-03-31 19:58:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 19:58:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] Option 2: 640 pixels (Retro PC / VGA width)
2026-03-31 19:58:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] 640x480 (or 640x288p) is the classic standard for retro computers and consoles.
2026-03-31 19:58:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 12 cycles per pixel
2026-03-31 19:58:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 20 words per line
2026-03-31 19:58:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 19:58:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] Here is how to implement either one:
2026-03-31 19:58:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 19:58:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] 1. The C Code Updates
2026-03-31 19:58:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 19:58:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] Change your constants at the top of the C file depending on your choice:
2026-03-31 19:58:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 19:58:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] • For 768 pixels:*
2026-03-31 19:58:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 19:58:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] const int words=24;
2026-03-31 19:58:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] const int width=768;
2026-03-31 19:58:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] const int height=312;
2026-03-31 19:58:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] const int wordCount=3+words*312;
2026-03-31 19:58:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] // ...
2026-03-31 19:58:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] int initFrame(){
2026-03-31 19:58:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	frame[0]=28511;
2026-03-31 19:58:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	frame[1]=311;
2026-03-31 19:58:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	frame[2]=767;  // 768 pixels - 1
2026-03-31 19:58:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] // ...
2026-03-31 19:58:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 19:58:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 19:58:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] • For 640 pixels:*
2026-03-31 19:58:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 19:58:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] const int words=20;
2026-03-31 19:58:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] const int width=640;
2026-03-31 19:58:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] const int height=312;
2026-03-31 19:58:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] const int wordCount=3+words*312;
2026-03-31 19:58:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] // ...
2026-03-31 19:58:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] int initFrame(){
2026-03-31 19:58:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	frame[0]=28511;
2026-03-31 19:58:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	frame[1]=311;
2026-03-31 19:58:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	frame[2]=639;  // 640 pixels - 1
2026-03-31 19:58:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] // ...
2026-03-31 19:58:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 19:58:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 19:58:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] 2. The PIO Updates
2026-03-31 19:58:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 19:58:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] Because both resolutions use exactly 7,680 active cycles, the blanking periods are identical for
2026-03-31 19:58:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] both. You only need to change the delay on the `out` instruction in the `active` loop.
2026-03-31 19:58:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 19:58:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] Update the bottom half of your `vidblit.pio` to this:
2026-03-31 19:58:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 19:58:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 19:58:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] line:
2026-03-31 19:58:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	set	pindirs, 0
2026-03-31 19:58:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	set	pins, 1
2026-03-31 19:58:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	; active = 51.71 µs = 7,680 cycles
2026-03-31 19:58:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	mov	y,isr
2026-03-31 19:58:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] active:
2026-03-31 19:58:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	out	pindirs, 1 [8]	; Use [8] for 768 pixels (10 cycles/pix). Use [10] for 640 pixels (12 cycles/pix).
2026-03-31 19:58:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	jmp	y--,active
2026-03-31 19:58:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 19:58:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	; blanking = 12.28 µs = 1,824 cycles (including 3 from line setup)
2026-03-31 19:58:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	set	pindirs, 0
2026-03-31 19:58:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	set	pins, 0
2026-03-31 19:58:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 19:58:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	; front porch = ~1.64 µs = 243 cycles (8 * 30 + 3 setup)
2026-03-31 19:58:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	set	y, 7
2026-03-31 19:58:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] frontporch:
2026-03-31 19:58:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	jmp	y--,frontporch [29]
2026-03-31 19:58:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 19:58:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	; hsync = ~4.66 µs = 692 cycles (23 * 30 + 2 setup)
2026-03-31 19:58:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	set	pindirs, 1
2026-03-31 19:58:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	set	y, 22
2026-03-31 19:58:31 [gemini-3.1-pro] hsync:
2026-03-31 19:58:31 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	jmp	y--,hsync [29]
2026-03-31 19:58:31 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 19:58:31 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	; back porch = ~5.87 µs = 872 cycles (29 * 30 + 2 setup)
2026-03-31 19:58:31 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	set	pindirs, 0
2026-03-31 19:58:31 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	set	y, 28
2026-03-31 19:58:31 [gemini-3.1-pro] backporch:
2026-03-31 19:58:31 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	jmp	y--,backporch [29]
2026-03-31 19:58:31 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 19:58:31 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	jmp	x--,line [13]
2026-03-31 19:58:31 [gemini-3.1-pro] .wrap
2026-03-31 19:58:31 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 19:58:31 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 19:58:31 [gemini-3.1-pro] Using 768 width is highly recommended for PAL, as a circle drawn in code will actually look
2026-03-31 19:58:31 [gemini-3.1-pro] perfectly round on the TV.
