2026-03-31 20:01:31 [gemini-3.1-pro] With that loop, each pixel takes exactly 5 cycles (1 for `out`, 3 for delay, 1 for `jmp`).
2026-03-31 20:01:31 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 20:01:31 [gemini-3.1-pro] To fit nicely within the ~52 µs PAL active window at 148.5 MHz, the ideal resolution is 1536
2026-03-31 20:01:31 [gemini-3.1-pro] pixels (which is exactly 48 words).
2026-03-31 20:01:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 20:01:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] 1536 pixels × 5 cycles = 7,680 active cycles (51.71 µs).
2026-03-31 20:01:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] This leaves exactly 1,824 cycles for the blanking period to maintain your strict 9,504 cycle line
2026-03-31 20:01:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] length.
2026-03-31 20:01:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 20:01:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] Here are the exact C and PIO values to make that work:
2026-03-31 20:01:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 20:01:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] • C Code:*
2026-03-31 20:01:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 20:01:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] const int words=48;
2026-03-31 20:01:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] const int width=1536;
2026-03-31 20:01:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] const int height=312;
2026-03-31 20:01:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] const int wordCount=3+words*312;
2026-03-31 20:01:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] // ...
2026-03-31 20:01:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] int initFrame(){
2026-03-31 20:01:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	frame[0]=28511;
2026-03-31 20:01:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	frame[1]=311;
2026-03-31 20:01:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	frame[2]=1535; // 1536 pixels - 1
2026-03-31 20:01:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 20:01:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 20:01:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] • PIO Code:*
2026-03-31 20:01:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] Because the active cycles (7,680) are identical to the 768-pixel example I mentioned previously,
2026-03-31 20:01:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] you can use the exact same blanking timings to hit 9,504:
2026-03-31 20:01:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
