2026-03-31 20:10:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 20:10:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] At your system clock of 148.5 MHz, 5 cycles equals a 29.7 MHz pixel clock.
2026-03-31 20:10:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 20:10:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] To properly fill the ~51.7 µs active video period of a standard PAL line, the only resolution that
2026-03-31 20:10:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] fits this specific loop is 1536 pixels (1536 pixels × 5 cycles = 7,680 cycles).
2026-03-31 20:10:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 20:10:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] If you pass a lower resolution (like 768 or 704) into that exact 5-cycle loop, it will draw the
2026-03-31 20:10:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] pixels too fast (occupying only half the screen width) and your total line timing will fall short
2026-03-31 20:10:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] of the required 64 µs, breaking the PAL signal.
2026-03-31 20:10:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 20:10:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] As noted in your original file header (`// pal - 64us line - 9504 cycles per line - 11 cycles per
2026-03-31 20:10:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] pixel`), if you want a lower standard resolution like ~704 pixels stretched across the full screen,
2026-03-31 20:10:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] you would need 11 cycles per pixel:
2026-03-31 20:10:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 20:10:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 20:10:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] active:
2026-03-31 20:10:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	out	pindirs, 1 [9]
2026-03-31 20:10:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	jmp	y--,active
2026-03-31 20:10:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 20:10:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 20:10:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] This timing gives you a horizontal resolution of 704 pixels (704 × 11 = 7,744 cycles, or
2026-03-31 20:10:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] ~52.14 µs).
2026-03-31 20:10:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 20:13:31 [nitro@ryzen5] no more code just discussion please, is an active line length of 7680 cycles to w wide? by how much?
2026-03-31 20:13:47 [gemini-3.1-pro] At a system clock of 148.5 MHz, an active line length of 7,680 cycles equates to approximately
2026-03-31 20:13:47 [gemini-3.1-pro] 51.71 µs.
2026-03-31 20:13:47 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 20:13:47 [gemini-3.1-pro] This is actually slightly narrower (shorter in duration) than the strict PAL standard, not
2026-03-31 20:13:47 [gemini-3.1-pro] wider.
2026-03-31 20:13:47 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 20:13:47 [gemini-3.1-pro] The standard active video duration for PAL is typically defined as 52.0 µs. At 148.5 MHz, 52.0 µs
2026-03-31 20:13:47 [gemini-3.1-pro] exactly equals 7,722 cycles.
2026-03-31 20:13:47 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 20:13:47 [gemini-3.1-pro] Therefore, your 7,680-cycle active area is 42 cycles (about 0.28 µs) shorter than the theoretical
2026-03-31 20:13:47 [gemini-3.1-pro] maximum width.
2026-03-31 20:13:47 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 20:13:47 [gemini-3.1-pro] This is perfectly acceptable and often preferred. Analog displays (CRTs) naturally overscan, hiding
2026-03-31 20:13:47 [gemini-3.1-pro] the edges of the picture behind the bezel. Keeping your active video slightly under the 52 µs
2026-03-31 20:13:47 [gemini-3.1-pro] maximum ensures you stay safely out of the horizontal blanking interval and keeps more of your
2026-03-31 20:13:47 [gemini-3.1-pro] pixels visible within the safe area of the screen.
2026-03-31 20:13:47 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 20:14:37 [nitro@ryzen5] ok so what choices do i have for porch counts?
2026-03-31 20:15:01 [gemini-3.1-pro] With your total line fixed at exactly 9504 cycles (64 µs) and your active video taking 7680 cycles,
2026-03-31 20:15:01 [gemini-3.1-pro] you have a strict budget of exactly 1824 cycles remaining for the entire horizontal blanking
2026-03-31 20:15:01 [gemini-3.1-pro] interval.
2026-03-31 20:15:01 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 20:15:01 [gemini-3.1-pro] This interval must encompass your front porch, horizontal sync, back porch, and any state machine
2026-03-31 20:15:01 [gemini-3.1-pro] overhead (like moving your pixel counter into the scratch register).
2026-03-31 20:15:01 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 20:15:01 [gemini-3.1-pro] Here is how you can allocate those 1824 cycles:
2026-03-31 20:15:01 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 20:15:01 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 1. Horizontal Sync (The Fixed Anchor)*
2026-03-31 20:15:01 [gemini-3.1-pro] The PAL standard dictates a 4.7 µs sync pulse. At 148.5 MHz, this is exactly 698 cycles. You do not
2026-03-31 20:15:01 [gemini-3.1-pro] have much choice here; modern displays are somewhat forgiving, but you should keep your sync pulse
2026-03-31 20:15:01 [gemini-3.1-pro] duration as close to 698 cycles as possible to ensure stable tracking.
2026-03-31 20:15:01 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 20:15:02 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 2. Front and Back Porches (The Sliders)*
2026-03-31 20:15:02 [gemini-3.1-pro] Subtracting the sync pulse and a few cycles of overhead leaves you with roughly 1120 cycles to
2026-03-31 20:15:02 [gemini-3.1-pro] distribute between the front and back porches.
2026-03-31 20:15:02 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 20:15:02 [gemini-3.1-pro] Standard PAL timing targets roughly:
2026-03-31 20:15:02 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Front Porch: ~1.65 µs (approx. 245 cycles)
2026-03-31 20:15:02 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Back Porch: ~5.6 µs (approx. 831 cycles)
2026-03-31 20:15:02 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 20:15:02 [gemini-3.1-pro] Because your active video is slightly narrower than the theoretical maximum, you actually have a
2026-03-31 20:15:02 [gemini-3.1-pro] slight surplus of blanking cycles. Your choice of how to distribute these remaining cycles directly
2026-03-31 20:15:02 [gemini-3.1-pro] controls the horizontal centering of your image:
2026-03-31 20:15:02 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 20:15:02 [gemini-3.1-pro] • To shift the image left: Increase the front porch cycles and decrease the back porch
2026-03-31 20:15:02 [gemini-3.1-pro] cycles by the exact same amount.
2026-03-31 20:15:02 [gemini-3.1-pro] • To shift the image right: Decrease the front porch cycles and increase the back porch
2026-03-31 20:15:02 [gemini-3.1-pro] cycles by the exact same amount.
2026-03-31 20:15:02 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 20:15:02 [gemini-3.1-pro] As long as the total cycle count for the line remains exactly 9504 and the sync pulse stays near
2026-03-31 20:15:02 [gemini-3.1-pro] 698 cycles, you are free to adjust the ratio of the front and back porches to perfectly center your
2026-03-31 20:15:02 [gemini-3.1-pro] active video window on the screen.
2026-03-31 20:15:02 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 20:18:53 [nitro@ryzen5] latest version, i am still off sorry
2026-03-31 20:20:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] Your PIO horizontal cycle math is absolutely flawless.
2026-03-31 20:20:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 20:20:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] 3 (setup) + 7680 (active) + 3 + 240 (front porch) + 2 + 690 (hsync) + 2 + 870 (back porch) + 14
2026-03-31 20:20:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] (loop jump) = exactly 9504 cycles.
2026-03-31 20:20:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 20:20:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] If the signal is still failing or unstable on your display, the issue is almost certainly one of
2026-03-31 20:20:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] these two things outside of the horizontal PIO timing:
2026-03-31 20:20:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 20:20:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 1. Vertical Line Count (49.6Hz vs 50Hz)*
2026-03-31 20:20:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] Your `vsync` loop runs for exactly 3 lines (28512 cycles). Then your `line` loop runs 312 times
2026-03-31 20:20:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] (`frame[1] = 311`).
2026-03-31 20:20:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] This means you are outputting 315 lines per frame.
2026-03-31 20:20:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] 148.5 MHz / (9504 cycles * 315 lines) = 49.6 Hz.
2026-03-31 20:20:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] While some analog TVs tolerate this, many digital scalers or modern TVs will reject the signal or
2026-03-31 20:20:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] roll vertically because it's too far from 50.00 Hz.
2026-03-31 20:20:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Fix:* Reduce your active lines to 309 (`frame[1] = 308`) so your total frame is exactly 312 lines
2026-03-31 20:20:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] (50.08 Hz).
2026-03-31 20:20:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 20:20:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 2. PIO FIFO Starvation (The most likely culprit)*
2026-03-31 20:20:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] You are feeding the video PIO from your main `while` loop. With TX and RX FIFOs joined, the PIO can
2026-03-31 20:20:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] buffer a maximum of 8 words (256 pixels). At your pixel clock, 256 pixels take about 8.6 µs
2026-03-31 20:20:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] to draw.
2026-03-31 20:20:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] If the CPU takes longer than 8.6 µs to get back to the video loop, the PIO FIFO empties. When this
2026-03-31 20:20:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] happens, the PIO stalls on the `out pindirs, 1` instruction, artificially stretching the line
2026-03-31 20:20:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] length and completely destroying your horizontal sync.
2026-03-31 20:20:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] Because you called `stdioinitall()`, background USB CDC interrupts are active and will
2026-03-31 20:20:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] routinely steal the CPU for 10–20 µs at a time. `ReadBootSel()` also contains a blocking delay.
2026-03-31 20:20:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 20:20:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] To get a rock-solid video signal, the video frame buffer really needs to be fed to the PIO using a
2026-03-31 20:20:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] DMA channel. This bypasses the CPU entirely and guarantees the PIO never stalls, regardless
2026-03-31 20:20:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] of what the main loop or USB interrupts are doing.
2026-03-31 20:20:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 20:20:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] Let me know if you'd like to see the DMA setup code to replace the manual `piosmput` loop.
2026-03-31 20:20:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 20:22:48 [nitro@ryzen5] the comb filter does not quite align with the rectangles so am getting bright sides, at the lower res this was not apparent
2026-03-31 20:23:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] What you are seeing is analog ringing (overshoot) or cross-luma artifacts.
2026-03-31 20:23:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 20:23:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] Because you increased the horizontal resolution to 1536, your pixel clock is now 29.7 MHz. When you
2026-03-31 20:23:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] draw a sharp vertical edge (like the side of a rectangle), the 1-bit transition from 0 to 1 happens
2026-03-31 20:23:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] almost instantly.
2026-03-31 20:23:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 20:23:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] This creates high-frequency square-wave harmonics that extend well into the 4.43 MHz band that the
2026-03-31 20:23:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] TV's PAL comb filter monitors for color. The comb filter misinterprets these sharp luma edges as
2026-03-31 20:23:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] chroma information (or the TV's analog front-end simply rings trying to process the
2026-03-31 20:23:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] infinite-bandwidth edge), resulting in bright borders around high-contrast transitions. At lower
2026-03-31 20:23:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] resolutions, the pixels are physically wider, which changes the spatial frequency and makes the
2026-03-31 20:23:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] comb filter less likely to catch them.
2026-03-31 20:23:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 20:23:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] Since you are using a strictly 1-bit framebuffer, you cannot anti-alias or soften the edges in
2026-03-31 20:23:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] software.
2026-03-31 20:23:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 20:23:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] The most common fix for 1-bit microcontroller video is a hardware low-pass filter. If you add a
2026-03-31 20:23:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] small capacitor (around 100pF to 220pF) between your VIDEO OUT line and GND, it forms an RC filter
2026-03-31 20:23:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] with your 330Ω resistor. This gently rounds off the sharp digital edges before they reach the TV,
2026-03-31 20:23:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] keeping the frequencies below the comb filter's threshold and eliminating the bright sides.
2026-03-31 20:23:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 20:28:46 [nitro@ryzen5] love it, the bag i have says CAP CER NPO 100P 50V 5%
2026-03-31 20:29:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] That is literally the perfect component for this.
2026-03-31 20:29:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 20:29:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] NP0 (also known as C0G) ceramic capacitors are the gold standard for analog video and RF filtering
2026-03-31 20:29:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] because they are incredibly stable and don't distort the signal as voltage changes.
2026-03-31 20:29:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 20:29:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] Here is exactly where to place it in your circuit:
2026-03-31 20:29:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 20:29:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 20:29:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	3.3V
2026-03-31 20:29:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	 │
2026-03-31 20:29:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	trimpot ~3.3kΩ
2026-03-31 20:29:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	 │
2026-03-31 20:29:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] GP28─┴─330Ω──┬──┬──► VIDEO OUT
2026-03-31 20:29:13 [gemini-3.1-pro]              │  │
2026-03-31 20:29:13 [gemini-3.1-pro]              │ 75Ω (TV)
2026-03-31 20:29:13 [gemini-3.1-pro]            100pF│
2026-03-31 20:29:13 [gemini-3.1-pro]              │  │
2026-03-31 20:29:13 [gemini-3.1-pro]             GND GND
2026-03-31 20:29:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 20:29:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 20:29:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] • What to expect:*
2026-03-31 20:29:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] Placing this 100pF capacitor between the output side of your 330Ω resistor and Ground creates a
2026-03-31 20:29:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] low-pass filter with a cutoff frequency around 26 MHz. Since your pixel clock is 29.7 MHz, this
2026-03-31 20:29:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] will perfectly round off the infinite-bandwidth digital square waves without turning your
2026-03-31 20:29:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] 1536-pixel horizontal resolution into a blurry mess.
2026-03-31 20:29:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 20:29:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] If you wire it up and the bright edges are better but still slightly visible, simply twist
2026-03-31 20:29:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] two of those 100pF capacitors together in parallel. That will give you 200pF, dropping the cutoff
2026-03-31 20:29:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] frequency to around 13 MHz, which will aggressively smooth out the comb filter artifacts.
2026-03-31 20:29:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 20:31:05 [nitro@ryzen5] it sort of works, what would soften it more?
2026-03-31 20:31:43 [gemini-3.1-pro] To soften it further, you need to lower the cutoff frequency of that RC filter.
2026-03-31 20:31:43 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 20:31:43 [gemini-3.1-pro] Right now, with a single 100pF capacitor and the effective resistance of your circuit (which drops
2026-03-31 20:31:43 [gemini-3.1-pro] to about 60Ω when connected to the TV's 75Ω load), your cutoff frequency is around 26 MHz.
2026-03-31 20:31:43 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 20:31:43 [gemini-3.1-pro] Standard PAL video has a bandwidth of about 5 to 5.5 MHz. The TV's comb filter operates around 4.43
2026-03-31 20:31:43 [gemini-3.1-pro] MHz. To stop the ringing, you want to push your filter's cutoff frequency down closer to that 5–10
2026-03-31 20:31:43 [gemini-3.1-pro] MHz range.
2026-03-31 20:31:43 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 20:31:43 [gemini-3.1-pro] Since you have a bag of 100pF capacitors, the easiest way to do this is to wire several of them in
2026-03-31 20:31:43 [gemini-3.1-pro] parallel (twist the legs together side-by-side). Capacitors in parallel add their values
2026-03-31 20:31:43 [gemini-3.1-pro] together:
2026-03-31 20:31:43 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 20:31:43 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 2 caps (200pF): ~13 MHz cutoff (Noticeably softer)
2026-03-31 20:31:43 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 3 caps (300pF): ~8.8 MHz cutoff (Getting close to standard analog video)
2026-03-31 20:31:43 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 4 caps (400pF): ~6.6 MHz cutoff (The sweet spot for PAL)
2026-03-31 20:31:43 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 20:31:43 [gemini-3.1-pro] Try twisting 3 or 4 of those 100pF capacitors together and placing that bundle in the exact same
2026-03-31 20:31:43 [gemini-3.1-pro] spot (between VIDEO OUT and GND).
2026-03-31 20:31:43 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-03-31 20:31:43 [gemini-3.1-pro] This will aggressively round off the digital square waves. Just be aware that if you add too many
2026-03-31 20:31:43 [gemini-3.1-pro] (e.g., going over 1000pF), the filter will start eating into your actual pixel data, and the whole
2026-03-31 20:31:43 [gemini-3.1-pro] image will become blurry and smeared horizontally. 300pF to 400pF should give you a clean,
2026-03-31 20:31:43 [gemini-3.1-pro] ringing-free analog look.
2026-03-31 20:31:43 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
