2026-04-01 08:51:08 [gemini-3.1-pro] The error occurs because the conversation history is preserved between model switches. If a model
2026-04-01 08:51:08 [gemini-3.1-pro] like DeepSeek hallucinated or output malformed JSON for a tool call's arguments (e.g., outputting
2026-04-01 08:51:08 [gemini-3.1-pro] plain text instead of `{}`), `JSON.parse` will throw a `SyntaxError` when `prepareGeminiPrompt`
2026-04-01 08:51:08 [gemini-3.1-pro] attempts to rebuild the history for Gemini.
2026-04-01 08:51:08 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-01 08:51:08 [gemini-3.1-pro] To fix this robustly across all providers, we can introduce a reusable parsing function that falls
2026-04-01 08:51:08 [gemini-3.1-pro] back to an empty object when a model provides invalid JSON.
2026-04-01 08:51:08 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-01 08:51:08 [gemini-3.1-pro] Here is the suggested fix:
2026-04-01 08:51:08 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-01 08:51:08 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-01 08:51:08 [gemini-3.1-pro] function parseToolArguments(args: string): object {
2026-04-01 08:51:08 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	if (!args) return {};
2026-04-01 08:51:08 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	try {
2026-04-01 08:51:08 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		return JSON.parse(args);
2026-04-01 08:51:08 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	} catch (error) {
2026-04-01 08:51:08 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		echo("[FOUNTAIN] Warning: failed to parse tool arguments", args);
2026-04-01 08:51:08 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		return {};
2026-04-01 08:51:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	}
2026-04-01 08:51:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] }
2026-04-01 08:51:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-01 08:51:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-01 08:51:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] You can then update `prepareGeminiPrompt` to use this function instead of calling `JSON.parse`
2026-04-01 08:51:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] directly:
2026-04-01 08:51:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-01 08:51:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-01 08:51:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] 			case "assistant":{
2026-04-01 08:51:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] 					if (item.tool_calls) {
