2026-04-01 08:56:44 [gemini-3.1-pro] No, your original code did not have excess parentheses. They were actually required.
2026-04-01 08:56:44 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-01 08:56:44 [gemini-3.1-pro] In JavaScript and TypeScript, when an arrow function implicitly returns an object literal on a
2026-04-01 08:56:44 [gemini-3.1-pro] single line, you must wrap the object in parentheses `({ ... })`. Otherwise, the engine interprets
2026-04-01 08:56:44 [gemini-3.1-pro] the curly braces `{}` as the start of a function block rather than an object.
2026-04-01 08:56:44 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-01 08:56:44 [gemini-3.1-pro] Because the updated version expands the arrow function into a multi-line block to add the
2026-04-01 08:56:44 [gemini-3.1-pro] `try/catch` logic, we switch to using standard function braces `{ ... }` and an explicit `return {
2026-04-01 08:56:44 [gemini-3.1-pro] ... }` statement. That is why the outer parentheses are no longer needed.
2026-04-01 08:56:44 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
